### The manual: one entry per command, with its summary (first sentence,
### reused by the NAME line and by whatis), its description and its attributes:
###   .synopsis     how to write it (technical identifiers are not translated)
###   .option-X     the explanation of option -X
###   .example-N    the explanation of the N-th example (the command is in the code)
### See src/engine/commands/manual.ts.

man-a2dismod = disables an Apache module: removes its links from /etc/apache2/mods-enabled (see a2enmod). Then restart Apache (systemctl restart apache2). A directive of the module still present in the configuration makes it invalid: apache2ctl configtest says so. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2dismod module…
    .example-1 = stop listing the contents of directories without an index page
man-a2disconf = disables an Apache configuration snippet: removes its link from /etc/apache2/conf-enabled (see a2enconf). Then reload Apache. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2disconf conf…
    .example-1 = disable the CGI scripts configuration
man-a2dissite = disables an Apache site: removes its link from /etc/apache2/sites-enabled (the file stays in sites-available). Then reload Apache. The 000-default site, installed with the package, answers any request whose name matches no other site on the same port: it is often disabled. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2dissite site…
    .example-1 = disable the default site
    .example-2 = then reload Apache
man-a2enconf = enables an Apache configuration snippet (a .conf file in /etc/apache2/conf-available, read for every site) by creating its link in conf-enabled. Then reload Apache. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2enconf conf…
    .example-1 = the available snippets
    .example-2 = enable /etc/apache2/conf-available/servername.conf
man-a2enmod = enables an Apache module, such as mod_rewrite (rewriting addresses) or mod_headers: creates in /etc/apache2/mods-enabled the links to its .load (LoadModule) and .conf files from mods-available, and first enables the modules it depends on. A directive whose module is not loaded makes the configuration invalid ("Invalid command", "perhaps misspelled or defined by a module not included in the server configuration"); in an .htaccess, the page answers 500. Then restart Apache (systemctl restart apache2). apache2ctl -M lists the loaded modules. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2enmod module…
    .example-1 = enable mod_rewrite
    .example-2 = then restart Apache
    .example-3 = the loaded modules
man-a2ensite = enables an Apache site: creates, in /etc/apache2/sites-enabled, a link to its file in /etc/apache2/sites-available. The file name must end in .conf: that is what IncludeOptional sites-enabled/*.conf in apache2.conf reads (a link without .conf, made by hand with ln -s as for nginx, is ignored). Then reload Apache (systemctl reload apache2). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2ensite site…
    .example-1 = the available sites
    .example-2 = enable sites-available/site.conf
    .example-3 = then reload Apache
man-ansible = runs an Ansible module on machines from the inventory, without a playbook (an "ad-hoc" command). The pattern says which: all, a group, a host, several separated by commas. -m gives the module (command by default), -a its arguments ("name=htop state=present", or the command for command and shell), -b runs it with sudo (become). Ansible connects over ssh, never asking a question: the host key must be in ~/.ssh/known_hosts and the account (remote_user in ansible.cfg, -u, or ansible_user) must have the key. The configuration is read from ./ansible.cfg (the current directory), else ~/.ansible.cfg, else /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg. Each host answers SUCCESS (nothing changed), CHANGED, FAILED or UNREACHABLE.
    .synopsis = ansible PATTERN [-m module] [-a arguments] [-b] [-i inventory] [-u account] [--list-hosts]
    .option-m = the module (ping, apt, copy, service…).
    .option-a = the module's arguments.
    .option-b = with sudo (become).
    .option-i = the inventory to read.
    .option-u = the account for the ssh connection.
    .option-l = only these hosts (limit).
    .option-C = only checks, changing nothing (check).
    .option-D = shows the differences (diff).
    .example-1 = reach the three centres
    .example-2 = run a command everywhere
    .example-3 = reload ssh on one centre, with sudo
    .example-4 = who is in the centres group
man-ansible-doc = the documentation of Ansible's modules: -l lists them, one line each; ansible-doc MODULE gives its options (= in front of the required ones) and examples. The ansible.builtin. prefix is optional.
    .synopsis = ansible-doc -l ; ansible-doc MODULE
    .option-l = the list of modules.
    .example-1 = the modules
    .example-2 = the copy module's options
man-ansible-inventory = shows the inventory as Ansible understands it: --graph the tree of groups and their hosts, --list everything, in JSON, with each host's variables. The inventory is an INI file: one [group] per section, one host per line (with its variables: ansible_host=…, ansible_user=…).
    .synopsis = ansible-inventory --graph ; ansible-inventory --list
    .example-1 = the groups and their hosts
    .example-2 = everything, in JSON
man-ansible-playbook = plays a playbook: a YAML file, a list of plays; each play has hosts, and tasks that each call a module, in order, on each host. become: true goes through sudo. A task that changes something can notify a handler (notify), played only once at the end of the play. The output says, for each task and each host, ok (nothing to change), changed, skipping or fatal; PLAY RECAP sums it up. A good playbook run again says changed=0 (idempotence). --check plays without changing anything, --diff shows the differences in files, --limit restricts to some hosts, --syntax-check only checks the YAML.
    .synopsis = ansible-playbook PLAYBOOK [--check] [--diff] [--limit PATTERN] [--syntax-check] [--list-hosts] [--force-handlers]
    .option-i = the inventory to read.
    .option-l = only these hosts (limit).
    .option-C = only checks, changing nothing (check).
    .option-D = shows the differences (diff).
    .option-b = with sudo (become), for all plays.
    .option-u = the account for the ssh connection.
    .example-1 = play the playbook
    .example-2 = the rehearsal: what would change, without changing anything
    .example-3 = one centre first
    .example-4 = check the YAML
man-apache2ctl = checks Apache's configuration and drives the service (under systemd, start, stop, restart and graceful go through systemctl). The configuration starts from /etc/apache2/apache2.conf, which loads the modules (mods-enabled), the ports (ports.conf: Listen, the addresses and ports Apache listens on), then the snippets (conf-enabled) and the sites (sites-enabled/*.conf). A site is a <VirtualHost address:port> block with ServerName (its name), ServerAlias (its other names) and DocumentRoot (the directory of the pages); a <Directory path> section sets up a directory (Require all granted, Options, AllowOverride). For a request, Apache first picks the VirtualHosts of the address and port, then the one whose name matches the Host header, otherwise the first one read. With AllowOverride (All, FileInfo…), Apache reads the directories' .htaccess files on every visit: nothing to reload, but every directive must be allowed and its module loaded. Service errors: /var/log/apache2/error.log (or the site's ErrorLog).
    .synopsis = apache2ctl configtest ; apache2ctl -S ; apache2ctl -M ; apache2ctl start|stop|restart|graceful ; apache2ctl -v
    .option-t = checks the configuration (like configtest): "Syntax OK", or the file and line of the error.
    .option-S = the virtual hosts, by address and port: which one answers by default, which name, which file.
    .option-M = the loaded modules.
    .option-v = the version.
    .option-V = the version and build options.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading
    .example-2 = who answers which address, under which name
    .example-3 = the loaded modules (rewrite_module?)
    .example-4 = reload without cutting visits in progress
man-apachectl = the same script as apache2ctl (see man apache2ctl).
    .synopsis = apachectl configtest ; apachectl -S ; apachectl -M ; apachectl start|stop|restart|graceful
    .option-t = checks the configuration.
    .option-S = the virtual hosts, by address and port.
    .option-M = the loaded modules.
    .option-v = the version.
    .option-V = the version and build options.
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = the virtual hosts
man-aide = lists the commands you know.
    .synopsis = help
man-apropos = searches for a word in the summary of every command. Same as man -k.
    .synopsis = apropos word
man-apt = installs and updates software on Debian, as packages downloaded from the project's repositories. apt update downloads the list of available packages: on a brand-new server it is empty, and apt finds nothing until you have run it; it also tells you how many updates are waiting. apt install installs a package and the ones it needs; a package that provides a service (nginx…) starts and enables it right away. apt list --upgradable shows the updates, apt upgrade installs them (full-upgrade may also remove some packages). A new kernel, or a new libc, only takes effect after a reboot: /var/run/reboot-required says so, and /var/run/reboot-required.pkgs says why. An interrupted upgrade (a dropped connection) leaves dpkg half done: you need dpkg --configure -a. On a shaky connection, run it inside tmux. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = apt update ; apt install [-y] package... ; apt list --installed|--upgradable ; apt upgrade [-y]
    .example-1 = fetch the list of available packages (do this first)
    .example-2 = install the nginx web server
    .example-3 = the packages already installed
    .example-4 = the updates that are waiting
    .example-5 = install the updates
    .example-6 = the packages that require a reboot
man-dpkg = the low-level tool that installs .deb packages (apt uses it). If an installation was interrupted (a dropped connection, a power cut), some packages stay unpacked but not configured, and apt refuses to go on: dpkg --configure -a finishes configuring them. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = dpkg --configure -a
    .example-1 = finish an interrupted installation
man-caddy = Caddy, a web server that obtains and renews its HTTPS certificates by itself (automatic HTTPS). It is configured in a Caddyfile (on Debian: /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, read by the caddy service): one block per site, which starts with the site's address. A domain name (cycles.example) gives an HTTPS site, with a certificate from Let's Encrypt and the redirection from HTTP to HTTPS; :80 or http://… give an HTTP-only site. In the block, one directive per line: root * DIRECTORY and file_server serve files, reverse_proxy passes to an app, redir sends elsewhere, respond answers with a text. Subcommands: validate checks a configuration, fmt formats it (--overwrite to rewrite the file), adapt translates it to JSON (the native format), reload sends it to the running Caddy (through its API, on localhost:2019), version gives the version. --config FILE names the configuration (otherwise, the Caddyfile of the current directory).
    .synopsis = caddy validate|adapt|reload [--config FILE] ; caddy fmt [--overwrite] [FILE] ; caddy version
    .example-1 = check the service's Caddyfile
    .example-2 = see the Caddyfile formatted
    .example-3 = rewrite the file, formatted
    .example-4 = send the configuration to the running Caddy (like systemctl reload caddy)
    .example-5 = Caddy's version
man-mailq = shows Postfix's queue: the emails that couldn't leave yet, with their ID, size, date, sender, the reason in brackets (the other server's answer) and the recipients. Same as postqueue -p. "Mail queue is empty": nothing is waiting.
    .synopsis = mailq
    .example-1 = the waiting emails, and why
man-opendkim-genkey = creates a DKIM key pair: SELECTOR.private (the private key, for opendkim, never to be shared) and SELECTOR.txt (the TXT record to publish in the DNS zone, under SELECTOR._domainkey.domain, with the public key). -d gives the domain (it only appears in the .txt's comment), -s the selector (default: default), -D the directory (default: the current directory), -b the size in bits (2048). The files belong to whoever runs the command: opendkim must be able to read the private key (sudo -u opendkim, or chown afterwards).
    .synopsis = opendkim-genkey [-b bits] [-d domain] [-D directory] [-s selector] [-v]
    .option-b = the key size, in bits.
    .option-d = the domain.
    .option-D = the directory to write the files to.
    .option-s = the selector (the key's name).
    .option-v = says what it does.
    .option-r = restricts the key to email.
    .example-1 = a "mail" key for gardens.example, created by the opendkim account
    .example-2 = the record to publish
man-opendkim-testkey = checks the DKIM key published in DNS (SELECTOR._domainkey.domain): that it exists, and that it matches the private key (-k, or KeyFile in /etc/opendkim.conf). Without -v, says nothing when all is well. "key not secure" only means the DNS answer isn't protected by DNSSEC: it isn't an error. It asks the machine's DNS server: also check what the internet sees (dig @… on another server).
    .synopsis = opendkim-testkey -d domain -s selector [-k key] [-vvv]
    .option-d = the domain.
    .option-s = the selector.
    .option-k = the private key to compare.
    .option-v = more details (up to -vvv).
    .example-1 = check gardens.example's "mail" key
man-postconf = shows and changes Postfix's configuration (/etc/postfix/main.cf). With no argument, all parameters; -n only those written in main.cf; -d the default values; NAME, one parameter's value; -x expands $variables. NAME=VALUE (with -e, or without) changes main.cf, then Postfix must be reloaded. A misspelt name is reported: "unused parameter". Some parameters: myhostname, mydestination (the domains received here), myorigin, smtpd_milters (filters for mail received over SMTP), non_smtpd_milters (for mail submitted on the machine), milter_default_action.
    .synopsis = postconf [-n] [-d] [-x] [NAME…] ; postconf -e "NAME = VALUE"
    .option-n = only the parameters in main.cf.
    .option-d = the default values.
    .option-x = expands $variables.
    .option-e = edits main.cf.
    .option-h = the value only, without "NAME =".
    .example-1 = what is set in main.cf
    .example-2 = two parameters
    .example-3 = also sign mail submitted on the machine
man-postfix = controls Postfix, the mail server: check (checks the configuration), reload (rereads it without stopping the service), start, stop, status, flush (retries everything waiting, like postqueue -f). On Debian, you also go through systemctl (reload postfix). Reserved for root, except status.
    .synopsis = postfix check|reload|start|stop|status|flush
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = reread main.cf
    .example-3 = is Postfix running?
man-postqueue = Postfix's queue: -p shows it (like mailq), -f flushes it, i.e. immediately retries sending every waiting message (otherwise Postfix retries by itself, less and less often).
    .synopsis = postqueue -p ; postqueue -f
    .option-p = shows the queue.
    .option-f = retries sending everything waiting.
    .example-1 = what's waiting
    .example-2 = retry now
man-postsuper = maintenance of Postfix's queue: -d ID deletes a message (ALL: all of them; followed by deferred: only the waiting ones). A deleted message will never leave: only for mail that no longer makes sense. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = postsuper -d ID|ALL [deferred]
    .option-d = deletes messages from the queue.
    .example-1 = delete one message
    .example-2 = empty the waiting messages
man-unattended-upgrade = installs updates with nobody in front of the screen (package unattended-upgrades). It reads apt's configuration: 50unattended-upgrades (in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d) says WHAT to install — Origins-Pattern, "key=value,…" patterns that must match a package's origin (origin=Debian, codename=trixie-security, label=Debian-Security…; by default, Debian and its security updates, not third-party repositories), Package-Blacklist (packages never to touch), Mail (who gets the report), Automatic-Reboot and Automatic-Reboot-Time (reboot on its own if a new kernel requires it, and at what time; otherwise right away). 20auto-upgrades says WHEN: the apt-daily-upgrade service, started every morning by its timer, does nothing as long as APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade is "0" (or missing). Its log: /var/log/unattended-upgrades/unattended-upgrades.log. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = unattended-upgrade [--dry-run] [-d | -v]
    .option-d = (--debug) explains each package: its origin, and why it is taken or left.
    .option-v = (--verbose) shows what it does.
    .option-h = (--help) the help.
    .example-1 = see what WOULD be installed, without installing anything, with the reasons
    .example-2 = run a pass right now, showing what happens
    .example-3 = what happened during the automatic passes
man-apt-config = reads apt's configuration as apt understands it: all the files in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d, in alphabetical order, then /etc/apt/apt.conf (a value read later replaces the previous one). apt-config dump shows everything; followed by the start of a key, only what starts that way. A syntax error (a missing ";") is reported with the file and the line.
    .synopsis = apt-config dump [key]
    .example-1 = the whole configuration
    .example-2 = what controls the daily tasks (20auto-upgrades)
    .example-3 = the unattended-upgrades configuration
man-apt_conf = apt's configuration: files in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d, read in the alphabetical order of their names (hence the numbers: 20auto-upgrades, 50unattended-upgrades). A line is written Key::Subkey "value"; — the semicolon is mandatory. A list is written between braces: Key {"{"} "a"; "b"; {"}"};. Comments: // or /* … */. A value read later replaces the previous one; a list, however, grows. apt-config dump shows the result.
    .synopsis = /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/*
    .example-1 = apt's configuration files
    .example-2 = turn on automatic updates
    .example-3 = check what apt understood
man-iostat = shows disk activity (package sysstat). Without an interval, one report; iostat 1 3 gives three, one second apart (the first one sums up what happened since boot). At the top, CPU usage, including %iowait: the time spent waiting for the disk. Then one line per disk: with -x, r/s and w/s (reads and writes per second), rkB/s and wkB/s (throughput), r_await and w_await (the average wait of a request, in ms: a few ms on a healthy disk, hundreds on a saturated one), aqu-sz (the queue) and %util (the share of time the disk is busy: near 100%, it is saturated).
    .synopsis = iostat [-c | -d] [-x] [-k | -m] [-y] [interval [count]]
    .option-c = only the CPU.
    .option-d = only the disks.
    .option-x = detailed statistics (wait, queue, %util).
    .option-k = throughput in kilobytes (default).
    .option-m = throughput in megabytes.
    .option-y = without the first report (the one since boot).
    .option-z = without idle disks.
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = the CPU and the disks, once
    .example-2 = disk details, every second, three times
    .example-3 = only the disks, in MB/s
man-pidstat = shows the activity of each process (package sysstat). -d: its disk I/O (kB_rd/s, kB_wr/s, and iodelay, the time spent waiting for the disk); -u: its CPU usage (%wait: the time spent waiting). With an interval and a count, several measurements, then their average (Average).
    .synopsis = pidstat [-d | -u] [interval [count]]
    .option-d = the disk I/O of each process.
    .option-u = CPU usage (default).
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = who is reading and writing to the disk right now
    .example-2 = the same thing, for five seconds, then the average
man-ionice = gets or sets the I/O priority of a process (util-linux). Three classes: 1, realtime (root only); 2, best-effort (the normal class, with a priority from 0, the highest, to 7); 3, idle (the process only gets the disk when nobody else asks for it). Beware: these priorities only matter if the disk's scheduler supports them (bfq, mq-deadline: see /sys/block/DISK/queue/scheduler); "none", common on virtual disks and NVMe SSDs, ignores them. To limit a service no matter what: systemd's IOWriteBandwidthMax=.
    .synopsis = ionice -p PID ; ionice -c class [-n prio] -p PID
    .option-c = the class: 1 (realtime), 2 (best-effort), 3 (idle).
    .option-n = the priority within the class, from 0 (the highest) to 7.
    .option-p = the target process (its PID).
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = the priority of a process
    .example-2 = make it idle
    .example-3 = the disk's scheduler, which decides whether ionice matters
man-tmux = a terminal multiplexer: sessions that live on the machine, independently of your connection. If the connection drops, whatever runs in tmux keeps going; you reattach to it afterwards. tmux new -s name opens a session (its name shows before the prompt); tmux detach leaves it while keeping it running (on a real keyboard: Ctrl+b then d); tmux ls lists the sessions; tmux attach -t name reattaches to one; exit, inside the session, closes it. For a long operation on a server (upgrade, copy, migration), always run it in tmux.
    .synopsis = tmux new [-s name] ; tmux ls ; tmux attach [-t name] ; tmux detach ; tmux kill-session -t name
    .example-1 = open a session named upgrade
    .example-2 = leave it while keeping it running
    .example-3 = the running sessions
    .example-4 = reattach to the upgrade session (after a disconnection, for example)
    .example-5 = close the upgrade session
man-uname = prints information about the system: -r the version of the kernel that is RUNNING (not necessarily the installed one: a new kernel only starts at reboot), -a everything.
    .synopsis = uname [-r] [-a]
    .option-r = the version of the running kernel (release).
    .option-a = everything: system, machine name, kernel, architecture.
    .example-1 = the version of the running kernel
    .example-2 = everything
man-bash = your session's shell (Bourne-Again SHell): it reads the commands you type. bash script runs a script, like sh. Several commands fit on one line, as a list: "a ; b" runs a then b; "a && b" runs b only if a succeeded (exit code 0); "a || b" runs b only if a failed. && and || have the same priority and are read from left to right. $? gives the exit code of the last command. Inside quotes, ; && and || are plain text. In this game, a command that leaves the session (exit, ssh, reboot) ends the line: what follows it is not run.
    .synopsis = bash script [argument...] ; command1 && command2 ; command1 || command2
    .example-1 = run a script
    .example-2 = reload nginx only if its configuration is valid
    .example-3 = say so when a line is missing
    .example-4 = go somewhere, then list it
man-certbot = requests and renews free HTTPS certificates from Let's Encrypt. To prove you control the site, certbot drops a "challenge" file that the authority comes to fetch over HTTP: with --webroot, in the directory given by -w, which must be the one the web server serves for that name; with --nginx (package python3-certbot-nginx), nginx serves it. Each certificate is valid for 90 days; a systemd timer runs certbot renew twice a day, which renews whatever expires in less than 30 days. How to renew is recorded in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal/name.conf (rewritten at each successful renewal). A deploy hook is a command run after each successful renewal, for example to reload nginx; so are the executable scripts in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = certbot certificates ; certbot renew [--dry-run] [--webroot -w directory] [--deploy-hook command] ; certbot certonly --webroot -w directory -d name [-d name...] ; certbot reconfigure --cert-name name [options]
    .example-1 = the certificates, their names and their expiry dates
    .example-2 = dress rehearsal: the whole renewal, without changing anything
    .example-3 = renew by dropping the challenge in the site's directory
    .example-4 = reload nginx after each successful renewal
    .example-5 = change how renewal is done, tested without renewing now
man-createdb = creates an empty PostgreSQL database. You need the right to do so: sudo -u postgres createdb name.
    .synopsis = createdb name
    .example-1 = a test database, to try out a restore
man-date = prints the machine's date and time. With +FORMAT, writes them your way: %Y the year, %m the month, %d the day, %H the hour, %M the minutes, %S the seconds, %F for 2027-05-17, %T for 20:30:12. Handy for naming a directory or a backup: date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S. %Z and %z give the time zone (CEST, +0200). The time shown is in the machine's time zone (timedatectl). -d STRING shows another date than now: yesterday, tomorrow, "3 days ago", "+2 weeks", 2028-09-01 (GNU date only: BSD's date, on FreeBSD, uses -v). -s sets the clock by hand (root): best avoided, NTP does it better.
    .synopsis = date [-d STRING] [+FORMAT] ; date -s "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"
    .example-1 = the date and time
    .example-2 = a name that sorts in order: year, month, day, hour, minutes, seconds
    .example-3 = the date alone, in ISO format
    .example-4 = the time, with the time zone's offset from UTC
man-dropdb = deletes a PostgreSQL database and everything in it, without confirmation. Keep it for test databases.
    .synopsis = dropdb name
    .example-1 = delete the test database
man-logrotate = rotates logs so they do not grow forever: every day (or week…), the log is renamed (.1), the older ones move up a notch (.2, .3…), the oldest is deleted, and old ones are compressed (.gz). Run every day by cron or a systemd timer; its configuration: /etc/logrotate.conf, and one file per program in /etc/logrotate.d/. One block per log pattern, between braces: daily, weekly; rotate 7 (how many to keep); compress, delaycompress; missingok (no error if it is missing); notifempty (not if it is empty); create 0640 radio adm (the new file) or copytruncate (copy then empty, for a program that cannot reopen its log); postrotate … endscript (a command afterwards, for example systemctl reload). Beware: a program keeps its file open; once renamed, it keeps writing into it, hence copytruncate or postrotate. -d tries the configuration without doing anything; -f forces the rotation.
    .synopsis = logrotate [-d] [-f] [-v] configuration_file
    .example-1 = try a configuration, without touching anything
    .example-2 = force a rotation now
    .example-3 = an example configuration shipped with Debian
man-lsblk = lists disks and their partitions (list block devices): their name (/dev/sdb1…), their size, whether they are removable (RM = 1, a USB stick or disk) and where they are mounted. -f adds the file system and the label (LABEL), handy for recognizing a disk.
    .synopsis = lsblk [-f] [-d] [-o COLUMNS]
    .option-f = shows the file system, the label and the UUID (filesystems).
    .option-o = chooses the columns, separated by commas: NAME, SIZE, TYPE, MOUNTPOINTS, MODEL (the disk model), SERIAL (its serial number), FSTYPE, UUID…
    .option-d = only the disks, without their partitions (nodeps).
    .example-1 = the disks and their partitions
    .example-2 = with their labels
    .example-3 = each disk, its size, its model and its serial number
man-smartctl = asks a disk about its health (SMART, the monitoring built into the disk; smartmontools package). -H gives the overall verdict: PASSED only means that no attribute has crossed the manufacturer's threshold, not that the disk is fine. The attributes (-A) tell what matters: Reallocated_Sector_Ct (5), damaged sectors the disk has replaced with spare ones; Current_Pending_Sector (197), sectors it can no longer read and is waiting to replace; Offline_Uncorrectable (198); UDMA_CRC_Error_Count (199), cable errors rather than disk errors. A count that keeps growing announces a failure: replace the disk before it dies. -t short runs a short test (two minutes), whose result shows in -l selftest. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = smartctl [-a | -H | -i | -A] [-l error|selftest] [-t short|long] /dev/DISK
    .option-a = everything: identity, verdict, attributes, error and test logs.
    .option-H = the overall verdict (health).
    .option-i = the identity of the disk: model, serial number, capacity.
    .option-A = the SMART attributes, with their value, threshold and raw value (RAW_VALUE).
    .option-l = a log: error (the errors the disk saw) or selftest (the test results).
    .option-t = runs a test: short (a few minutes) or long (several hours).
    .option-x = even more than -a (here, the same thing).
    .example-1 = the overall verdict for a disk
    .example-2 = everything about a disk
    .example-3 = run a short test
    .example-4 = the test results
man-mdadm = manages Linux software RAID (md): arrays of disks, or of partitions, that act as one. In RAID 1, each member keeps a full copy: if one dies, the array carries on with the others (it is "degraded"). cat /proc/mdstat shows the state of every array: [UU], both members are fine; [U_], one is missing; (F), a faulty member; and the rebuild in progress (recovery). To replace a disk: mark its member faulty (--fail), remove it (--remove), then, once the new disk is partitioned, add the new member (--add): the array rebuilds it by copying the others. A disk often holds several partitions, each in its own array: remove them from each one. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = mdadm --detail /dev/mdN ; mdadm [--manage] /dev/mdN --fail PARTITION --remove PARTITION ; mdadm [--manage] /dev/mdN --add PARTITION
    .option-D = the detailed state of an array (--detail): its members, their role, the rebuild.
    .option-f = marks a member faulty (--fail): the array stops using it.
    .option-r = removes a faulty member from the array (--remove); "failed" removes them all.
    .option-a = adds a member (--add): if a member is missing, it takes its place and is rebuilt; --re-add puts back a former member.
    .option-C = creates an array (--create /dev/mdN --level=1 --raid-devices=2 DISKS); mdadm asks for confirmation (--run to skip it). The array is usable right away, during its first synchronization (resync). "missing" instead of a disk leaves its slot empty: the array is born degraded, to be completed later with --add.
    .example-1 = the state of every array
    .example-2 = the details of an array
    .example-3 = take a member out of the md0 array
    .example-4 = add a new member (it gets rebuilt)
    .example-5 = create a RAID 1 array on two new disks
    .example-6 = its ARRAY line, to add to /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf
man-pvs = lists LVM physical volumes (PV): the disks, partitions or RAID arrays LVM uses, their group (VG), their size and their free space (PFree). LVM writes sizes in units of 1024: 1.79t, and "<" when the rounded figure is larger than the real size. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvs
    .example-1 = the physical volumes
man-vgs = lists LVM volume groups (VG): how many PVs (#PV) and logical volumes (#LV) they hold, their size (VSize) and the space left to hand out (VFree). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgs
    .example-1 = the volume groups
man-lvs = lists LVM logical volumes (LV): the ones carrying file systems, their group and their size (LSize). The file system can be smaller than its LV: df shows the former, lvs the latter. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = lvs
    .example-1 = the logical volumes
man-pvcreate = prepares a device (disk, partition or RAID array) for LVM: it becomes a physical volume (PV), which you then add to a group (vgextend). It refuses a partitioned disk, or one that is already part of a RAID array. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvcreate DEVICE…
    .example-1 = make the md2 array a physical volume
man-vgextend = adds one or more physical volumes to a volume group: its free space (VFree) grows accordingly. A device that is not a PV yet is prepared along the way. Careful: a group is no safer than its least safe PV; a lone disk, without RAID, added to a RAID group loses everything if it dies. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgextend GROUP DEVICE…
    .example-1 = add md2 to the lab group
man-vgreduce = removes a physical volume from a group. It refuses while a logical volume sits on it ("still in use"): pvmove first moves its data away. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgreduce GROUP DEVICE…
    .example-1 = take sdc out of the lab group
man-pvmove = moves a physical volume's data to the other PVs of its group (or to the ones you name), while everything keeps running. There must be enough free space elsewhere. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvmove SOURCE [DESTINATION…]
    .example-1 = empty sdc into md2
man-pvremove = wipes the LVM label from a device: it stops being a physical volume and becomes free again (for a RAID array, for example). It refuses a PV still in a group: vgreduce first. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvremove DEVICE…
    .example-1 = make sdc free again
man-lvextend = grows a logical volume: -L gives a size (+500G: 500 GiB more; 2T: 2 TiB in total), -l extents (+100%FREE: all the group's free space). The file system does not follow on its own: df does not change until you grow it too (resize2fs), except with -r, which does it right away. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = lvextend [-r] (-L [+]SIZE | -l [+]EXTENTS[%FREE]) VG/LV [PV…]
    .option-L = the new size, or what to add with + (units k, m, g, t).
    .option-l = the size in 4 MiB extents, or as a share of the free space (+100%FREE).
    .option-r = also grows the file system (resizefs).
    .example-1 = 500 GiB more for the photos volume
    .example-2 = all the free space, file system included
man-resize2fs = grows (or shrinks) an ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system; without a size, it takes all the space of its device (after an lvextend, for example). A mounted ext4 grows without unmounting (on-line resizing); to shrink it, you must unmount it. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = resize2fs DEVICE [SIZE]
    .example-1 = take all the space of the logical volume
    .example-2 = check the new size
man-update-initramfs = rebuilds the initramfs: the small system the kernel loads at boot, before the disks. It carries a copy of /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf: after creating an array or changing that file, rebuild it, or the array comes up under another name (md127) or not at all. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = update-initramfs -u
    .option-u = updates the initramfs of the running kernel.
    .example-1 = rebuild the initramfs after changing mdadm.conf
man-sfdisk = reads and writes partition tables (util-linux), without dialogue: through a script. -l shows them; -d writes a disk's table as a script, which sfdisk can read back to copy it onto another disk (sfdisk -d /dev/sda | sfdisk /dev/sdb: the new disk is then split exactly like the other one). Mind the direction: the disk named last is the one overwritten. sfdisk refuses to touch a disk in use (a mounted partition, or one in a RAID array). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = sfdisk -l [DISK] ; sfdisk -d DISK ; sfdisk DISK < script
    .option-d = writes the disk's table as a script (dump).
    .option-l = shows the partitions (list).
    .example-1 = the partitions of a disk
    .example-2 = its table, as a script
    .example-3 = copy sda's table onto sdb (the new disk)
man-grub-install = installs GRUB, the boot loader, on a disk: it is what the computer runs first. On a RAID 1 server, install it on each disk: if the one that boots dies, the other must be able to boot alone. On a GPT table booted by the BIOS, GRUB needs its small "BIOS boot" partition. Aim at the whole disk (/dev/sdb), not a partition. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = grub-install /dev/DISK
    .example-1 = make the sdb disk able to boot
man-lsof = lists open files, and the processes holding them (list open files). +L1 only shows files that were deleted but are still open: their space is only freed when they are closed (when the program stops or reopens its file). It is the classic cause of a full df while du finds nothing. Without being root, you only see your own processes.
    .synopsis = lsof [+L1] [-p pid] [-u account] [file]
    .example-1 = files deleted but still open
    .example-2 = who is holding this log open?
    .example-3 = the files opened by a process
man-mount = attaches a file system (a partition, a USB stick, a network share) to the tree, at a mount point: an existing directory, whose visible content becomes that of the device. Without arguments, lists what is mounted. -o ro mounts read-only: nothing can be written or deleted there (the right way to plug in a backup you only want to read). Reserved for root. umount detaches. mount -t nfs server:/folder /point mounts a network share (see man nfs).
    .synopsis = mount [-o ro] device mount_point ; mount mount_point ; mount -a
    .example-1 = what is mounted
    .example-2 = attach the sdb1 partition read-only on /mnt/usb
    .example-3 = detach it
    .example-4 = mount everything /etc/fstab describes (except noauto): the right way to test that file before rebooting
    .command-2 = sudo mount -o ro /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb
    .command-3 = sudo umount /mnt/usb
    .example-5 = mount the NAS's photos over NFS
man-fstab = /etc/fstab describes the file systems to mount at boot, one line each: the source, the mount point, the type (ext4…), the options (defaults, ro, nofail, noauto…), dump (0) and the fsck check order (pass: 1 for /, 2 for the others, 0 for none). Give the source preferably as UUID=… or LABEL=… (blkid or lsblk -f show them): names like /dev/sdc1 depend on the order the disks are found in, and change when you add one. nofail: if the source is missing, the boot carries on (essential for a USB disk). At boot, systemd waits 90 seconds for each source; a missing source without nofail stops the machine in emergency mode. After changing fstab: findmnt --verify, mount -a, then systemctl daemon-reload (systemd derives its mount units from this file).
    .synopsis = SOURCE  MOUNT_POINT  TYPE  OPTIONS  DUMP  PASS
    .example-1 = read it
    .example-2 = check it
    .example-3 = mount what it describes, to try it without rebooting
man-blkid = shows device identifiers: their UUID, their label (LABEL) and their type (ext4, linux_raid_member, LVM2_member…). That is what belongs in /etc/fstab, rather than /dev/sdX, which can change. -U and -L find the device for a UUID or a label.
    .synopsis = blkid [device…] ; blkid -U uuid ; blkid -L label
    .option-U = the device carrying this UUID.
    .option-L = the device carrying this label.
    .example-1 = every device and its identifiers
    .example-2 = those of one partition
    .example-3 = which device carries the SCANS label?
man-findmnt = shows what is mounted, as a tree (TARGET, SOURCE, FSTYPE, OPTIONS). findmnt --verify rereads /etc/fstab and reports what would get stuck at boot: a source that can't be found, a type that doesn't match, a missing mount point. Run it after every change to fstab, before rebooting.
    .synopsis = findmnt [mount_point] ; findmnt --verify [--verbose]
    .example-1 = everything that is mounted
    .example-2 = what is mounted on /srv
    .example-3 = check /etc/fstab before rebooting
man-openssl = the cryptography toolbox (TLS, certificates, keys). openssl x509 reads a certificate: its dates (-dates), its holder (-subject), its issuer (-issuer), its names (-ext subjectAltName); -checkend seconds tells whether it expires within that time. openssl s_client connects to a server over TLS and shows the certificate it really presents, and whether it can be trusted (Verify return code).
    .synopsis = openssl x509 [-in file] -noout [-dates] [-subject] [-issuer] [-checkend seconds] ; openssl s_client -connect host:port [-servername name]
    .example-1 = the validity dates of a certificate
    .example-2 = the certificate the server really presents, and its dates
    .example-3 = does it expire within fifteen days (1,296,000 seconds)?
man-awk = a small language for reading lines split into fields. Each line is cut at spaces: $1 is the first field, $2 the second, $NF the last, $0 the whole line; NR is the line number. A program is a series of "pattern {"{"} action {"}"}": the action applies to the lines where the pattern is true (no pattern: all of them; no action: the line is printed). BEGIN runs before the first line, END after the last. Arrays are great for counting: c[$1]++ then, in END, for (k in c) print c[k], k. Put the program between single quotes, so the shell leaves the $ alone.
    .synopsis = awk [-F separator] [-v variable=value] 'program' [file...]
    .example-1 = the first field of each line (the address, in a web log)
    .example-2 = the lines whose 9th field (the HTTP code) is 502
    .example-3 = count the HTTP codes
    .example-4 = add up the 10th field (the bytes sent)
    .example-5 = with another separator (the accounts in /etc/passwd)
man-cat = prints the content of files (concatenate: it can join several end to end). Without a file, copies whatever comes in through a pipe. -A shows what you can't see: a $ at the end of each line, ^M for a carriage return (a file written on Windows), ^I for a tab.
    .synopsis = cat [-AEnTv] file...
    .option-A = show everything: the same as -vET.
    .option-E = a $ at the end of each line.
    .option-T = tabs shown as ^I.
    .option-v = control characters shown with ^ (a carriage return as ^M).
    .option-n = number the lines.
    .example-1 = print a file
    .example-2 = see its invisible characters: ^M$ at the end of a line means Windows line endings (CR LF)
man-cd = changes place (change directory). "cd .." goes up one level, "cd ~" or plain "cd" takes you back home, "cd /" to the root, "cd -" to the previous place.
    .synopsis = cd place
    .example-1 = enter the place Port
    .example-2 = go up one level
    .example-3 = go back home
    .example-4 = go to the root
    .example-5 = go back to the previous place (and print it)
man-chmod = changes permissions (change mode). E.g.: chmod o-w place takes away from others (o) the right to write (w); chmod 755 place gives rwx to the owner, r-x to others. Only the owner (or root) can do it. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory. g+s sets the setgid bit on a directory (an s instead of the group's x in ls -l): what is created inside gets the directory's group, not that of the person creating it; in octal, it is the leading 2 (2775). +t (sticky, the leading 1): in a shared directory, everyone can only delete their own files.
    .synopsis = chmod [-R] mode file
    .option-R = acts on the directory and on everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = take away the others' right to write
    .example-2 = rwx for the owner, r-x for others
    .example-3 = give the group the right to write, across a whole directory
    .example-4 = a shared directory: what is created inside keeps the directory's group
    .command-1 = chmod o-w memories
    .command-2 = chmod 755 place
    .command-3 = sudo chmod -R g+w /srv/team
    .command-4 = sudo chmod g+s /srv/team
man-chage = shows and changes an account's dates (change age): -l shows them, -E sets the account's expiry date (a date YYYY-MM-DD; 0: already expired; -1: never). An expired account can no longer log in, neither with a password nor with an SSH key. Reserved for root (except -l for your own account).
    .synopsis = chage -l account ; chage -E date account
    .option-l = shows the account's dates (list).
    .option-E = sets the account's expiry date (expire date).
    .example-1 = the dates of marc's account
    .example-2 = marc's account has expired: no more logins
    .example-3 = the account no longer expires
man-clear = clears the screen.
    .synopsis = clear
man-cp = copies a file (copy). The original stays in place. -r copies a whole place with all its content; -a too, keeping the rights and dates (the right reflex before editing a configuration: cp -a /etc/nginx /etc/nginx.bak). With several sources, the destination is a place. -i asks before replacing a file, -n never replaces one, -v says what it copies.
    .synopsis = cp [-r|-a] [-p] [-i|-n] [-v] source… destination
    .option-r = copies a whole place, with everything it contains (recursive).
    .option-R = the same as -r.
    .option-a = archive: like -r -p (everything, the rights and dates kept).
    .option-p = keeps the rights and the dates (preserve); root also keeps the owner.
    .option-i = asks before replacing an existing file (interactive): y to replace.
    .option-n = never replaces an existing file (no clobber).
    .option-v = says what is copied (verbose).
    .option-f = replaces without asking (force).
    .example-1 = make a copy next to it
    .example-2 = copy a file from elsewhere to here (.)
    .example-3 = save nginx's configuration before touching it
    .example-4 = copy, asking before replacing, and say it
man-curl = fetches a web page, like a browser but without displaying it nicely. It also talks to HTTP APIs: -s keeps it quiet (no progress, no error message unless -S), -G sends the data given with -d or --data-urlencode in the URL's query string rather than in a POST, --data-urlencode NAME=VALUE encodes the value (spaces, braces, quotes of a PromQL query), -X chooses the method (POST…), -f fails with error 22 on an HTTP error. Braces and brackets in a URL are curl's patterns: quote the URL and add -g (--globoff), or pass the query with --data-urlencode. -I only asks for the response header (the status code, for example 200 = OK). Over HTTPS (https://…), curl checks the server's certificate like a browser: validity dates, site name, trusted authority; if there is a problem, it stops with error 60. -4 and -6 choose the address family (IPv4, IPv6); without them, curl tries IPv6 first, then IPv4 (happy eyeballs): a site broken in IPv6 can still show. An IPv6 address goes in brackets: http://[2001:db8::80]/. -v (verbose) shows each attempt ("Trying [addr]:80...", "Connection refused"), the request sent (>) and the headers received (<).
    .synopsis = curl [-4|-6] [-v] [-I] [-i] [-k] [-s] [-G] [-X METHOD] [-d DATA] [--data-urlencode NAME=VALUE] address
    .option-4 = IPv4 only.
    .option-6 = IPv6 only.
    .option-v = shows the connection, the request and the response headers (verbose).
    .option-I = only asks for the response header (HEAD).
    .option-i = shows the response header, then the page (include).
    .option-k = does not check the certificate (insecure): only for a test, never for real.
    .option-s = silent: no progress meter, and no error message unless -S is given too (the exit code still tells).
    .option-G = sends the data of -d and --data-urlencode in the query string of a GET (get).
    .option-X = the request's method: -X POST, -X DELETE…
    .example-1 = only the header of the local site
    .example-2 = the site in IPv6 only, step by step
    .example-3 = the booking page in IPv4 only
    .example-4 = the local site through the IPv6 loopback
    .example-5 = Prometheus's targets, indented by jq
    .example-6 = a PromQL query, its braces and quotes encoded by curl
man-cut = splits each line into fields according to the separator and keeps field N (or N,M). E.g.: cut -d " " -f 1 keeps the first word.
    .synopsis = cut -d separator -f N [file]
man-dmesg = prints the kernel messages (display message): the hardware detected at boot, disk errors… and the OOM killer ("Out of memory: Killed process"), when the kernel kills a process for lack of memory. Each line starts with the time elapsed since boot, in seconds; -T turns it into a date and time. Reserved for root on Debian; journalctl -k shows the same messages.
    .synopsis = dmesg [-T]
    .option-T = readable date and time, instead of seconds since boot.
    .example-1 = the kernel messages
    .example-2 = with the date and time
    .example-3 = only those from the OOM killer
man-df = free space on storage (disk free): disk, USB stick… -h writes in K, M, G. -i counts inodes instead of bytes: each file (and each place) uses an inode, and their number is fixed when the file system is created. So storage can be "full" (No space left on device) with free space left, if it has no inode left.
    .synopsis = df [-h] [-i] [place]
    .option-h = readable sizes: K, M, G.
    .option-i = the inodes (IUsed: used, IFree: free) instead of bytes.
    .example-1 = the space on each storage device
    .example-2 = the inodes on each storage device
    .example-3 = the inodes of one storage device, in readable numbers
man-dig = asks the DNS a question: "what is the IP address of this name?" (domain information groper). @server chooses the DNS server to query (otherwise, the one in /etc/resolv.conf); you can ask for another record type than A (IPv4 address): AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), NS (the zone's servers), TXT, SOA (including the serial number). +short keeps only the answer. In the answer, the number before IN is the TTL: how many seconds a resolver may keep this answer in its cache. The aa flag (authoritative answer) says it comes from a server that is authoritative for the zone.
    .synopsis = dig [@server] name [type] [+short]
    .example-1 = the address of a name, asked of the usual resolver
    .example-2 = the same question, asked directly of an authoritative server
    .example-3 = the DNS servers of a zone
    .example-4 = the SOA: its serial number changes with every modification
man-docker = runs applications in containers: each with its own files, isolated from the rest of the machine, from an image (a ready-to-use application, downloaded with pull). docker run creates and starts a container: -d in the background, --name to name it, -p host:container publishes a port, -v name:/path mounts a volume into it (or -v /directory:/path a directory of the machine), -e VARIABLE=value, --restart unless-stopped so it comes back with the machine, --user uid:gid (or -u) to run it under another account than the one the image plans for: without USER in the image nor --user, the container runs as root. A container's uid is the same as on the machine (root inside is root on the mounted directories); a name (--user node) must exist in the image's /etc/passwd. --read-only forbids writing anywhere but in volumes, --cap-drop ALL removes root's special privileges, --security-opt no-new-privileges prevents regaining any. Beware: whatever a container writes outside a volume lives in its own layer, and disappears with it (docker rm); updating an application means deleting the container and creating a new one with the new image. docker ps (-a) lists containers, logs shows their log, exec runs a command inside, cp copies files between the machine and a container. You must be root (or in the docker group, which amounts to the same thing).
    .synopsis = docker run [-d] [--name name] [-p port:port] [-v volume:/path] [-e VAR=value] [--restart policy] [--user uid:gid] [--read-only] [--cap-drop ALL] [--security-opt no-new-privileges] image ; docker ps [-a] ; docker logs container ; docker exec [-u user] container command ; docker cp source destination ; docker stop|start|rm container ; docker pull image ; docker images ; docker volume ls|create|rm ; docker inspect container
    .example-1 = the containers, even stopped ones
    .example-2 = a container's log
    .example-3 = what is in a directory of the container
    .example-4 = copy a directory from the container to the machine
    .example-5 = a container in the background, its port published, its data in a volume, and which comes back with the machine
    .example-6 = a container's volumes
    .example-7 = which account does the container run as?
    .example-8 = a container that does not run as root
man-docker-compose = runs several containers that go together (an app and its database, for example), described in the compose.yaml file of the current directory: under services:, one block per service, with image:, ports:, volumes:, environment:, depends_on: (start first), restart: and user: (the account, like docker run --user; for example user: "1001:1001", in quotes). Compose creates a network for the project: each service can be reached on it by its name (db, webapp). Named volumes are declared under volumes: at the bottom of the file. ${"{"}VARIABLE{"}"} is replaced by the value from the directory's .env file (handy to keep secrets out of the file). You type docker compose (with a space): it is a docker subcommand.
    .synopsis = docker compose up [-d] ; docker compose down [-v] ; docker compose ps ; docker compose logs [service] ; docker compose config ; docker compose restart [service] ; docker compose exec service command
    .example-1 = create and start (or recreate) the services, in the background
    .example-2 = the state of the services
    .example-3 = a service's log
    .example-4 = the file as Compose understands it, variables replaced
    .example-5 = stop and delete everything (with -v, the volumes too: their data is lost)
man-diff = compares two files line by line, and shows what to change to go from the first to the second. In normal format, "3c3" means: line 3 of the first becomes line 3 of the second (c for change, d for delete, a for add); lines starting with < come from the first file, those with > from the second. -u gives the unified format, the one of git diff: - for what goes, + for what comes, with three lines around. With two places, diff compares the files with the same name, and -r goes down into sub-places. Nothing is printed if the files are identical. --color shows what goes in red and what comes in green (on screen only; --color=always even in a pipe).
    .synopsis = diff [-u] [-r] [-q] [--color[=WHEN]] file1 file2
    .option-u = unified format (unified): - and +, with three lines of context.
    .option-r = also compares sub-places, recursively.
    .option-q = only says whether the files differ (quiet).
    .example-1 = what changes between two files
    .example-2 = the same thing, in git diff format
    .example-3 = everything that differs between two places
man-du = measures the space used (disk usage), place by place. A file with several names (hard links) is only counted once. -s gives only the total, -h writes in K, M, G, -d N stops N levels deep. --inodes counts files (inodes) instead of bytes: to find the place that holds thousands of files. -b gives the apparent size in bytes (GNU du only). E.g.: du -sh *
    .synopsis = du [-s] [-h] [-d N] [--inodes] [place...]
    .option-h = readable sizes: K, M, G.
    .option-s = a single total per place (summary), without the details.
    .option-d = only show N levels below the given place (--max-depth=N).
    .option-b = the apparent size in bytes (GNU du only).
    .example-1 = the space taken by each log
    .example-2 = the number of files, place by place, one level deep
    .example-3 = the number of files in each place
man-echo = prints the text. Very useful with > to write into a file: echo "hello" > note.txt. With -e, backslash escapes are read: \n a new line, \t a tab, \\ a backslash. Only words at the start made of -n, -e and -E are options (echo -x prints -x). A shell built-in (help echo, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = echo [-neE] text
    .option-n = no new line at the end.
    .option-e = reads the escapes \n, \t, \\, \0NNN (octal), \xHH, \c (stops there).
    .option-E = does not read them (the default).
    .example-1 = write a line into a file
    .example-2 = two lines, with an escape
man-exit = leaves the shell. In an ssh session, closes the connection and takes you back to the machine you came from (here, your workstation).
    .synopsis = exit
man-find = searches for files in a place and all its sub-places. E.g.: find . -name "*.pdf"; -type f keeps only files, -type d only directories; -mtime +30 those modified more than 30 days ago (-mtime -7: less than 7 days); -maxdepth 1 does not go down into sub-places; -delete deletes what is found (careful: run it first without -delete to see the list); -printf FORMAT prints each one your way (%p the path, %f the name, %s the size, %u the owner, %m the rights, \n a new line: GNU find only). find walks through places by itself: it deletes hundreds of thousands of files where rm * fails ("Argument list too long").
    .synopsis = find [place] [-maxdepth N] [-name pattern] [-type f|d] [-mtime [+|-]N] [-delete]
    .example-1 = the text files, here and below
    .example-2 = the statistics older than 30 days (to look)
    .example-3 = delete them
man-free = shows the machine's RAM (and swap): total, used, free, the disk cache (buff/cache) and above all available, what is really left for starting programs. -h writes in Ki, Mi, Gi; -m in megabytes. To find out WHO is taking the memory: top or ps aux.
    .synopsis = free [-h] [-m]
    .option-h = readable sizes: Ki, Mi, Gi.
    .option-m = in megabytes (MiB).
    .option-b = in bytes.
    .option-k = in kilobytes (default).
    .option-g = in gigabytes.
    .example-1 = the memory, in readable sizes
man-git =
    keeps the history of a directory (a "repository"). The main commands:
      git init                    turns the place into a repository
      git status                  what has changed since the last commit
      git add file (or .)         stages a file for the next commit
      git commit -m "message"     records a snapshot of all the staged files
      git commit -am "message"    the same, first staging all modified tracked files
      git rm file                 deletes a file and stops tracking it
      git log [--oneline] [-p]    the history of commits (-p: with each one's changes)
      git diff [ID] [file]        the differences (- before, + after)
      git show [ID]               a commit and its changes
      git restore --source=ID f   puts file f back as it was in commit ID
    To work with others, or to publish, you send your commits to another repository (a "remote"):
      git init --bare place.git   creates a bare repository (no working copy), like on a server
      git clone place.git         copies a repository (its remote is called origin)
      git remote add name place   declares a remote; git remote -v lists them
      git push name main          sends your commits to the remote
      git pull name main          fetches the remote's commits
    A bare repository can react to a push: its hooks (hooks/pre-receive, hooks/post-receive) are scripts run on each reception, if they are executable (chmod +x). pre-receive can refuse the push (non-zero exit code). To deploy, post-receive checks the files out into a place: git --work-tree=place checkout -f main.
    On screen, status, diff, log and show are in colour (color.ui=auto): what goes in red, what comes in green; --no-color turns them off.
    A commit is designated by the start of its identifier (at least 4 characters), or by HEAD (the latest), HEAD~1 (the one before)…
    .synopsis = git [--git-dir=repository] [--work-tree=place] command
man-grep = prints the lines that contain the pattern. The pattern is a regular expression: . means "any character", ^ the start of the line, $ the end, [abc] one character among a, b, c, and * "as many times as you like" whatever precedes it; to search for an actual dot, write \. (in single quotes). -E also allows + ? | and parentheses (extended expressions), -P Perl's (\d for a digit…, GNU grep only), -F searches for the text as is. -i ignores case, -v keeps the lines that do NOT contain it, -c counts the lines, -q prints nothing (only the exit code matters: success if the pattern is found, useful in a script). Without a file, searches what comes in through a pipe. "grep word *" searches all the files in the place. On screen, what matches is highlighted in red (--color=auto, like Debian's alias); --color=never turns it off. -n numbers the lines, -o prints only what matches, -m NUM stops after NUM lines. Around each line found, -A NUM also prints the NUM lines after it, -B NUM those before, -C NUM (or -NUM) both: these context lines have a "-" after the name and the number (file-12-text) where the lines found have a ":" (file:13:text), and "--" separates groups that don't follow each other (--group-separator=SEP changes it, --no-group-separator removes it). -c ignores the context. -r searches a whole tree (the current directory without a file; --include=GLOB, --exclude=GLOB and --exclude-dir=GLOB choose the files), -l lists only the names of the files that match, -L those that don't. -w wants whole words, -x whole lines; -e gives a pattern (several -e: any of them), -f reads the patterns from a file. -s hides the messages about missing or unreadable files. The exit code is 0 if a line is found, 1 if none, 2 after an error.
    .synopsis = grep [-i] [-v] [-c] [-q] [-n] [-o] [-w|-x] [-l|-L] [-r|-R] [-s] [-E|-F] [-h|-H] [-m NUM] [-A NUM] [-B NUM] [-C NUM] [--include=GLOB] [--exclude=GLOB] [--exclude-dir=GLOB] [--color[=WHEN]] {"{"}pattern | -e pattern... | -f file{"}"} [file...]
    .option-i = ignores the difference between uppercase and lowercase.
    .option-v = inverts the search: keeps the lines that do NOT contain the pattern.
    .option-c = counts the matching lines instead of printing them.
    .option-q = silent (quiet): prints nothing, succeeds if the pattern is found, fails otherwise.
    .option-E = extended regular expressions: + ? | ( ) have their special meaning.
    .option-P = Perl-compatible regular expressions (\d, \w, lookarounds): GNU grep only.
    .option-F = searches for the pattern as is, without regular expressions (fixed strings).
    .option-h = never prints the file name before the lines (by default, it does with several files).
    .option-H = always prints the file name before the lines, even with a single file.
    .option-n = prints each line's number before it (--line-number).
    .option-e = PATTERN: the pattern to search for (--regexp); repeat it to search for several, a pattern starting with - too.
    .option-f = FILE: reads the patterns from the file, one per line (--file).
    .option-w = only matches whole words: not inside a longer word (--word-regexp).
    .option-x = only matches whole lines (--line-regexp).
    .option-l = prints only the names of the files that contain a matching line (--files-with-matches).
    .option-L = prints only the names of the files that contain none (--files-without-match).
    .option-r = searches every file of the directories, recursively; without a file, the current directory (--recursive). Skips symbolic links met on the way.
    .option-R = like -r, but follows symbolic links (--dereference-recursive).
    .option-s = no message about missing or unreadable files (--no-messages); the exit code still says 2.
    .option-o = prints only the parts that match, one per line (--only-matching).
    .option-m = NUM: stops after NUM lines found (--max-count); the trailing context still follows.
    .option-A = NUM: also prints the NUM lines after each line found (--after-context).
    .option-B = NUM: also prints the NUM lines before each line found (--before-context).
    .option-C = NUM: the NUM lines before and after (--context); -NUM is the same.
    .example-1 = the lines that mention the Eraser
    .example-2 = in all the files here, uppercase or not
    .example-3 = a pattern with spaces, in quotes
    .example-4 = the lines that contain ".env" (the dot escaped)
    .example-5 = the GET or POST requests to the till app
    .example-6 = each error with its line number and the two lines around it
    .example-7 = the first failure and the three lines that follow it
    .example-8 = every .conf file under /etc that grants sudo without a password, with the line number
    .example-9 = the lines that contain the word root or admin
    .command-1 = grep Eraser chronicle.txt
    .command-2 = grep -i eraser *
    .command-3 = grep "my password" journal.txt
man-gpg = encrypts, decrypts, signs and checks files (GnuPG). Two everyday jobs for a sysadmin: encrypt a backup, and check that a downloaded file really comes from who it claims. --list-keys (-k) shows the keyring, --list-secret-keys (-K) the keys you can sign and decrypt with, --import a key someone sent you. gpg -c file encrypts it with a passphrase (symmetric) into file.gpg; gpg -e -r name file encrypts it to a person's public key; gpg -d file.gpg (or just gpg file.gpg) decrypts it. gpg -b file makes a detached signature file.sig (or file.asc with -a), gpg --clearsign wraps a readable text with its signature; gpg --verify file.asc file checks a signature and says "Good signature" or "BAD signature", or warns when the key is not in your keyring. -o chooses the output file, -a writes ASCII (.asc) rather than binary, -u picks which key signs. In the game the cryptography is simulated: it teaches the gestures, it does not really hide anything.
    .synopsis = gpg -k | -K ; gpg --import key.asc ; gpg [-a] -c|-e -r name file ; gpg -d file.gpg ; gpg [-a] -b|--clearsign file ; gpg --verify file.asc [file]
    .option-k = list the public keys of the keyring.
    .option-K = list the secret keys (the ones you can sign or decrypt with).
    .option-a = ASCII output (.asc), easier to email, instead of binary (.gpg/.sig).
    .option-o = write the result to this file instead of the screen or the default name.
    .option-r = the recipient of an encryption (a name, email or key id); may be repeated.
    .option-u = which secret key signs (by default, the only one).
    .example-1 = see the keys you have
    .example-2 = encrypt a backup with a passphrase (symmetric)
    .example-3 = encrypt a backup to someone's public key
    .example-4 = decrypt it again
    .example-5 = import a project's release key
    .example-6 = check that a download is signed by that key
man-head = prints the beginning of a file (the first 10 lines, or N).
    .synopsis = head [-n N] [file]
    .option-n = number of lines to print (10 by default): head -n 3 file.
man-kill = sends a signal to a process (by default TERM: "please stop"). PID is its number, given by ps or pgrep. -SIGNAL or -s SIGNAL chooses another one, by name or number: -KILL (9) ends it at once, without letting it clean up; -HUP (1) often asks a daemon to reread its configuration; -STOP and -CONT pause and resume it; -0 only checks that the process exists. A process can catch or ignore TERM, HUP and INT (a script's trap), never KILL nor STOP. kill -l lists the signals. You can only signal your own processes (or all of them, as root). A zombie (<defunct>) reacts to no signal: it is already dead, only its parent can reap it.
    .synopsis = kill [-SIGNAL | -s SIGNAL] PID… ; kill -l
    .example-1 = ask process 3302 to stop (TERM)
    .example-2 = end process 3301 at once (KILL, as root)
    .example-3 = the signals' names and numbers
    .example-4 = does process 3301 still exist? (exit code 0 if yes)
man-last = lists the latest logins to the machine (from /var/log/wtmp): who, from which address, when, and for how long (still logged in: still connected). With an account name, only its logins; -n N, only the last N. Useful for piecing together a night of incident: who stepped in, and at what time.
    .synopsis = last [-n N] [account…]
    .option-n = only the N most recent logins.
    .example-1 = all recent logins
    .example-2 = those of one account
man-less = shows a file page by page. Here, files are short: less does the same as cat.
    .synopsis = less file
man-ln = creates a link (link). With -s, a symbolic link: a small file that holds the path of another, and that commands follow to read, write or enter (ls -l shows it with an arrow ->). If the destination is a place, the link inside it takes the target's name. Beware: a relative path is read from the link's place, not from where you are; when in doubt, give an absolute path. Deleting the link (rm) does not delete the target file. Without -s, a hard link: a second name for the same file. To replace a link that points to a place (a "current" link to the live version), you need -f and -n: without -n, ln goes into the target place and creates the new link there. Replacing a link is instantaneous: that is how you switch from one version to another, and how you roll back.
    .synopsis = ln [-s] [-f] [-n] [-v] target [link | place]
    .option-s = symbolic link (otherwise: hard link).
    .option-f = replaces the link if it already exists (force).
    .option-n = if the destination is a link to a place, treat it as a file (no-dereference): with -f, the link is replaced.
    .option-v = says what was done (verbose).
    .example-1 = enable an nginx site, the Debian way
    .example-2 = see the links and what they point to
    .example-3 = switch the current link to another version, in one go
man-ls = lists the content of a place (list). -a also shows hidden files, whose name starts with a dot. -l (long) shows the permissions, the owner and the size; -h writes sizes in K, M, G; -n shows the numbers (uid, gid) instead of the names. Names come in alphabetical order: -t sorts by date (the most recent first: ls -ltr puts the last modified file at the bottom), -S by size (the largest first), -r reverses the order; -R also lists every place below. On screen, names are in colour (--color=auto, like the alias in Debian's ~/.bashrc): places in blue, links in cyan, programs in green; --color=never turns colours off, --color=always keeps them even in a pipe.
    .synopsis = ls [-aAlhndi1] [-t|-S|-U|-X] [-r] [-R] [--sort=WORD] [--color[=WHEN]] [place…]
    .option-a = shows everything (all), including hidden files, whose name starts with a dot, and the places . and ..
    .option-A = like -a, without . and .. (almost all).
    .option-l = long format: type, permissions, owner, group, size and name, one entry per line.
    .option-h = with -l, writes sizes in a readable way (human-readable): 1.2K, 34M, 5G.
    .option-n = like -l, but with the numbers of the owner (uid) and the group (gid) instead of their names (numeric).
    .option-i = shows the inode number of each file: two names with the same number are the same file (a hard link).
    .option-d = shows the place itself, not what it contains (directory): with -l, its own permissions.
    .option-t = sorts by modification time, the most recent first (--sort=time).
    .option-r = reverses the order (reverse).
    .option-S = sorts by size, the largest first (--sort=size).
    .option-R = also lists the places below, each under its name (recursive).
    .option-U = does not sort: the order the place keeps them in (--sort=none).
    .option-X = sorts by extension (--sort=extension).
    .option-1 = one name per line, even on screen.
    .example-1 = what is here
    .example-2 = everything, even what is hidden
    .example-3 = the permissions of the place memories
    .example-4 = the permissions of the /srv/till directory itself
    .example-5 = the logs, the most recently written at the bottom
    .example-6 = the largest logs first
    .example-7 = nginx's configuration, every level
    .command-3 = ls -l ~/memories
man-mail = sends mail (mailx): the text comes through a pipe (echo … | mail -s subject recipient). -s gives the subject; -E sends nothing if the text is empty (handy after a command that stays silent when all is well). On a server, local mail goes to /var/mail/account; /etc/aliases can forward it elsewhere (root: zoe). With no recipient, mail lists the messages received.
    .synopsis = mail [-E] [-s subject] recipient… ; mail
    .example-1 = send one line to zoe
    .example-2 = send only if the command wrote something
    .example-3 = read your mailbox
    .command-1 = echo "Hello" | mail -s Test zoe
    .command-2 = command | mail -E -s Alert zoe
man-man = displays the manual of a command (manual). It's the first reflex when you no longer remember how a command works: its syntax, its options, examples. On a real Linux, the page opens full screen: you scroll with the arrow keys, search with /word and quit with q.
    .synopsis = man [-k word] command
    .option-k = searches for a word in the summary of every command (like apropos), to find the command that does what you want.
    .example-1 = the manual of ls
    .example-2 = which commands talk about copying?
    .example-3 = a quick summary, without opening the manual
man-mkdir = creates a new place, that is, a directory (make directory). -p also creates the missing parent places, and says nothing if the place already exists.
    .synopsis = mkdir [-p] place
    .option-p = also creates the parent places (parents), with no error if they exist.
    .example-1 = create a place
    .example-2 = create a place and all its parents at once
man-mv = moves a file into a place (move), or renames it if the destination is a new name. With several sources, the destination is a place. -i asks before replacing a file, -n never replaces one, -v says what it moves.
    .synopsis = mv [-i|-n|-f] [-v] source… destination
    .option-i = asks before replacing an existing file (interactive): y to replace.
    .option-n = never replaces an existing file (no clobber).
    .option-v = says what is moved (verbose).
    .option-f = replaces without asking (force).
    .example-1 = move crab into the place Bucket
    .example-2 = rename
    .example-3 = move and rename at once
    .example-4 = move the logs into old/, asking before replacing
    .command-1 = mv crab Bucket
    .command-2 = mv draft.txt letter.txt
    .command-3 = mv draft.txt ~/Harbour/letter.txt
man-nc = netcat, the Swiss army knife of networking. Here, to test whether a TCP port on a machine answers, as anyone on the internet would: -z tests without sending anything, -v reports the result. "succeeded": the port is open; "Connection refused": nobody is listening (or the firewall rejects); "timed out": the firewall drops the packets. Testing from ANOTHER machine shows what the world sees; from the machine itself, the firewall does not get involved. -4 and -6 choose the address family (by default, IPv6 first when the name has one).
    .synopsis = nc -zv [-4|-6] machine port[-port]
    .example-1 = does port 22 (SSH) of the machine workshop answer?
    .example-2 = ports 5430 to 5435
    .example-3 = the database port, in IPv6
man-nginx = the web server (and proxy). You don't start it by hand: systemd takes care of it (systemctl start, reload nginx). Its configuration starts from /etc/nginx/nginx.conf, which includes the sites in /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/: one server block per site, with listen (the port; default_server for the site that answers when no name matches, only one per port), server_name (the site's names), root (the directory of the pages) and index (the home page). Every directive ends with a semicolon. Before any reload: sudo nginx -t, which gives the file and line of the error. Several sites on one machine (virtual hosts): one server block per site, each with its own server_name. In a location block (a path prefix), proxy_pass hands the visit to another program (reverse proxy): with an address ending in a slash (http://127.0.0.1:3000/), the location prefix is replaced by /, otherwise the path is passed as is. return 301 https://$host$request_uri; redirects elsewhere. Service errors go to /var/log/nginx/error.log. Rate limiting: limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=name:10m rate=2r/s; (in the http block, one key per address, 2 requests per second) then limit_req zone=name burst=10 nodelay; where you want to limit: above the rate, a burst of 10 requests is still served; beyond that, refusal (503, or the code from limit_req_status, for example 429). deny address; refuses an address (403). Spreading load across several copies of an app: an upstream name {"{"} server 127.0.0.1:3001 weight=9; server 127.0.0.1:3002 weight=1; {"}"} block in the http block (a file from sites-enabled is included there), then proxy_pass http://name/; weight gives each one's share (here 9 visits out of 10 for the first), down sets it aside, backup is only used if the others don't answer. A server that doesn't answer is skipped; a page that answers 500 reaches the visitor as is. IPv6: listen [::]:80; listens in IPv6 only (ipv6only=on by default), next to listen 80; for IPv4; listen [::]:80 ipv6only=off; takes both, and then must not sit next to listen 80; (the sockets clash when nginx opens them, which nginx -t does not do). proxy_pass http://localhost:3000/ tries every address of localhost (::1, then 127.0.0.1); write the one the app listens on.
    .synopsis = nginx -t ; nginx -T ; nginx -v
    .option-t = checks the configuration (test) and stops.
    .option-T = checks, then displays the whole configuration read, file by file.
    .option-v = displays the version.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading
    .example-2 = reread the whole configuration, includes and all
    .example-3 = the nginx version
man-pg_dump = backs up a PostgreSQL database while it runs, consistently: everything it contained at a given moment (copying the files of /var/lib/postgresql while the database is writing does not guarantee that). With no option, writes SQL; -Fc writes an archive in "custom" format, to be read back with pg_restore (which can restore just one table). -f gives the file (written with the rights of the account running pg_dump), -t takes only one table.
    .synopsis = pg_dump [-Fc] [-f file] [-t table] database
    .example-1 = an archive of the gamelib database, to read back with pg_restore
    .example-2 = the database's SQL, on screen
man-pg_restore = restores an archive written by pg_dump -Fc into a database (-d), which must exist (createdb). -l lists what the archive contains, -t restores only one table, --data-only (-a) only the data (into tables that already exist), --clean (-c) first deletes what exists. A dump in text format is read back with psql -f.
    .synopsis = pg_restore [-l] [-d database] [-t table] [--data-only] [--clean] archive
    .example-1 = what the archive contains
    .example-2 = restore it into a test database
    .example-3 = put back only the rows of the loans table
man-ping = sends small messages (ICMP "echo") to a machine and measures the round-trip time. Here, 4 sends by default (on Linux, ping keeps going until Ctrl+C). Beware: many servers don't answer ping, without being down for all that. IPv6: -6 (or ping6) uses IPv6, -4 IPv4; without either, a name with an AAAA record is reached in IPv6. An IPv6 output says "56 data bytes". A link-local address (fe80::…) exists on every link at once: give the interface after a % (fe80::1%eth0) or with -I eth0, otherwise ping refuses ("connect: Invalid argument"). "Destination unreachable: Address unreachable" comes from a router that found nobody at that address on its link.
    .synopsis = ping [-4|-6] [-c N] [-I interface] machine ; ping6 machine
    .example-1 = three pings to an IPv4 address
    .example-2 = three pings in IPv6, to a name
    .example-3 = the router's link-local address, on eth0
man-ping6 = ping in IPv6: the same program as ping -6 (on Debian, a link to ping). A link-local address needs its interface: ping6 fe80::1%eth0.
    .synopsis = ping6 [-c N] [-I interface] machine
    .example-1 = the IPv6 loopback
    .example-2 = the router's link-local address, on eth0
man-ps = lists processes, that is, the programs currently running (process status). With no option: yours; "ps -p PID": that one only; "ps aux": everyone's, with their share of processor (%CPU) and memory (%MEM). "ps -ef": everyone's too, with each process's parent (PPID), the one that started it. "ps -u account": those of one account. -o picks the columns (pid, ppid, user, ni: the nice value, %cpu, %mem, stat, comm: the program's name, args: the whole command line), --sort orders by one of them (a minus sign first: descending). A process marked <defunct> is a zombie: finished, but its parent hasn't read its result yet.
    .synopsis = ps [aux] ; ps -ef ; ps -u account ; ps -eo COLUMNS [--sort=[-]COLUMN]
    .example-1 = everyone's processes
    .example-2 = with each one's parent
    .example-3 = marc's processes
    .example-4 = PID, nice value, CPU and name, the busiest first
    .example-5 = only process 3301 (nothing but the header, exit code 1, if it doesn't exist)
man-psql = the PostgreSQL client: sends an SQL query to a database and displays the result. You connect under your account's name: for the database administrator, sudo -u postgres psql. -d picks the database, -c gives the query (in quotes), -f reads a file of SQL (a text-format dump, for example), -l lists the databases. A few commands: \l (the databases), \dt (the tables), \d table (its columns), SELECT count(*) FROM table; (how many rows). Beware, DELETE FROM table; with no WHERE deletes ALL the rows. For the server: pg_stat_activity (one row per connection: who, from where, its state: active, idle, idle in transaction, and its last query), pg_terminate_backend(pid) (ends a connection), SHOW setting, ALTER SYSTEM SET setting = 'value' (written to postgresql.auto.conf) then SELECT pg_reload_conf(); (or systemctl reload postgresql; some settings, like max_connections, need a restart). EXPLAIN SELECT … shows the plan PostgreSQL chooses (Seq Scan: it reads the whole table; Index Scan: it goes through an index); EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs the query and adds the real times. CREATE INDEX ON table (column) creates an index, \di lists them, \d table shows them under the columns. The superuser (sudo -u postgres) can still connect when the other roles are refused: a few connections are kept for it (superuser_reserved_connections). Roles: CREATE ROLE name LOGIN PASSWORD '…', GRANT SELECT ON table (or ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public) TO role, REVOKE, ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE owner IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO role (for the tables still to come); \du lists the roles, \dp each table's privileges (r read, a insert, w update, d delete), \ddp the default ones; SET ROLE name; runs what follows (in the same -c, separated by ;) with that role's privileges. On a standby: pg_is_in_recovery(), pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp(), pg_stat_wal_receiver, pg_promote().
    .synopsis = psql [-d database] [-c query | -f file] ; psql -l
    .example-1 = the list of databases
    .example-2 = the tables of the gamelib database
    .example-3 = how many rows in the loans table
    .example-4 = who is connected, in what state, doing what
    .example-5 = the plan of a query
man-pwd = displays the full path of the place where you are (print working directory).
    .synopsis = pwd
man-readlink = displays what a symbolic link points to. -f gives the full path, following every link: useful to know which version is live behind a "current" link.
    .synopsis = readlink [-f] link
    .option-f = the full path, all links followed (canonicalize).
    .example-1 = where the link points
    .example-2 = the full path, links followed
man-restic = backs up directories into an encrypted repository, as snapshots: each backup keeps the full state of the moment, but only new data takes up space (deduplication). You need the repository (-r, or the RESTIC_REPOSITORY variable) and its password (--password-file or -p, the RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE or RESTIC_PASSWORD variable, otherwise it asks): without the password, nothing is readable, not even the list. init creates a new repository; snapshots lists the snapshots; ls, find, diff and dump look inside; restore ID --target directory recreates the files (with their full path) under that directory (latest: the last snapshot), --include to take only part of them; forget removes snapshots according to a policy (--keep-daily 7…) or by their ID, without freeing space: prune is what deletes the data no longer needed (or forget --prune); check verifies the repository's structure, check --read-data also reads back every byte of data. The data is encrypted with a master key; each key (key list, key add, key remove ID, key passwd) unlocks it with its own password: several people or jobs can each have their own.
    .synopsis = restic -r repository [--password-file file] init|snapshots|ls ID [directory]|find pattern|diff ID ID|dump ID file|restore ID --target directory [--include path]|backup directory…|forget [--keep-daily N --keep-weekly N --keep-monthly N] [--prune] [--dry-run]|prune|check [--read-data]|stats|key list|key add [--new-password-file file]|key remove ID|key passwd
    .example-1 = the list of snapshots
    .example-2 = which snapshots contain this file, with its size and date
    .example-3 = what changed between two snapshots
    .example-4 = display a file as it was in a snapshot
    .example-5 = restore a single file, under /tmp/restore
    .example-6 = keep 7 days, 4 weeks, 12 months, and free the space (try with --dry-run first)
    .example-7 = check the repository
    .example-8 = create a new repository (the password is asked twice)
    .example-9 = the keys that open the repository (* the one you are using)
    .example-10 = add a key, with a new password (asked twice)
    .example-11 = check the repository, reading back all of its data
    .example-12 = restore the last snapshot beside, under /srv/restore
    .command-1 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password snapshots
    .command-2 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password find --long journal.txt
    .command-3 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password diff 1a2b3c4d 5e6f7a8b
    .command-4 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password dump latest /home/you/collection/journal.txt
    .command-5 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password restore 1a2b3c4d --target /tmp/restore --include /home/you/collection/journal.txt
    .command-6 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune
    .command-7 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password check
man-rndc = controls the running DNS server named (BIND). rndc reload rereads the zone files (all of them, or just one); named then notifies the secondary servers (NOTIFY), which only copy the zone again if its serial number (in the SOA) has increased. The named log (journalctl -u named) says what was loaded. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = rndc reload [zone] ; rndc status
    .example-1 = reload a zone after editing it (and increasing its serial)
    .example-2 = the server's status
man-rm = deletes a file (remove). Beware: rm goes through no bin, it's permanent. (The trash of graphical desktops, ~/.local/share/Trash, is just a folder: a file goes there with gio trash, never with rm.) -i asks for each file, -f says nothing of a missing file and never asks, -v says what it deletes, -d deletes an empty place (like rmdir).
    .synopsis = rm [-f|-i] [-v] [-d] file…
    .option-f = no error for a missing file, no question (force).
    .option-i = asks before each deletion: y to delete.
    .option-v = says what is deleted (verbose).
    .option-r = deletes whole places, everything they contain (recursive): refused in this game.
    .option-R = the same as -r.
    .option-d = deletes an empty place (dir).
    .example-1 = delete a file, forever
    .example-2 = delete the .tmp files, one question each
    .example-3 = no error if it is not there
man-rsnapshot = keeps snapshots of one or more directories: the full state of the moment, at regular intervals. Files that haven't changed from one snapshot to the next are not copied again but linked (hard links: the same file under two names, see ls -i); each snapshot looks complete, but only the changes take up space. Everything is set in /etc/rsnapshot.conf, with TABS between the items (never spaces): snapshot_root (where to store the snapshots, with a trailing slash), retain name count (a level, from most frequent to rarest, and how many to keep: retain daily 7, then retain weekly 4), backup source destination (backup /srv/share/ localhost/). rsnapshot configtest checks the file. rsnapshot daily rotates the daily level: daily.0 is the most recent, the oldest is deleted; a rarer level (weekly) takes over the oldest daily. -t shows what would be done, without doing anything. rsnapshot du measures the real space used. You run it with cron: the rarest level a little before the most frequent.
    .synopsis = rsnapshot [-t] [-v] [-c file] configtest | du | level
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = see what a run of the daily level would do, without doing anything
    .example-3 = rotate the daily level
    .example-4 = the space taken by each snapshot
    .example-5 = a crontab line: every night at 3:30 a.m.
man-scp = copies files from one machine to another, over ssh (secure copy): the same keys, the same ~/.ssh/config (ProxyJump included). You write the other machine in front of the path: machine:path (a relative path starts from the home of the remote account). -r copies a whole directory, -P picks the port, -J goes through a gateway. For many files, or to copy only what changed, rsync is better suited. To avoid: FTP, which sends passwords and files in clear text (prefer scp, sftp, or FTPS if the host requires it).
    .synopsis = scp [-r] [-P port] [-J gateway] source… [account@]machine:destination ; scp [account@]machine:source destination
    .example-1 = send a file into the home of the remote account
    .example-2 = fetch an archive from the wiki machine
    .example-3 = send a whole directory, going through the gateway
man-sh = runs a script: a file of commands, one per line (the shell /bin/sh, dash on Debian). A script starts with #!/bin/sh; once made executable (chmod +x), it can also be run by its path: ./deployer.sh. In a script: # comments, NAME=value creates a variable read with $NAME, $(command) is replaced by what the command writes, set -e stops everything at the first command that fails, exit 1 ends in failure. "a && b" runs b only if a succeeded, "a || b" only if it failed. In this game, no if, for or while: one command per line.
    .synopsis = sh script [argument...]
    .example-1 = run a script
    .example-2 = run an executable script by its path
    .example-3 = make it executable
man-sort = sorts lines: alphabetically, or -n numerically (2 before 10), -r in reverse.
    .synopsis = sort [-n] [-r] [file]
    .option-n = sorts numbers by value (2 before 10).
    .option-r = sorts in reverse.
    .example-1 = from smallest to largest
man-ssh = connects to another machine, encrypted (Secure Shell). ssh reads ~/.ssh/config to find the machine's real name, the user and the key to use, then checks the machine's identity in ~/.ssh/known_hosts. The first time, it asks you to confirm its fingerprint (yes). You authenticate with a private key, which must be readable only by you (chmod 600), whose public key is in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote account; otherwise, if the server allows it, with a password. exit closes the connection. -J goes through an intermediate machine (a bastion, or gateway) to reach an internal machine you can't reach directly; ProxyJump does the same in ~/.ssh/config. -L port:machine:port opens a tunnel: the port on your machine leads to the service as seen from the remote machine (localhost: the remote machine itself); with -f -N, ssh keeps it open in the background without opening a session. A command after the machine name runs over there and gives control back.
    .synopsis = ssh [-G] [-i key] [-p port] [-J gateway] [-L port:machine:port] [-f] [-N] [user@]machine [command]
    .option-i = uses this private key file (identity file).
    .option-p = port to contact (22 by default).
    .option-J = goes through this machine to reach the destination (jump host).
    .option-L = tunnel: a local port leads to a service on the other side (local forwarding).
    .option-N = no session or command: only the tunnels.
    .option-f = goes to the background after connecting (with -N, for a tunnel).
    .option-G = displays the configuration that would apply (from ~/.ssh/config), without connecting.
    .example-1 = connect to the machine kingdom (name defined in ~/.ssh/config)
    .example-2 = the same without ~/.ssh/config: user, full name and key
    .example-3 = see the machines you know
    .example-4 = reach an internal machine through the gateway
    .example-5 = a tunnel to the admin page of an internal machine
    .example-6 = run a command on the remote machine
man-ssh-copy-id = installs your public key on another machine: it adds it to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote account (creating ~/.ssh with the right permissions). You have to authenticate one last time another way (the password); after that, the key is enough, and you can close password access (PasswordAuthentication no in sshd_config). By default, it takes the most recent key in ~/.ssh; -i picks one.
    .synopsis = ssh-copy-id [-i file] [account@]machine
    .example-1 = install your key on the community machine
    .example-2 = install a specific key
man-ssh-keygen = creates and manages SSH keys. With no option, or with -t ed25519, it creates a key pair: the private key (~/.ssh/id_ed25519, to keep to yourself, chmod 600) and the public key (.pub, to give to servers, in their ~/.ssh/authorized_keys; ssh-copy-id does that). A passphrase protects the private key if you lose it. -C adds a comment (often account@machine), -f picks the file, -N gives the passphrase without asking for it. It also manages the list of known machines (~/.ssh/known_hosts): when a machine has been reinstalled, its host key changes and ssh refuses to connect to it; after checking the new fingerprint by some other means, you remove the old key with -R, under the exact name written in known_hosts.
    .synopsis = ssh-keygen [-t ed25519] [-C comment] [-f file] [-N passphrase] ; ssh-keygen -R machine [-f file] ; ssh-keygen -F machine ; ssh-keygen -l -f file
    .option-t = the key type (here, ed25519).
    .option-C = a comment, written at the end of the public key.
    .option-N = the passphrase (empty: none), without asking for it.
    .option-R = removes all keys of this machine from known_hosts (the old file is kept as .old).
    .option-F = looks for this machine in known_hosts and displays its lines.
    .option-l = displays the fingerprint of the keys in a file (with -f).
    .option-f = the file to read or modify (by default ~/.ssh/known_hosts).
    .example-1 = forget the machine's old key (after checking the new fingerprint!)
    .example-2 = is the machine in known_hosts?
    .example-3 = the fingerprints of all known machines
    .example-4 = create an ed25519 key pair
man-sudo = runs a command as root, the administrator who has all rights (superuser do). Asks for YOUR password, not root's. With -u account, the command runs under an account other than root (for example postgres, for the database). Who may do what is written in /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d (man sudoers); sudo -l lists what you can do, sudo -l -U account what another account can do.
    .synopsis = sudo [-u account] command ; sudo -l [-U account]
    .example-1 = read a file restricted to root
    .example-2 = list the databases, as postgres
    .example-3 = what you are allowed to do with sudo
    .example-4 = what lina is allowed to do with sudo
man-sudoers = the sudo policy: who can run what, under which account. /etc/sudoers also reads every file in /etc/sudoers.d (@includedir line), EXCEPT those whose name contains a dot or ends with ~: "till" is read, "till.conf" is ignored. A rule: "who machines=(account) commands", for example "%till ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart till" (the till group, on all machines, as root, that command only). who: an account, %group, or ALL. Commands are written with their full path (which systemctl); with no arguments, all arguments are allowed; ALL allows everything. NOPASSWD: in front of the commands, sudo doesn't ask for a password. A single syntax error, and sudo refuses everything: always edit these files with visudo, which checks before saving.
    .synopsis = /etc/sudoers ; /etc/sudoers.d/
    .example-1 = the main policy
    .example-2 = the included files
    .example-3 = write a rule in its own file, checked before being saved
man-visudo = edits /etc/sudoers (or, with -f, another rules file, such as /etc/sudoers.d/till) checking the syntax before saving: an error in sudoers would block sudo for everyone. You edit a copy; if it has an error, visudo shows it and asks "What now?": e to go back to editing, x to quit without saving anything, Q to save anyway (danger). -c checks the policy without modifying anything. The saved file belongs to root, with mode 0440.
    .synopsis = visudo [-c] [-f file]
    .option-c = only checks (check), without modifying anything.
    .option-f = the rules file to edit (file).
    .example-1 = edit the main policy
    .example-2 = write rules in a file of /etc/sudoers.d
    .example-3 = check the whole policy
man-systemctl = manages services, those programs that run all the time (web server…), through systemd. status shows a service's state and its latest messages; start, stop, restart start it, stop it or restart it; reload makes it reread its configuration. enable makes it start with the machine, disable doesn't: start only lasts until the next reboot. --failed lists failed services. Only root can change a service's state. list-timers lists the timers (systemd's scheduled tasks) and when they next fire. To modify a service without touching the shipped file (in /usr/lib/systemd/system), you write an override (drop-in): edit opens it in the editor (/etc/systemd/system/service.service.d/override.conf), with the section ({"["}Unit] or {"["}Service]) and only the lines to change, for example After=postgresql.service, MemoryMax=512M (the memory beyond which systemd kills the service) or Restart=on-failure (restart it if it dies in failure). After writing a unit file or an override by hand, daemon-reload makes systemd reread it. cat shows the unit file and its overrides, as systemd reads them; list-dependencies shows what a unit pulls in (Requires=, Wants=), and with --after what it waits for. After too many failed starts (start-limit-hit), reset-failed lets you start it again. set-property changes a property right away and keeps it: systemctl set-property service MemoryMax=512M.
    .synopsis = systemctl [status|start|stop|restart|reload|enable|disable|is-active|is-enabled|edit|cat|reset-failed] service ; systemctl list-dependencies [--after|--before] [unit] ; systemctl set-property service property=value ; systemctl daemon-reload ; systemctl --failed ; systemctl reboot
    .example-1 = the state of nginx and its latest messages
    .example-2 = the failed services (the first reflex after a reboot)
    .example-3 = restart nginx
    .example-4 = start nginx now AND at every boot of the machine
    .example-5 = write an override for a service (e.g. After=, MemoryMax=, Restart=)
    .example-6 = limit a service's memory right away
    .example-7 = the unit file of menu and its overrides
    .example-8 = what menu pulls in when it starts
    .example-9 = what menu waits for (After=)
    .example-10 = forget menu's failures and its start limit
man-systemd-analyze = tools for systemd. calendar tests an OnCalendar= expression (a timer's): its normalized form, and when it will next fire (*:0/5 every 5 minutes; *-*-* 03:30:00 every day at 3:30 a.m.; Mon..Fri 08:00 on weekdays; hourly, daily, weekly). time says how long the last boot took (kernel, then userspace, until multi-user.target); blame lists the units by the time they took to start; critical-chain shows the chain of units that delayed the end of the boot, or a unit: after @ when the unit was ready, after + how long it took to start. verify reads a unit file the way systemd would, and says what it would ignore (an unknown key, a misspelled section, a required unit that doesn't exist); no output means nothing to report.
    .synopsis = systemd-analyze calendar EXPRESSION… ; systemd-analyze time ; systemd-analyze blame ; systemd-analyze critical-chain [UNIT] ; systemd-analyze verify UNIT|FILE
    .example-1 = every 5 minutes
    .example-2 = every day at 3:30 a.m.
    .example-3 = how long the last boot took
    .example-4 = the units, slowest first
    .example-5 = what delayed the end of the boot
    .example-6 = check a unit file before using it
man-systemd_timer = a systemd timer (NAME.timer, in /etc/systemd/system) runs a service (NAME.service, often Type=oneshot) at a fixed time or at intervals: the modern equivalent of a crontab line, with the journal as a bonus (journalctl -u NAME). In the {"["}Timer] section: OnCalendar= a time (*:0/5 every 5 minutes, daily…, to test with systemd-analyze calendar), or OnBootSec= (after boot) with OnUnitActiveSec= (after the last run: on its own, it never fires, since the service has never run yet). In {"["}Install], WantedBy=timers.target, so that it can be enabled. Then: systemctl daemon-reload, systemctl enable --now NAME.timer, and systemctl list-timers to check.
    .synopsis = /etc/systemd/system/NAME.timer and NAME.service
    .example-1 = write the timer
    .example-2 = enable and start it
    .example-3 = the list of timers, with when they next fire
man-systemd_unit = a systemd unit file (NAME.service, NAME.target…): the package's in /usr/lib/systemd/system, yours in /etc/systemd/system, and overrides (drop-ins) in NAME.service.d/*.conf. Its {"["}Unit] section says what it needs. Requires= starts the listed units too, and doesn't start this one if they fail; Wants= is the soft version (start them, carry on even if they fail). After= and Before= only give the order: if both start, this one waits until the other is ready. Alone, Requires= starts both at the same time; alone, After= orders without pulling anything in: you usually write both. BindsTo= is a stronger Requires=: this unit stops when the other stops. PartOf= propagates stop and restart: when the other unit stops or restarts, this one does too. RequiresMountsFor=/srv/data requires the mount of that path and starts after it (useful for a disk mounted with nofail, which nothing else waits for). StartLimitIntervalSec= and StartLimitBurst= set the start limit (5 starts in 10 s by default). In {"["}Install], WantedBy=multi-user.target says what systemctl enable attaches it to. A list (After=…) can be reset in an override by an empty line: After=.
    .synopsis = /etc/systemd/system/NAME.service, /usr/lib/systemd/system/NAME.service, NAME.service.d/*.conf
    .example-1 = the unit file and its overrides
    .example-2 = write an override (After=, Requires=…)
    .example-3 = what the unit pulls in
    .example-4 = look for mistakes in the file
man-systemd_service = the {"["}Service] section of a unit file: what to run and how. ExecStart= the program (in an override, ExecStart= empty first clears the package's line, then a new ExecStart=), User= the account it runs as, EnvironmentFile= a file of NAME=value variables, Type= how systemd knows it is started (simple, forking, oneshot, notify). Restart= what to do when the program exits: no (default), on-failure (when it fails), always (even when it exits cleanly). RestartSec= how long to wait before restarting (100 ms by default: much too short if what it needs is restarting too). Past the start limit (StartLimitBurst= in StartLimitIntervalSec=, in {"["}Unit]), systemd gives up: "Start request repeated too quickly", Result: start-limit-hit; systemctl reset-failed then allows a new start.
    .synopsis = {"["}Service] Type= ExecStart= User= EnvironmentFile= Restart= RestartSec=
    .example-1 = read the service's settings
    .example-2 = change Restart= or RestartSec= in an override
    .example-3 = after a start-limit-hit, allow a new start
man-systemd_network = systemd-networkd's configuration: one file per link, /etc/systemd/network/NAME.network. {"["}Match] Name= says which link (eth0, eth1…); {"["}Network] how to configure it: Address= and Gateway= (fixed address) or DHCP=yes. {"["}Link] RequiredForOnline=no tells systemd-networkd-wait-online not to wait for this link: the network counts as "online" without it (for a link that may be absent, like a second card not wired yet). networkctl reload rereads the files (and a reboot, of course).
    .synopsis = /etc/systemd/network/NAME.network
    .example-1 = the link files
    .example-2 = change eth1's configuration
    .example-3 = the links and how far each one got
man-systemd-networkd-wait-online = the service behind network-online.target: at boot, it waits until the links configured by systemd-networkd are "online", then lets the units that wait for the network start (After=network-online.target, nginx's for example). By default it waits for every link that has a .network file and isn't marked RequiredForOnline=no; it gives up after 2 minutes (--timeout=) and the boot carries on. --any: one online link is enough; --interface=eth0 (or -i eth0): wait only for that link. To change its options, an override: ExecStart= empty, then ExecStart=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-networkd-wait-online --any.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/systemd/systemd-networkd-wait-online [--any] [--interface=LINK] [--timeout=SECONDS]
    .example-1 = what it did at the last boot
    .example-2 = its unit file and its overrides
    .example-3 = change its options in an override
man-networkctl = shows the links systemd-networkd manages, and how far each one got. OPERATIONAL is the link's state: routable (configured, with an address and a route), carrier (cable connected), no-carrier (no cable), off. SETUP is the configuration's: configured, configuring (systemd-networkd is still waiting, e.g. for the cable), unmanaged (no .network file). status LINK gives the details, including the .network file used and whether the link is required for "online". reload makes systemd-networkd reread its files.
    .synopsis = networkctl [list] ; networkctl status [LINK] ; networkctl reload
    .example-1 = the links and their state
    .example-2 = the details of eth1
    .example-3 = reread the .network files
man-tail = displays the end of a file (the last 10 lines, or N). Handy for logs, where the most recent is at the end. With -f, tail does not stop: it prints the lines as they are added, until Ctrl-C. That is how you watch a server live.
    .synopsis = tail [-n N] [-f] [file]
    .option-n = number of lines to display (10 by default): tail -n 3 file.
    .option-f = follows the file: prints what gets added, until Ctrl-C.
    .option-F = like -f, and keeps going if the file is replaced (log rotation).
    .example-1 = the 5 most recent events
    .example-2 = watch the visits arrive live (Ctrl-C to stop)
man-tar = archives (tape archive): -c creates an archive from files and places, -t lists its content, -x extracts it; -z compresses (gzip); -f gives the archive's name. The stored paths are relative: -x recreates them where you are, or in the place given by -C. When extracted by root, an archive keeps the owners and permissions of its files. E.g.: tar -czf backup.tar.gz folder
    .synopsis = tar -c|-t|-x [-z] -f archive [-C place] [places...]
    .option-c = creates an archive (create).
    .option-t = lists the content of an archive.
    .option-x = extracts an archive (extract).
    .option-z = compresses or decompresses with gzip.
    .option-v = displays the files processed (verbose).
    .option-f = the archive's name follows (file).
    .option-C = extracts into this place instead of the current place (change directory).
    .example-1 = archive and compress
    .example-2 = see what's inside (and check it can be read to the end)
    .example-3 = extract here
    .example-4 = restore a backup to its place, from the root
man-tee = copies what comes through a pipe both to the screen and into a file (like a T-junction). -a appends to the end instead of overwriting. With sudo, it's root that writes the file.
    .synopsis = tee [-a] file
    .option-a = appends to the end of the file instead of overwriting it.
man-test = checks a condition, without displaying anything: succeeds if it is true, fails otherwise. -f file (a file exists), -d place (a place exists), -e (either one), -s (non-empty file), -x (executable), -n text (non-empty), -z text (empty), a = b, a != b, and for numbers -eq, -ne, -lt, -gt. In a script, you often write [ condition ] (with spaces around the brackets): it's the same command. With || exit 1, a script stops if the condition is false.
    .synopsis = test condition ; [ condition ]
    .example-1 = does index.html exist?
    .example-2 = the same, with brackets (in a script)
    .example-3 = stop the script if the file is missing
man-timedatectl = shows and sets the machine's time (systemd). With no argument (status): the local time, universal time (UTC), the time zone, whether the clock is synchronized (System clock synchronized) and whether the NTP service is running. set-timezone changes the time zone (list-timezones lists them; Europe/Paris for France); services that schedule things (cron) are then restarted. set-ntp true enables and starts systemd-timesyncd, which keeps the clock on time thanks to an NTP server (Network Time Protocol), chosen with NTP= in /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf (reread when the service starts). timesync-status shows the server being queried and whether it answers. set-time sets the time by hand (refused if NTP is active). Settings are restricted to root.
    .synopsis = timedatectl [status] ; timedatectl set-timezone zone ; timedatectl set-ntp true|false ; timedatectl timesync-status ; timedatectl list-timezones ; timedatectl set-time "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"
    .example-1 = the time, the time zone and the NTP state
    .example-2 = the time zones of Europe
    .example-3 = switch to Paris time
    .example-4 = enable NTP synchronization
    .example-5 = the NTP server and its answer
man-top = the processes using the most resources, with the machine's state at the top: how long it has been running (up) and its load average (as uptime), the tasks (zombies included), the processor (%Cpu(s): us for programs, ni for niced programs, id idle; a share of all the CPUs) and the memory. By default, sorted by %CPU; -o %MEM sorts by memory. Columns: PR and NI, the priority and the nice value (see renice); %CPU, in percent of ONE CPU (a process can go up to 100 % per CPU it keeps busy); RES, the memory actually used (in KiB, or m, g); S, the state (S sleeping, R running or waiting for a CPU, T stopped, Z zombie). To track a memory leak, run top again and compare RES. The real top refreshes every 3 seconds until q; here, it shows a snapshot (like top -b -n 1).
    .synopsis = top [-b] [-n N] [-o field] [-c]
    .option-b = "batch" mode: displays and gives control back (for a script).
    .option-n = number of updates before stopping.
    .option-o = sorts by this field: %MEM, %CPU, RES, PID.
    .option-c = the full command line instead of the program name.
    .example-1 = the most memory-hungry processes
    .example-2 = a snapshot, for a script
man-touch = creates an empty file (or updates its date if it already exists).
    .synopsis = touch file
    .example-1 = create an empty file
man-traceroute = shows the path of packets, router by router ("hop" by "hop"), with each one's response time. "* * *": that hop didn't answer.
    .synopsis = traceroute machine
man-truncate = changes the size of a file: truncate -s 0 empties it without deleting it. The program writing it can carry on (at the same spot), and the space is freed right away, unlike rm on a file that is still open.
    .synopsis = truncate -s size file...
    .example-1 = empty a huge log without stopping the program
man-umount = detaches a mounted file system (unmount). You give it the mount point or the device. Impossible while you are inside it ("target is busy"): leave the directory first. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = umount mount_point | device
    .example-1 = detach the USB stick
    .command-1 = sudo umount /mnt/usb
man-uniq = merges identical lines that follow each other. -c shows how many times each one appears. You sort first (sort) so that duplicates follow each other: sort | uniq -c
    .synopsis = uniq [-c] [file]
    .option-c = counts how many times each line repeats.
    .example-1 = count the repetitions, then sort
man-wc = counts (word count) lines (-l), words (-w) and bytes (-c). With no option: all three.
    .synopsis = wc [-l] [-w] [-c] [file]
    .option-l = counts lines.
    .option-w = counts words.
    .option-c = counts bytes.
    .example-1 = how many lines contain "erreur"?
man-whatis = displays in one line what a command is for.
    .synopsis = whatis command
man-whoami = displays which user name you are acting as (who am I).
    .synopsis = whoami
man-hostname = displays the name of the machine you are on (read from /etc/hostname). Useful when you hop from one machine to another with ssh: the prompt shows it too (you@name).
    .synopsis = hostname [-f]
    .option-f = the full name (FQDN), from /etc/hosts.
    .example-1 = which machine am I on?
    .example-2 = its full name, with the domain

man-check_disk = monitoring plugin (monitoring-plugins, the Nagios and Icinga format): the free space on a volume. It writes one line (DISK OK, WARNING or CRITICAL, then the measurements after "|") and returns a code: 0 OK, 1 WARNING, 2 CRITICAL, 3 UNKNOWN. -w 20%: warning when less than 20% is free; -c 10%: critical below 10%. -W and -K do the same for free inodes (without them, inodes are not monitored). -p picks the volume (you can give several). On Debian, the plugins are in /usr/lib/nagios/plugins, outside the PATH: you run them by their full path.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_disk -w FREE% -c FREE% [-W FREE% -K FREE%] -p directory [-p directory…]
    .example-1 = the system disk: warning below 20% free, critical below 10%
    .example-2 = the data volume, inodes included
man-check_http = monitoring plugin: does a website or web service answer? -H the machine (or -I its address), -p the port, -u the path. HTTP OK for a 2xx or 3xx response, WARNING for 4xx, CRITICAL for 5xx or if nobody answers (Connection refused). Return codes: 0, 1, 2, 3 (UNKNOWN), like all plugins.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_http -H machine [-p port] [-u path]
    .example-1 = is the radio's live stream answering?
man-check_procs = monitoring plugin: counts processes (those of one command with -C) and alerts outside a range: -c 1: wants at least 1 process (critical otherwise), -w :10 at most 10, -c 1:1 exactly 1. Return codes: 0, 1, 2, 3, like all plugins.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_procs [-C command] [-w RANGE] [-c RANGE]
    .example-1 = is the stream relay running?
man-chgrp = changes the group of a file or directory (change group). The owner can choose a group they belong to; root can choose any group. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory.
    .synopsis = chgrp [-R] group file...
    .option-R = acts on the directory and everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = give the file to the web-team group
    .example-2 = a whole directory, with its content
    .command-1 = chgrp web-team report.txt
    .command-2 = sudo chgrp -R web-team /srv/team
man-chown = changes the owner of a file (change owner), and its group with owner:group. You can give names or numbers (uid:gid), even a number that no account on the machine has. Only root can do it. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory.
    .synopsis = chown [-R] owner[:group] file...
    .option-R = acts on the directory and everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = give a file to malo
    .command-1 = sudo chown malo report.txt
    .example-2 = owner and group at once, on a whole directory
    .example-3 = by numbers: uid 1001, gid 1001
man-groups = displays the groups an account belongs to: its primary group, then its secondary groups.
    .synopsis = groups [user...]
    .example-1 = my groups
    .example-2 = malo's groups
man-host = the short version of dig: a name's addresses and mail servers, in sentences. A second argument picks the DNS server to query.
    .synopsis = host name [server]
    .example-1 = a name's addresses
    .example-2 = the same question to another server
man-ip = shows and changes the network configuration (iproute2): interfaces (ip link), their addresses (ip addr, or ip a), routes (ip route). -br gives one line per interface; -d adds details (for a VLAN: its 802.1Q number). ip link add link eth1 name eth1.10 type vlan id 10 creates the interface for VLAN 10 on eth1; ip addr add gives it an address; ip link set … up brings it up. These changes take effect right away, but disappear on reboot: to make them last, you write them in /etc/network/interfaces (man interfaces). Changing the configuration is restricted to root. -c (color) adds colours: interface names in cyan, state UP in green and DOWN in red, addresses in magenta. IPv6: -6 keeps the IPv6 family (-4 the IPv4 one): ip -6 addr shows the inet6 addresses, scope link for fe80:: (valid on one link only), scope global, "dynamic" for an address built by SLAAC from the router's advertisements; ip -6 route shows the IPv6 routes, often "default via fe80::… dev eth0 proto ra"; ip -6 addr add 2001:db8::80/64 dev eth0 adds an address; ip neigh shows the neighbours (the router, its MAC).
    .synopsis = ip [-4|-6] [-br] [-d] [-c] addr|link|route|neigh [show|add|del|set] …
    .example-1 = the interfaces and their addresses
    .example-2 = the same, one line per interface
    .example-3 = the details of an interface (the VLAN number)
    .example-4 = create the interface for VLAN 10 on eth1
    .example-5 = give it an address
    .example-6 = bring it up
    .example-7 = the routes (including the default gateway)
    .example-8 = the IPv6 addresses only
    .example-9 = the IPv6 routes
    .example-10 = add an IPv6 address (until the next reboot)
    .example-11 = the neighbours: the router and its MAC address
man-ifup = brings up an interface according to /etc/network/interfaces (ifupdown, on Debian): creates the VLAN interface if needed, gives it its address, its gateway. -a brings up all interfaces marked "auto" (as at boot). An interface already brought up by ifup is not brought up again: ifdown first. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = ifup [-a] [interface...]
    .example-1 = bring up everything marked "auto"
    .example-2 = bring up VLAN 10
man-ifdown = takes down an interface brought up by ifup (and deletes a VLAN interface). Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = ifdown [-a] [interface...]
    .example-1 = take down VLAN 10
man-interfaces = Debian's persistent network configuration (/etc/network/interfaces), read at boot and by ifup. "auto eth1.10": to bring up at boot; "iface eth1.10 inet static" opens the description of an interface, followed by its indented options: address (10.10.10.1/24, or address and netmask), gateway (the default gateway). An interface named eth1.10 is VLAN 10 of eth1 (vlan package); with another name, vlan-raw-device eth1 says on which interface. "inet manual": the interface is brought up without an address (a trunk, for example). source reads other files (/etc/network/interfaces.d/*). IPv6 has its own stanzas: "iface eth0 inet6 static" with address 2001:db8::80/64 and gateway (often the router's link-local address); by default it turns off stateless autoconfiguration (autoconf 0), and accept_ra (0, 1, 2) says whether the router's advertisements are still accepted (they give the default route); "iface eth0 inet6 auto" lets SLAAC give the address.
    .synopsis = /etc/network/interfaces
    .example-1 = the current configuration
    .example-2 = edit it
man-dnsmasq = a small DHCP (and DNS) server for a local network. It reads /etc/dnsmasq.conf and the files in /etc/dnsmasq.d, one option per line: interface= (where to listen; eth1* means eth1 and everything starting with eth1), except-interface= (where never to listen), dhcp-range=start,end[,lease-time] (a range of addresses; dnsmasq picks the one that matches the network of the interface where the request arrives), dhcp-host=MAC,name,address (reserve an address for a device), dhcp-option= (gateway, DNS: by default, the router's address). --test checks the configuration. The log (journalctl -u dnsmasq) shows each exchange (DHCPDISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK) and /var/lib/misc/dnsmasq.leases the leases given out.
    .synopsis = dnsmasq --test ; /etc/dnsmasq.d/*
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = reread it (restart the service)
    .example-3 = the addresses given out
    .example-4 = the log of exchanges
man-nft = configures the kernel firewall (nftables). Rules live in tables (family ip, ip6, or inet for both); a table holds chains; a "base" chain hooks into one stage of the packets' journey (hook input: to the machine itself; forward: through it, from one network to another; output: from it; postrouting: just before leaving) and has a policy (policy accept or drop) for whatever no rule has decided. A rule reads from left to right: conditions (iifname "eth1.10": came in through this interface; oifname: will leave through that one; ip saddr, ip daddr: addresses, or networks 10.10.20.0/24; tcp dport 443; ct state established,related: a reply to an exchange already allowed), then actions (counter counts, log writes to the kernel log, accept, drop, reject, jump to another chain). NAT is done in a chain of type nat: masquerade (at the postrouting hook) replaces the source address with the one of the outgoing interface; dnat to 10.10.40.10 (at the prerouting hook, priority dstnat) replaces the destination, before routing: the forward chain already sees the new address. A set is written between braces: iifname "{"{"} "eth1.10", "eth1.20" {"}"}". nft -f reads a whole file and applies it in one go (nothing changes if there is an error); on Debian, /etc/nftables.conf is loaded at boot by the nftables service if it is enabled (systemctl enable nftables). What you type with nft add only lasts until the next reboot. On a machine run by ufw and fail2ban, nft list ruleset shows the rules they write (table ip filter, ufw-* chains; table inet f2b-table): in the game, you look at them there without touching them. Root only. In a table inet, ip saddr only matches IPv4 packets and ip6 saddr IPv6 ones; icmp type and icmpv6 type likewise. IPv6 needs ICMPv6 to live (neighbour discovery: icmpv6 type nd-neighbor-solicit, nd-neighbor-advert, nd-router-advert): an input chain that drops it cuts the machine off in IPv6.
    .synopsis = nft [-c] [-a] -f file ; nft [-a] list ruleset|tables|table family name|chain family table chain ; nft add|insert|delete … ; nft flush ruleset
    .option-f = reads the commands from this file, and applies them all together.
    .option-c = only checks, without applying anything.
    .option-a = shows the number (handle) of each rule, so you can delete it.
    .example-1 = the rules loaded right now, with their counters
    .example-2 = check the file without loading it
    .example-3 = load the file (like systemctl restart nftables)
    .example-4 = add a rule at the end of a chain (until the next reboot)
    .example-5 = the rules of a chain, with their numbers
    .example-6 = delete rule number 7
man-sysctl = reads and changes kernel settings, stored in /proc/sys (net.ipv4.ip_forward is the file /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward). net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 lets the machine pass packets from one network to another (route them); at 0, it only keeps those addressed to it. -w changes a value right away, but the kernel forgets everything on reboot: to make a setting last, you write it in a file in /etc/sysctl.d (man sysctl.d), which systemd-sysctl applies at boot; sysctl --system applies them without waiting. Changing a value is reserved for root.
    .synopsis = sysctl [-n] key... ; sysctl -w key=value ; sysctl -p [file] ; sysctl --system ; sysctl -a
    .option-w = changes the value (write).
    .option-p = applies the settings from a file (/etc/sysctl.conf by default).
    .option-a = shows all settings (all).
    .option-n = shows only the value, without the name.
    .example-1 = is routing enabled?
    .example-2 = enable it right away (until the next reboot)
    .example-3 = apply all the settings files, as at boot
    .example-4 = the same value, read from /proc/sys
man-sysctl_d = the kernel settings applied at boot: .conf files in /etc/sysctl.d (then /etc/sysctl.conf), read in the order of their names, one "key = value" line per setting (net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1). Lines starting with # or ; are comments. The last file that sets a value wins. sysctl --system applies them without rebooting.
    .synopsis = /etc/sysctl.d/*.conf ; /etc/sysctl.conf
    .example-1 = the settings files
    .example-2 = write your own
man-conntrack = shows the kernel's connection tracking table: each exchange going through the machine, with the outgoing direction (src=… dst=…) and the expected reply. [UNREPLIED]: no reply has come back; [ASSURED]: the exchange is established in both directions. If the reply is expected towards an address other than the original one, address translation (NAT) has taken place. An exchange blocked by the firewall does not appear. Root only.
    .synopsis = conntrack -L [-p proto] [-s address] [-d address] ; conntrack -C
    .option-L = lists the tracked exchanges (list).
    .option-C = counts the tracked exchanges (count).
    .option-p = only this protocol (tcp, udp).
    .option-s = only exchanges coming from this address (source).
    .option-d = only exchanges going to this address (destination).
    .example-1 = all current exchanges
    .example-2 = those of one device
    .example-3 = how many there are
man-wg = the WireGuard tool, a VPN: an encrypted tunnel between two machines, which recognize each other by keys (like ssh). wg genkey creates a private key (keep it secret: umask 077 first), wg pubkey derives the public key from it (to give to the other side). wg show displays the interfaces, their peers and, for each one, the latest handshake: without it, the two sides have not recognized each other. Root only, except genkey and pubkey.
    .synopsis = wg genkey ; wg pubkey < private-key ; wg show [interface] ; wg showconf interface
    .example-1 = first: files created afterwards will be readable only by you
    .example-2 = create the private key, and derive the public key from it
    .example-3 = the public key, to give to the peers
    .example-4 = the interfaces and their peers (handshake, transfers)
man-wg-quick = brings a WireGuard interface up or down from its file /etc/wireguard/NAME.conf. The [Interface] section gives the address inside the tunnel (Address), the UDP port to listen on (ListenPort) and the private key (PrivateKey); each [Peer] section describes a peer: its public key (PublicKey), the addresses it is allowed to use (AllowedIPs, a /32 address for a laptop), and, to reach it, its address and port (Endpoint). Warns if the file is world-readable. The wg-quick@NAME service does the same at boot (systemctl enable wg-quick@wg0). Root only.
    .synopsis = wg-quick up|down|strip interface
    .example-1 = bring wg0 up from /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf
    .example-2 = bring it down
    .example-3 = the same at boot, through the service
man-ldapsearch = searches an LDAP directory. -x: simple authentication (without SASL); without -D, anonymously (you can read everything except passwords). The filter says which entries: (uid=sami), (memberUid=camille), (objectClass=posixAccount), and combinations (&(…)(…)), (|(…)(…)), (!(…)); after the filter, the attributes to display (dn alone, uid uidNumber…). -b gives the base to search from (default: BASE in /etc/ldap/ldap.conf); -LLL shows the entries without comments. Answers are in LDIF format.
    .synopsis = ldapsearch -x [-LLL] [-b base] [-D dn -W] [filter] [attributes...] ; sudo ldapsearch -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// …
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind), not with SASL.
    .option-L = fewer comments (-LLL: the entries only).
    .option-b = the search base (the top of the branch).
    .option-s = the scope: base (the entry alone), one (its children), sub (the whole branch, the default).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as (bind DN).
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line (it stays in the history: avoid).
    .option-H = the server address: ldap://localhost, or ldapi:/// (the local socket).
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism: EXTERNAL, with ldapi:///, presents the Unix account (root: administrator on Debian).
    .example-1 = Malik's entry
    .example-2 = the groups Camille is still a member of
    .example-3 = the account numbers (to pick a free one)
    .example-4 = as root, without a password
man-ldapadd = adds entries to an LDAP directory, described in an LDIF file (-f): each entry starts with its dn: line, followed by its objectClass lines and its attributes; a blank line separates two entries. You must authenticate (-D and -W, or -Y EXTERNAL as root): anonymously, nothing gets written. Classes require certain attributes (inetOrgPerson: cn and sn; posixAccount: uid, uidNumber, gidNumber, homeDirectory); if one is missing, slapd rejects the entry (Object class violation). ldapadd is ldapmodify -a.
    .synopsis = ldapadd -x -D dn -W -f file.ldif ; sudo ldapadd -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -f file.ldif
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-f = the LDIF file to read.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .option-c = continue after an error.
    .example-1 = add the entry described in sami.ldif, as administrator
    .example-2 = the same as root, without a password
man-ldapmodify = modifies entries of an LDAP directory, from an LDIF file: after the dn: line, "changetype: modify", then changes "add: attribute", "replace: attribute" or "delete: attribute", each followed by its values and separated from the next by a "-" line. "changetype: add" adds an entry, "changetype: delete" deletes it. To remove someone from a group: delete: memberUid, then the value.
    .synopsis = ldapmodify -x -D dn -W -f file.ldif
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-f = the LDIF file to read.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .option-a = an entry without changetype is an addition (like ldapadd).
    .option-c = continue after an error.
    .example-1 = apply the changes in groups.ldif
man-ldapdelete = deletes entries from an LDAP directory, by their DN. An entry that has children cannot be deleted. Careful: groups that mention the person (memberUid) are not updated.
    .synopsis = ldapdelete -x -D dn -W dn-to-delete...
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .example-1 = delete Camille's entry
man-ldappasswd = changes the password of an LDAP entry; the server stores it as a hash ({"{"}SSHA{"}"}). -S prompts for the new password (twice); without -S or -s, ldappasswd makes one up and displays it. A person can change their own (by authenticating with their own DN); the administrator can change everyone's.
    .synopsis = ldappasswd -x -D dn -W [-S | -s new] [target-dn]
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password (to authenticate).
    .option-w = the password to authenticate with, on the command line.
    .option-S = prompt for the new password.
    .option-s = the new password, on the command line.
    .example-1 = the administrator gives Sami a password
    .example-2 = Sami changes their own
man-ldapwhoami = tells you which identity the directory knows you by: useful to check that a password works.
    .synopsis = ldapwhoami -x [-D dn -W]
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .example-1 = does Sami's password work?
man-slappasswd = computes the hash of a password, to put in userPassword (in an LDIF) instead of the plain-text password: {"{"}SSHA{"}"} followed by the salted hash. The same password gives a different hash every time (the salt changes).
    .synopsis = slappasswd [-s password]
    .option-s = the password (otherwise it is prompted for twice).
    .example-1 = prompt for the password, and show its hash
    .example-2 = the same, with the password on the line (it stays in the history)
man-slapcat = writes the whole content of the directory in LDIF format, straight from the database: it is the directory's backup (slapadd loads it back). Root only.
    .synopsis = slapcat
    .example-1 = the whole directory
    .example-2 = back it up to a file
man-ldif = the text format of LDAP entries (LDAP Data Interchange Format). An entry: "dn:" then one "attribute: value" line per value; a blank line between two entries; lines starting with # are comments, and those starting with a space continue the previous one. For changes: "changetype: add | modify | delete" after the dn, and in a modify, blocks "add:", "replace:", "delete:" separated by "-".
    .synopsis = file.ldif
    .example-1 = an example entry
man-freeradius = the RADIUS server: it answers the enterprise Wi-Fi access points (and switches) that ask it whether a person may get in (Access-Request), with yes (Access-Accept, with attributes: the VLAN, for example) or no (Access-Reject). Its configuration is in /etc/freeradius/3.0: clients.conf (the access points allowed to ask questions: their address, ipaddr, and the shared secret, secret), mods-enabled/ (the active modules: links to mods-available/, including ldap), mods-config/files/authorize (the "users" file: DEFAULT entries with conditions such as Ldap-Group == "offices", and replies such as Tunnel-Private-Group-Id = 10; the first one that matches wins). It only reads its configuration at startup: after a change, systemctl restart freeradius. -XC checks the configuration; -X runs the server in the foreground and narrates each request (the service must be stopped first).
    .synopsis = freeradius -XC ; freeradius -X
    .option-X = in the foreground, narrating everything (debug).
    .option-C = check the configuration, then stop.
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = stop the service…
    .example-3 = … to watch the server handle the access point's requests
    .example-4 = reload the configuration (the service)
    .example-5 = the requests received, accepted or rejected
man-radtest = sends an access request to a RADIUS server, as an access point would: a name, a password, the server, a port number on the access point (0), and the client's shared secret (localhost has testing123 in clients.conf). Shows the reply: Access-Accept and its attributes, or Access-Reject.
    .synopsis = radtest user password server nas-port secret
    .example-1 = can Sami get in, and into which VLAN?
man-sftp = transfers files over SSH, interactively: once connected (same keys, same ~/.ssh/config shortcuts as ssh), you type commands at the sftp> prompt: ls, cd, pwd for the remote machine; lls, lcd, lpwd for your own; get to fetch, put to send (with -r: a whole directory); mkdir, rm, rename; bye to quit. Everything travels encrypted, like ssh. Handy for looking before taking; for a one-shot copy, use scp or rsync.
    .synopsis = sftp [-P port] [-J jump] [-i key] [account@]machine[:directory]
    .option-P = the SSH port, if it is not 22.
    .option-J = go through another machine (like ssh -J).
    .option-i = the private key to use.
    .example-1 = connect to the wiki (through the ~/.ssh/config shortcut)
    .example-2 = (at the sftp> prompt) fetch the site's whole directory
    .example-3 = (at the prompt) send a file
    .example-4 = (at the prompt) the list of files on this side
    .example-5 = (at the prompt) quit
man-lftp = a command-line FTP client, the one you use with a shared hosting service that only offers FTP. By default, lftp encrypts the connection (FTPS: AUTH TLS, setting ftp:ssl-allow) and checks the server's certificate (ssl:verify-certificate): the name of the machine you ask for must be the one in the certificate (often the hosting provider's name, not your site's). Never turn these settings off: without encryption, the password travels in plain text. mirror copies a whole directory: from the hosting to you by default, from you to the hosting with -R (reverse); --delete removes on the other side what no longer exists, --dry-run shows what would be done without doing it. -u account prompts for the password; -e runs commands then stays open, -c runs them then quits.
    .synopsis = lftp [-u account] [-e "commands"] [-c "commands"] machine ; at the prompt: mirror [-R] [--delete] [--dry-run] [source [target]], ls, cd, lcd, put, get, set, bye
    .option-u = the account (the password is prompted for; "account,password" puts it in the history: avoid).
    .option-e = commands to run, separated by semicolons, before handing control back.
    .option-c = commands to run, then quit.
    .example-1 = connect to the hosting (the password is prompted for)
    .example-2 = (at the prompt) see what publishing would send, without sending anything
    .example-3 = (at the prompt) publish the site directory to public_html, removing what is no longer there
    .example-4 = the same in one go
man-id = prints an account's identity: its number (uid), its primary group (gid) and all its groups. A file's permissions are decided with this information. -u, -g or -G print only the uid, the gid or all the group numbers; with -n, their names (id -un is whoami).
    .synopsis = id [-u|-g|-G [-n]] [user]
    .option-u = only the user's number.
    .option-g = only the primary group's number.
    .option-G = only the numbers of all the groups.
    .option-n = names instead of numbers (with -u, -g or -G).
    .example-1 = who am I, and in which groups?
    .example-2 = malo's identity
    .example-3 = my name
    .example-4 = my groups' names
man-iptables = the classic Linux host firewall (the elder of nft, and what ufw drives underneath). It decides what happens to each packet by walking the rules of a chain: INPUT for what comes in, OUTPUT for what goes out, FORWARD for what is routed through. Each chain has a default policy (ACCEPT or DROP). -L lists the rules (-n without name lookups, -v with counters, --line-numbers numbered); -S prints them as commands. -A adds a rule at the end of a chain, -I inserts it (by default at the top), -D deletes one (by its number or by repeating it), -P sets the policy, -F empties a chain. A rule is matches then a target: -p tcp --dport 22 (the protocol and the destination port), -s 10.0.0.0/8 (the source), -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED (connections already opened); -j ACCEPT, DROP (drop silently: the sender waits), REJECT (refuse at once) or LOG. The usual host firewall: accept what is already established, accept the few services you offer, then set the INPUT policy to DROP. The rules are lost on reboot unless saved (iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4). Root only.
    .synopsis = iptables [-t filter|nat] -L|-S|-A|-I|-D|-P|-F chain [rule] ; iptables -L [-n] [-v] [--line-numbers]
    .example-1 = list the rules, numeric, with counters
    .example-2 = accept new SSH connections
    .example-3 = accept the replies of connections you opened
    .example-4 = drop everything else coming in (set last!)
    .example-5 = print the rules as commands
    .example-6 = save the rules so a reboot keeps them
man-iptables-save = prints every iptables rule in the format iptables-restore reads, so you can save the firewall to a file: iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4 (netfilter-persistent then reloads it at boot). Root only.
    .synopsis = iptables-save
    .example-1 = show the current rules
    .example-2 = save them so a reboot keeps them
man-usermod = modifies an account (user modify), in particular its groups. -G gives the list of supplementary groups, separated by commas: it REPLACES the current groups. With -a (append), the groups are ADDED. -L locks an account's PASSWORD, -U unlocks it. Careful: -L does not prevent logging in with an SSH key; to close the account, you must also expire it (-e 1, as the real manual says). -s changes the login shell (/usr/sbin/nologin: no more sessions). Root only. -u gives a new uid (user ID): on NFS, the server only sees numbers, so the same person needs the same uid on every machine.
    .synopsis = usermod [-a] -G group[,group...] user ; usermod -L|-U user ; usermod -e date user ; usermod -s shell user ; usermod -u UID user
    .option-a = adds the groups from -G instead of replacing the current groups (append).
    .option-G = the list of supplementary groups, separated by commas.
    .option-L = locks the account's password (lock).
    .option-U = unlocks the account's password (unlock).
    .option-e = the account's expiry date (YYYY-MM-DD; 1: expired since 1970; empty: never).
    .option-s = the account's login shell.
    .option-u = a new number (uid) for the account; it must not be taken.
    .example-1 = add malo to the adm group, without touching their other groups
    .example-2 = careful: malo is now only in adm (their other supplementary groups are removed)
    .example-3 = lock malo's password
    .example-4 = expire marc's account: no more logins at all, not even with a key
    .example-5 = no more sessions for marc (the account stays, and so do the files)
man-userdel = deletes an account (user delete), and removes it from its groups. With -r, its home directory and mailbox go too; its other files remain, owned by a number (uid) with no account. Refuses as long as a process of the account is running (-f forces it). Its crontab no longer runs (cron ignores unknown accounts). Before deleting an account, look at what it was running. Root only.
    .synopsis = userdel [-r] [-f] account
    .option-r = also removes the home directory and mailbox (remove).
    .option-f = even if the account is still in use (force).
    .example-1 = delete marc's account, keeping their files
    .example-2 = delete the account and its home directory
man-gpasswd = manages the members of a group: -a adds an account to the group, -d removes it (delete). Removing someone from the sudo group takes sudo away from them (on Debian). Root only.
    .synopsis = gpasswd -a account group ; gpasswd -d account group
    .option-a = adds the account to the group (add).
    .option-d = removes the account from the group (delete).
    .example-1 = remove marc from the sudo group
    .example-2 = add lina to the till group
man-pkill = stops processes chosen by their name or their account, without looking up their numbers: pkill pattern (the pattern is searched for in the program name), pkill -u account (all of the account's processes). Prints nothing if it found some (exit code 0), and exit code 1 if it found none. You can only stop your own processes, or any of them as root.
    .synopsis = pkill [-SIGNAL] [-f] [-u account] [pattern]
    .option-u = only the processes of this account (user).
    .option-f = search the pattern in the whole command line (not only the program's name).
    .example-1 = stop all of marc's processes (and close their sessions)
    .example-2 = stop the processes named tail
    .example-3 = send KILL to the processes whose command line contains sync-photos
man-killall = stops every process whose program name is exactly NAME (where pkill matches a pattern): killall tail. By default it sends TERM (please stop); -SIGNAL or -s SIGNAL chooses another (-KILL ends at once, -HUP often reloads a daemon). -u account limits it to that account, -v says what it did. Exit code 1 if a name matches nothing. You can only stop your own processes, or any of them as root.
    .synopsis = killall [-SIGNAL | -s SIGNAL] [-u account] [-v] name...
    .option-u = only the processes of this account.
    .option-v = say which process was signalled.
    .example-1 = stop the tail processes (by name)
    .example-2 = ask nginx to reload its configuration (HUP)
    .example-3 = stop all of marc's processes
    .command-2 = sudo killall -HUP nginx
    .command-3 = sudo killall -u marc
man-which = gives the full path of a command: the first file with that name in the PATH directories (/usr/local/bin, /usr/bin…). Useful where the full path is required, as in sudoers or a crontab. Prints nothing (exit code 1) for a shell builtin (cd, umask…).
    .synopsis = which command…
    .example-1 = where is systemctl?
    .example-2 = where is visudo? (in /usr/sbin, with the administration commands)
man-who = lists the sessions open on the machine: who, on which terminal (pts), since when, and from which address. A session lasts as long as its sshd process is running.
    .synopsis = who
    .example-1 = who is logged in right now?
man-history = displays the history: the commands typed on this machine, numbered. The shell can run them again without retyping: !! runs the last one again (sudo !! runs it again with sudo, when you forgot sudo), !12 command number 12, !-2 the one before last, !cat the last one starting with cat. The expanded line is shown before it runs. The ↑ and ↓ arrows and Ctrl-R go through the same history. A line starting with a space is not kept. A shell built-in (help history, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = history [N] | history -c
    .option-c = clears the history.
    .example-1 = all the commands typed, numbered
    .example-2 = the last 5
    .example-3 = find an ssh command typed earlier
    .example-4 = run the last command again
    .example-5 = run it again with sudo ("Permission denied"? sudo !!)
    .example-6 = run command number 12 again
man-umask = shows or changes the session's mask (umask): the permissions REMOVED from the files and directories you create. A file starts from 666 (rw-rw-rw-), a directory from 777, minus the mask: with 022 (the default), a file is rw-r--r-- and the group cannot write to it; with 002, it is rw-rw-r--. The change only applies to the session: to keep it, write umask 002 in ~/.profile. Shell builtin (help umask, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = umask [-S] [mask]
    .option-S = shows the allowed permissions, in symbolic form (u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx).
    .example-1 = the current mask
    .example-2 = the permissions it leaves, in letters
    .example-3 = the group will be able to write to what I create (for this session)

man-journalctl = reads the systemd journal, where services write their messages (and their errors). -u keeps only one service, -n the last N lines, -b the current boot, -p err the errors, -k the kernel messages (like dmesg). --since and --until keep a time period ("2027-09-12 02:00", today, yesterday, -1h). -x adds explanations and -e jumps to the end: journalctl -xeu nginx is the classic formula when a service refuses to start. -f follows the journal live, until Ctrl-C. You must be root, or in the adm or systemd-journal group, to read everything. --disk-usage and --vacuum-size=500M (or --vacuum-time=2weeks, --vacuum-files=5) measure and trim the persistent journal (/var/log/journal); its lasting limit is SystemMaxUse= in journald.conf.
    .synopsis = journalctl [-u service] [-n N] [-b] [-k] [-p priority] [--since DATE] [--until DATE] [-x] [-e] [-f] [--disk-usage] [--vacuum-size=SIZE]
    .option-k = only kernel messages, since boot (kernel).
    .option-u = only the messages of this service (unit).
    .option-n = only the last N lines.
    .option-b = only since the machine's last boot (boot).
    .option-p = only this priority and more severe ones: err, warning, info…
    .option-x = adds explanations to known messages.
    .option-e = jumps straight to the end of the journal.
    .option-f = follows the journal: the last 10 lines, then whatever arrives, until Ctrl-C.
    .example-1 = nginx's messages
    .example-2 = the formula when a service won't start
    .example-3 = errors since the last boot
    .example-4 = the last 20 messages
    .example-5 = everything that happened between 2:00 and 3:30 on a given night
    .example-6 = watch the ssh connections live (Ctrl-C to stop)
    .example-7 = how much space the journal takes on disk
    .example-8 = trim the archived files down to 500M
man-reboot = restarts the machine. Enabled services (systemctl enable) start again on their own; the others stay stopped. Over an ssh connection, the session is cut: you have to reconnect. Root only.
    .synopsis = reboot
    .example-1 = restart the machine

man-named-checkzone = checks a BIND zone file before loading it: the syntax, the addresses, the SOA. Prints the serial number it read and OK, or the errors with the file and line. In a zone file, a name that does not end with a dot is relative to the zone: www becomes www.cafe.example., but cafe.example (without a dot) becomes cafe.example.cafe.example.; @ stands for the zone itself.
    .synopsis = named-checkzone zone file
    .example-1 = check the zone before rndc reload
man-named-checkconf = checks the configuration of named (BIND): says nothing if it is correct.
    .synopsis = named-checkconf
    .example-1 = check the configuration
man-nano = a simple text editor, in the terminal. You type directly into the file; the shortcuts are at the bottom of the screen, ^ meaning Ctrl: ^O (Ctrl+O) writes the file, ^X (Ctrl+X) quits and offers to save the changes. To edit a file owned by root, open nano with sudo. (In the game, on mobile, the shortcuts are also buttons.)
    .synopsis = nano file
    .example-1 = write notes (the file is created if it does not exist)
    .example-2 = edit a configuration file owned by root

man-sshd = the SSH server: it is the one that accepts (or refuses) ssh connections. You don't start it by hand: systemd takes care of it (ssh service). But two options are used every day, with sudo: -t checks the configuration and says nothing if it is correct; -T shows the complete configuration as sshd would apply it, once all the files are read. After a change: sudo sshd -t, then sudo systemctl reload ssh.
    .synopsis = sshd [-t | -T] [-f file]
    .option-t = checks the configuration (test): says nothing if all is well, otherwise gives the file and line of the error.
    .option-T = shows the effective configuration, value by value (in lowercase).
    .option-f = reads this file instead of /etc/ssh/sshd_config.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading the service
    .example-2 = see the value of PasswordAuthentication actually applied
man-ssh_config = the SSH client's configuration file, ~/.ssh/config: shortcuts for the machines you connect to. Each block starts with "Host alias" (* wildcards are allowed) and its lines apply when you type ssh alias. For each option, the FIRST value found wins: specific blocks first, "Host *" at the end. Useful options: HostName (the real name or address, as seen by the machine the connection starts from), User, Port, IdentityFile (the key to use), ProxyJump (go through a gateway, like ssh -J: the destination machine's name is then looked up by the gateway). ssh -G alias shows the configuration that would apply.
    .synopsis = ~/.ssh/config
    .example-1 = add or edit a shortcut
    .example-2 = see the options ssh would apply for wiki
man-sshd_config = the SSH server's configuration file: one option per line, "Keyword value". Lines starting with # are comments (often the default value). Careful: for each keyword, the FIRST value read is the one that counts, and the Include line reads other files at the place where it appears (on Debian, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf, at the very top). Useful options: PermitRootLogin no (no direct root login), PasswordAuthentication no (keys only), PubkeyAuthentication yes, AllowUsers (the only accounts allowed), Port (the listening port, 22 by default; the only exception to the first-value rule: you can repeat Port, and sshd then listens on each one). Changes only apply after sudo systemctl reload ssh; sessions already open are never cut.
    .synopsis = /etc/ssh/sshd_config
    .example-1 = edit the configuration
    .example-2 = the included files, which come BEFORE the rest of the main file

man-ufw = manages the machine's firewall (Uncomplicated Firewall): which INCOMING connections are accepted. You set a default policy (the safest: deny everything incoming), then allow the useful doors one by one, by port number (22/tcp), by service name (ssh, http) or by application profile (OpenSSH, "Nginx Full"). The first rule that matches decides. limit accepts but slows down connections that come too close together (against brute force). Before enable, allow SSH: otherwise, no new connections at all. Root only. IPv6: with IPV6=yes in /etc/default/ufw, each rule also exists in IPv6 (the "(v6)" lines); with IPV6=no, ufw leaves IPv6 unfiltered. That file is read when ufw starts (ufw reload, or disable then enable); a rule added while IPV6=no exists for IPv4 only: add it again.
    .synopsis = ufw status [verbose|numbered] ; ufw enable|disable|reload ; ufw default allow|deny|reject [incoming|outgoing] ; ufw allow|deny|reject|limit RULE ; ufw delete RULE|NUMBER ; ufw app list
    .example-1 = the firewall's status, default policies and rules
    .example-2 = allow SSH (application profile)
    .example-3 = allow SSH only from one address
    .example-4 = deny everything incoming by default
    .example-5 = enable the firewall (after allowing SSH!)
    .example-6 = delete a rule
    .example-7 = let the club's network reach Samba (an application profile, from a network)
    .example-8 = does ufw also filter IPv6?
    .example-9 = reread /etc/default/ufw and the rules
man-ss = shows the machine's "sockets": the ports where a program waits for connections, and the established connections (socket statistics). -t TCP, -u UDP, -l only what is listening, -a everything, -n port numbers rather than their names, -p the program (you must be root to see other accounts' programs). An address 0.0.0.0 means "on all interfaces", so reachable from the network; 127.0.0.1, only from the machine itself. In IPv6, [::] means every IPv6 address (and [::1] the IPv6 loopback); a * address is an IPv6 socket that also takes IPv4 (IPV6_V6ONLY off). -4 and -6 keep one family.
    .synopsis = ss [-t] [-u] [-l] [-a] [-n] [-p] [-4|-6]
    .option-4 = IPv4 sockets only.
    .option-6 = IPv6 sockets only (including the * ones).
    .option-t = TCP sockets.
    .option-u = UDP sockets.
    .option-l = only listening sockets (listening).
    .option-a = all sockets, listening and established.
    .option-n = port numbers, without translating them into service names.
    .option-p = the program using each socket.
    .example-1 = who is listening, on which address and which port?
    .example-2 = established TCP connections
    .example-3 = listening TCP sockets, IPv6 only, with numbers

man-netstat = shows the machine's network sockets, like ss but with the older net-tools interface: netstat -tlnp lists the listening TCP ports with their program. -t TCP, -u UDP, -l only what is listening, -a everything, -n numbers rather than service names, -p the program (root only, for other accounts' programs). An address 0.0.0.0 means "on every interface", so reachable from the network; 127.0.0.1, only from the machine itself. Prefer ss on a modern system; netstat is what you still find on older ones.
    .synopsis = netstat [-t] [-u] [-l] [-a] [-n] [-p]
    .option-t = TCP sockets.
    .option-u = UDP sockets.
    .option-l = only listening sockets.
    .option-a = all sockets, listening and established.
    .option-n = port numbers, without translating them to service names.
    .option-p = the program using each socket (root only).
    .example-1 = the listening TCP ports and their programs
    .example-2 = the established TCP connections, with numbers
man-fail2ban-client = controls fail2ban, the guard that reads logs and bans addresses that fail to log in too often (brute force). A "jail" watches a service: sshd for SSH. Other jails watch other logs: nginx-limit-req reads the rejections from nginx's rate limiting, recidive reads fail2ban's own log and bans for a long time the addresses already banned several times. status gives the list of jails; status sshd, the failures, the log being read and the banned addresses; set sshd unbanip ADDRESS lifts a ban in one jail, unban ADDRESS in all of them; get sshd bantime (or findtime, maxretry, ignoreip, logpath) reads a loaded setting; -t checks the configuration; reload reloads it. Root only.
    .synopsis = fail2ban-client status [jail] ; fail2ban-client set jail banip|unbanip ADDRESS ; fail2ban-client get jail bantime|findtime|maxretry|ignoreip ; fail2ban-client -t ; fail2ban-client reload
    .example-1 = the active jails
    .example-2 = the failures and banned addresses of the sshd jail
    .example-3 = unban an address in the sshd jail
    .example-4 = unban an address in all jails
    .example-5 = the ban time loaded for the recidive jail
    .example-6 = check the configuration
    .example-7 = reload the configuration
man-jail_conf = the fail2ban configuration. Do not edit /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf, which an update would replace: write your settings in /etc/fail2ban/jail.local (or jail.d/*.local), read AFTERWARDS and which take precedence. Format: sections ([DEFAULT] for everything, [sshd] for the SSH jail) and "key = value" lines. enabled = true enables a jail; maxretry: number of failures before the ban; findtime: the window in which they are counted; bantime: the ban duration (600, 10m, 1h, 1d, 1w; -1 for ever). A jail is enabled in ITS OWN section (enabled = true in [recidive], for example): put in [DEFAULT], enabled would enable every jail in jail.conf, and fail2ban would refuse to start because it cannot find their logs. logpath: the log being read; banaction: what does the blocking (banaction_allports: all ports). ignoreip: the addresses or networks (CIDR, e.g. 192.0.2.0/24) never banned, separated by spaces; port: the ports closed to banned addresses (ssh, 2222, or a list: ssh,2222). A value written in a jail's section always wins over [DEFAULT], whatever the file: the [sshd] in jail.conf says "port = ssh", so a different port is set in an [sshd] section of jail.local.
    .synopsis = /etc/fail2ban/jail.local
    .example-1 = write your settings
    .example-2 = the value applied
    .example-3 = what the jails block, in the firewall

man-rsync = copies files and directories, copying only what has changed: the classic backup tool. -a (archive) copies a whole directory while keeping permissions; -v shows what is copied; -n (--dry-run) shows what WOULD be done, without touching anything; --delete removes from the destination what is no longer in the source (an exact mirror: be careful, it copies deletions too). Mind the trailing slash: "rsync -a memories/ dest/" copies the CONTENT of memories into dest, "rsync -a memories dest/" creates dest/memories. To or from another machine, write machine:path (rsync then goes through ssh, with the same keys).
    .synopsis = rsync [-a] [-v] [-n] [--delete] source... destination ; rsync -a source... [account@]machine:destination
    .option-a = archive mode: recursive, keeping permissions and dates.
    .option-r = recursive: also copies the contents of directories.
    .option-v = verbose: lists what is copied (verbose).
    .option-n = trial run: shows what would be done, without writing anything (dry run).
    .example-1 = back up the content of one directory into another
    .example-2 = try first, with --delete: nothing is touched
    .example-3 = restore a single file from the backup
    .example-4 = publish a site to another machine (over ssh)
    .command-1 = rsync -av ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/
    .command-2 = rsync -avn --delete ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/
    .command-3 = rsync -a /mnt/backup/memories/first-letter.txt ~/memories/
man-crontab = manages your scheduled tasks: commands that cron runs on its own, at set times. crontab -e opens your crontab in the editor; when you close it, it is checked and then installed. crontab -l displays it, crontab -r deletes it. One line = five time fields, then the command: minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month (1-31), month (1-12), day of week (0-7, 0 and 7 = Sunday). * means "every", 1-5 a range, 1,15 a list, */10 "every 10". Or a shortcut: @daily (every night at midnight), @hourly, @weekly, @reboot (at boot). Tasks run from your home directory; whatever they output is sent to you by mail. root can view and edit another account's crontab with -u: sudo crontab -u malo -l.
    .synopsis = crontab [-u account] -e | -l | -r | file
    .example-1 = edit your scheduled tasks
    .example-2 = display them
    .example-3 = a crontab line: every night at 3:15
    .example-4 = another account's crontab
    .command-3 = 15 3 * * * rsync -a ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/

## Manual layout and messages from man-db and coreutils

# Header of each page, between "LS(1)" and "LS(1)"
manual-header = Game Manual
manual-header-bsd = FreeBSD General Commands Manual
manual-header-solaris = User Commands (SunOS 5.10)
# IBM i's CL commands (WRKACTJOB…): their pages say (CL) and list parameters rather than options.
manual-header-cl = IBM i Control Language (CL)
manual-header-ps = Windows PowerShell Help (cmdlet)
# Windows' programs (icacls, net, sc.exe…): their pages say (CMD) and list /options.
manual-header-cmd = Windows Commands (cmd and programs)
manual-section-parameters = OPTIONS (PARAMETERS)
manual-section-name = NAME
manual-section-synopsis = SYNOPSIS
manual-section-description = DESCRIPTION
manual-section-options = OPTIONS
manual-section-examples = EXAMPLES
manual-section-see-also = SEE ALSO
# First line of "command --help"
manual-use = Usage: { $synopsis }
manual-complete = Full manual: man { $command }
manual-option-invalid =
    { $command }: invalid option -- '{ $letter }'
    Try '{ $command } --help' for more information.
# An unknown long option (--frobnicate), as getopt prints it in coreutils. { $option }: the option typed
manual-option-long-unknown =
    { $command }: unrecognized option '{ $option }'
    Try '{ $command } --help' for more information.

man-nmap = the network explorer: it sends packets to a machine (or a whole range of addresses) and says, for each TCP port, whether it is open (a program answers), closed (the machine answers "nobody here") or filtered (a firewall drops the packet: no answer at all). The ports are "seen from the internet": that is what an attacker, or an auditor, sees. -sV also asks the version of the service that answers (useful to spot outdated software); -p chooses the ports (-p 22,80; -p 1-1024; -p- for all of them); -sn only says which machines answer; -Pn ignores a missing answer to ping; -oN keeps the result in a file (evidence to attach to the report); -F looks only at the most common ports; -n does not look up names. Use nmap only on what is in your letter of engagement: probing a machine you have no right to probe is a fault, sometimes a crime. 127.0.0.1 is the machine itself, seen from the inside (the firewall does not apply there). -6 scans in IPv6 (a name's AAAA record, or an IPv6 address, which nmap only accepts with -6): a machine can be closed in IPv4 and open in IPv6.
    .synopsis = nmap [options] target...  (a name, an address, a network 192.0.2.0/29, a range 192.0.2.20-25)
    .option-6 = IPv6 scanning.
    .option-sV = the version of the service listening on each open port.
    .option-sn = only say which machines answer, without scanning the ports.
    .option-Pn = do not first test whether the machine answers (it may hide its answer to ping).
    .option-n = do not look up the names of machines.
    .option-F = only the most common ports (100) instead of the usual 1000.
    .option-p = the ports to examine: 22,80 or 1-1024; -p- for all 65535.
    .option-oN = write the result, as you read it, into the given file.
    .example-1 = the open ports of a machine
    .example-2 = with versions, keeping the trace in a file
    .example-3 = only SSH, http and https
    .example-4 = does this machine answer?
    .example-5 = all ports, not just the most common ones
    .example-6 = the same ports, seen in IPv6
man-lynis = the security audit tool for a server. "lynis audit system" examines the machine it runs on (with sudo: it reads what only root can see) and sorts its remarks into warnings (to fix first) and suggestions (leads, of uneven seriousness), each with its test identifier (SSH-7408, PKGS-7392…) and an address that explains it. At the end, the hardening index sums everything up out of 100: it is for comparing before and after, not for chasing 100 at any price. Lynis changes nothing: it is up to you to decide, suggestion by suggestion, what is worth the effort. The details of each audit are kept in /var/log/lynis-report.dat (hardening_index=, warning[]=, suggestion[]=), which you read with grep.
    .synopsis = lynis audit system ; lynis show version
    .example-1 = the full audit of the machine
    .example-2 = only the suggestions of the last audit
    .example-3 = the hardening index of the last audit
    .example-4 = lynis's version
man-uptime = how long the machine has been running, how many users are connected, and its load average: the number of processes that are running or waiting for a CPU, averaged over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. Compare it with the number of CPUs (nproc): a load of 2 on 2 CPUs means they are busy without a queue; 9 on 2 CPUs, that at any moment about seven processes wait their turn. The three figures tell the trend: rising if the first is the biggest. The load counts what wants to run, whatever its priority: renice changes who goes first, not the load.
    .synopsis = uptime [-p] [-s]
    .option-p = in words: "up 12 days, 2 hours, 11 minutes".
    .option-s = since when (the date and time of the boot).
    .example-1 = the load average
    .example-2 = the number of CPUs, to compare
    .example-3 = how long, in words
man-nproc = the number of processing units (CPUs, or vCPUs on a virtual machine) available: what the load average should be compared with.
    .synopsis = nproc [--all]
    .example-1 = how many CPUs
man-nice = runs a command with a nice value (the scheduling priority): from -20, served first, to 19, served last; nice -n 19 command is the usual way to start a long batch job without hurting the services. With no adjustment, +10; with no command, nice prints the current nice value. Only root may give a negative value. To change a process that is already running: renice.
    .synopsis = nice [-n ADJUSTMENT] [COMMAND [ARG]…]
    .example-1 = the current nice value
    .example-2 = an archive at the lowest priority
man-renice = changes the nice value of running processes: -p the given PIDs (default), -u all the processes of an account, -g a process group. The nice value goes from -20 (served first) to 19 (served last); the scheduler shares the CPU in proportion to a weight that drops by about 25 % per step. A nice value belongs to each process: renice on a PID doesn't reach its children already started (renice -u reaches them all if they belong to the same account). Without being root, you may only raise the nice value of your own processes; lowering it (more priority) is reserved for root. renice doesn't stop anything, and caps nothing: a niced process still takes all the CPU nobody else wants (for a cap: systemd's CPUQuota=).
    .synopsis = renice [-n] PRIORITY [-p|-u|-g] ID…
    .option-n = the nice value to set (from -20 to 19).
    .option-p = the following IDs are PIDs (default).
    .option-u = the following IDs are accounts (name or UID): all their processes.
    .option-g = the following IDs are process groups.
    .example-1 = lowest priority for process 2102
    .example-2 = lowest priority for all of jules's processes
    .example-3 = check: the NI column
man-pgrep = the PIDs of the processes whose name matches a pattern (a regular expression); -u only an account's; -l adds the name, -a the whole command line; -f searches the whole command line instead of the name. Returns 1 if nothing matches. Handy before kill or renice.
    .synopsis = pgrep [-l|-a] [-f] [-u ACCOUNT] [PATTERN]
    .option-u = only the processes of these accounts (comma-separated).
    .option-l = the name next to each PID.
    .option-a = the whole command line next to each PID.
    .option-f = match the pattern against the whole command line.
    .example-1 = all of jules's processes
    .example-2 = the processes named stack…, with their name
    .example-3 = the node processes, with their command line
man-systemd-cgtop = the control groups (cgroups) by resource use, like top but per service: each service is a group (/system.slice/NAME.service), with its tasks, its CPU (in percent of one CPU, all its processes together) and its memory. The real one refreshes every second until q; here, one snapshot.
    .synopsis = systemd-cgtop
    .example-1 = the services, the busiest first
man-systemd_resource-control = the resource-control settings of a unit ({"["}Service] section), applied by the kernel's control groups (cgroups) to all the service's processes together. CPUQuota= caps the CPU time: CPUQuota=80% is at most 0.8 CPU, even when the CPU is free (100 % = one CPU, 200 % = two). CPUWeight= (1 to 10000, 100 by default) shares the CPU between services when it is scarce, like nice between processes. MemoryMax= caps the memory (beyond it, the OOM killer). IOReadBandwidthMax=, IOWriteBandwidthMax= cap a disk's throughput. systemctl set-property applies one at once to a running service and keeps it (in /etc/systemd/system.control); in a drop-in written by hand, a daemon-reload applies it. Nice= (in systemd.exec) is not one of them: it applies when the processes start.
    .synopsis = {"["}Service] CPUQuota=PERCENT% CPUWeight=WEIGHT MemoryMax=BYTES
    .example-1 = cap a running service, at once and for good
    .example-2 = or write it in a drop-in
    .example-3 = check
man-ulimit = the limits of your shell, and of what you start from it (a bash builtin): -n the number of open files, -a all of them. A process has a soft limit (what it may use, shown by default; -S) and a hard one (the ceiling, -H): it may raise its soft limit up to its hard one by itself; only root may raise the hard one. With a value, ulimit changes the limit (both, without -S or -H). ulimit changes nothing for processes already running, nor for services: systemd sets a service's limits when it starts it (LimitNOFILE= in its unit), and login sessions get theirs from /etc/security/limits.conf.
    .synopsis = ulimit [-H|-S] [-n | -a] [LIMIT]
    .option-n = the number of open files.
    .option-H = the hard limit.
    .option-S = the soft limit.
    .option-a = all the limits.
    .example-1 = your shell's open-files limit (soft)
    .example-2 = its hard limit
    .example-3 = all the limits
man-prlimit = shows or changes the limits of a process that is running (--pid; without it, prlimit's own). --nofile alone shows the open-files limit; --nofile=SOFT:HARD changes it at once (SOFT: keeps the hard one). Another account's process, or a higher hard limit, needs root. The change only lasts until the process restarts: for a service, write it in its unit (LimitNOFILE=).
    .synopsis = prlimit [--pid PID] [--nofile[=SOFT:HARD]]
    .example-1 = all the limits of process 840
    .example-2 = only its open-files limit
    .example-3 = change it at once (until it restarts)
man-systemd_exec = the {"["}Service] settings of a unit about the execution environment of its processes: User=, Group=, WorkingDirectory=, Environment=, Nice= (the nice value), and the limits: LimitNOFILE= (open files: 4096, or SOFT:HARD; systemd's default is 1024:524288), LimitNPROC=, LimitCORE=… They are set when the processes start: after changing them, restart the service. Neither /etc/security/limits.conf nor ulimit apply to services.
    .synopsis = {"["}Service] User= Nice= LimitNOFILE=SOFT[:HARD]
    .example-1 = add LimitNOFILE= in a drop-in, then restart the service
    .example-2 = check the running process's limits
man-signal = the signals: a short message the kernel delivers to a process, with kill. Each has a number and a default action. HUP (1): end, often handled by daemons as "reread your configuration". INT (2): end, sent by Ctrl-C. QUIT (3): end with a core dump. KILL (9): end at once, can't be caught nor ignored. TERM (15): end, the polite request (kill's default), can be caught to clean up. STOP (19) and CONT (18): pause and resume (STOP can't be caught). CHLD (17): a child ended. A program (or a shell script with trap) can ignore or handle every signal except KILL and STOP. The children of a process that ends are not signalled: they are adopted by init.
    .synopsis = kill -SIGNAL PID ; trap 'commands' SIGNAL…
    .example-1 = the names and numbers
    .example-2 = the polite request
    .example-3 = the one that can't be refused
man-pg_lsclusters = lists the PostgreSQL clusters of a Debian machine (postgresql-common): version, name, port, status (online, down; online,recovery for a standby replaying a primary), owner, data directory and log file.
    .synopsis = pg_lsclusters
    .example-1 = the clusters and their state
man-pg_ctlcluster = controls a PostgreSQL cluster on Debian: start, stop, restart, reload (through systemd), status, and promote: a standby in recovery becomes a primary (it leaves recovery, takes a new timeline and accepts writes). Reserved for root and the postgres account.
    .synopsis = pg_ctlcluster VERSION CLUSTER start|stop|restart|reload|status|promote
    .example-1 = is the server running?
    .example-2 = turn the standby into the primary
man-pg_isready = checks whether a PostgreSQL server accepts connections: "accepting connections" (exit code 0), "rejecting connections" (1), "no response" (2). -h the host (the local socket by default), -p the port.
    .synopsis = pg_isready [-h host] [-p port]
    .option-h = the server's host.
    .option-p = its port (5432 by default).
    .example-1 = the local server
    .example-2 = another server

man-logger = writes a message to the system log, like a program would: rsyslog receives it and applies its rules (which file, which server), and so does the journal. The quickest way to test a logging rule: send a message that should match, then look for it where it should land. -t sets the tag (the program's name, $programname for rsyslog), -p the facility and severity (local0.warning, auth.info…; user.notice by default).
    .synopsis = logger [-t tag] [-p facility.severity] message…
    .option-t = the tag of the message (the program's name); your account's name by default.
    .option-p = facility and severity: auth.info, cron.err, local0.warning… (user.notice by default).
    .example-1 = a test message
    .example-2 = check a rule that catches sshd's failed logins
    .example-3 = a message with a facility and a severity
man-rsyslogd = the syslog daemon: it receives messages (from local programs through /dev/log, from the kernel, and from other machines over UDP or TCP if an input says so), then applies the rules of /etc/rsyslog.conf, in order, to write them in files, send them elsewhere or drop them. It reads its configuration when it starts: after a change, rsyslogd -N1 checks it without touching the running service (errors with the file and line), then systemctl restart rsyslog. Its own errors go to its journal (journalctl -u rsyslog).
    .synopsis = rsyslogd [-N level] [-f file] [-n]
    .option-N = only checks the configuration (level 1), then exits; status 1 if there are errors.
    .option-f = another configuration file than /etc/rsyslog.conf.
    .option-n = stays in the foreground (how systemd starts it).
    .example-1 = check the configuration before restarting
    .example-2 = apply the configuration
    .example-3 = rsyslog's own errors
man-rsyslog_conf = rsyslog's configuration: /etc/rsyslog.conf, which includes /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf (in alphabetical order, where $IncludeConfig stands: on Debian, before the default rules). Modules: module(load="imudp") then input(type="imudp" port="514") to receive syslog over UDP (imtcp for TCP). Rules: a selector "facility.severity  file" (auth.* /var/log/auth.log; *.* everything; auth.none excludes; a - before the file: no sync after each line), or a condition: if $programname == 'sshd' and $msg contains 'Failed password' then FILE. Properties: $msg, $programname, $hostname, $fromhost-ip, $syslogfacility-text, $syslogseverity-text; operators ==, !=, contains, startswith, and, or, not. Templates build a file name from the message: template(name="PerHost" type="string" string="/var/log/remote/%HOSTNAME%.log") then action(type="omfile" dynaFile="PerHost"). Several actions for one rule go between {"{"} and {"}"}. Every rule that matches is applied, in order: stop ends the message's journey (no file after it receives it).
    .synopsis = /etc/rsyslog.conf, /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf
    .example-1 = receive the logs of other machines over UDP
    .example-2 = a file name built from the sender's name
    .example-3 = one file per machine of a network, and nothing else for them
    .example-4 = only sshd's failed logins
    .example-5 = a classic selector: authentication messages
man-journald_conf = systemd-journald's configuration: /etc/systemd/journald.conf, and drop-ins in /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/*.conf (the last one wins). In the {"["}Journal] section, SystemMaxUse= limits the space of the persistent journal (/var/log/journal): 500M, 1G… By default, 10 % of the file system, up to 4G. When the limit is reached, journald deletes the oldest archived files. SystemKeepFree= keeps some space free, MaxRetentionSec= deletes what is older. journald reads this file when it starts: systemctl restart systemd-journald.
    .synopsis = /etc/systemd/journald.conf, /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/*.conf
    .example-1 = set SystemMaxUse=
    .example-2 = apply it
    .example-3 = check the space taken
man-exportfs = maintains the table of what the NFS server shares (exports), from /etc/exports and /etc/exports.d/*.exports. The server reads these files when it starts; after a change, exportfs -ra reloads the table (-r: re-export everything, -a: all of it), and warns about mistakes (a folder that doesn't exist, an unknown option). Without options, lists what is shared and with whom; -v adds every option in force (including the defaults: ro, root_squash…); -s shows what the files say. -u client:/folder stops sharing one.
    .synopsis = exportfs [-v] ; exportfs -ra[v] ; exportfs -s ; exportfs -u client:/folder
    .option-r = re-exports everything: the table becomes what the files say.
    .option-a = all the exports (with -r: exportfs -ra).
    .option-v = verbose: every option in force, and what is exported.
    .option-s = shows the exports as the files describe them.
    .option-u = stops exporting a folder to a client (unexport).
    .example-1 = what the server shares, with every option
    .example-2 = reload /etc/exports after a change
    .example-3 = what the files say
man-exports = the list of what an NFS server shares: /etc/exports (and /etc/exports.d/*.exports). One line per folder, then the clients, each with its options in parentheses, WITHOUT a space before them: "/srv/photos 192.0.2.32(rw)" gives rw to that client, but "/srv/photos 192.0.2.32 (rw)" gives rw to everyone! A client is an address, a network (192.0.2.0/24), a name, or * (everyone). Options: rw or ro (the default: read-only), sync (writes reach the disk before the answer), no_subtree_check, root_squash (the default: root on the client becomes nobody, 65534, on the server), no_root_squash (root stays root: dangerous), all_squash (everyone becomes nobody; anonuid= and anongid= choose who). With sec=sys (the default), the server trusts the client's numbers (uid, gid): the same person needs the same uid everywhere. After a change: exportfs -ra.
    .synopsis = /etc/exports, /etc/exports.d/*.exports
    .example-1 = one client, read-write
    .example-2 = a whole network, read-only
    .example-3 = a backup server whose root must stay root (only if you trust it completely)
man-showmount = asks an NFS server what it shares (-e, the export list), or which clients have something mounted (without options). It goes through rpcbind (port 111): a firewall that only lets NFS (2049) through makes it time out, even when mount works.
    .synopsis = showmount [-e] [server]
    .option-e = the server's export list.
    .example-1 = what the NAS shares, seen from a client
    .example-2 = what this machine shares
    .example-3 = the clients that mounted something from here
man-nfs = mounting an NFS share (Network File System) on a client: mount -t nfs server:/folder /mount_point (mount.nfs; type nfs or nfs4). The server's folder then appears in the client's tree, and what one writes, the other sees. The server decides every access, with the numbers the client sends (uid and gids): a uid that has no account on the client shows as a number (ls -l), and root may become nobody (root_squash). In /etc/fstab: server:/folder /point nfs defaults,_netdev 0 0 (_netdev: wait for the network at boot). Errors: "Connection timed out" (a firewall drops port 2049), "access denied by server" (not in the server's exports), "mount point … does not exist".
    .synopsis = mount -t nfs server:/folder /point [-o options] ; in /etc/fstab: server:/folder /point nfs options 0 0
    .example-1 = mount the NAS's photos
    .example-2 = the same, at every boot (a line of /etc/fstab)
    .example-3 = the NFS mounts and their options
    .example-4 = the owners as numbers (what the server really sees)
man-testparm = checks Samba's configuration (/etc/samba/smb.conf): unknown parameters (ignored by Samba), sections without a path, then prints the configuration as Samba understands it (the "dump"). -s doesn't wait for Enter before the dump. Run it after every change; smbd reads smb.conf for each new connection.
    .synopsis = testparm [-s] [file]
    .option-s = prints the dump without waiting for Enter (suppress prompt).
    .example-1 = check smb.conf and see what Samba understands
    .example-2 = the same, with the pause before the dump
man-smbpasswd = manages Samba's passwords, which are not the Unix ones: an account (which must exist on the machine) needs one to connect to a share. -a adds a user (or changes their password), -x deletes them, -d disables them, -e enables them again. Root only.
    .synopsis = smbpasswd -a|-x|-d|-e user
    .option-a = adds the user to Samba (asks for the password twice).
    .option-x = deletes the user from Samba.
    .option-d = disables the user (they can no longer connect).
    .option-e = enables the user again.
    .example-1 = give Sam a Samba password
    .example-2 = delete Tom from Samba
    .example-3 = disable Tom's account, without deleting it
man-pdbedit = manages Samba's user database (passdb). -L lists the users who have a Samba password (name:uid:full name). Root only.
    .synopsis = pdbedit -L
    .option-L = lists the users.
    .example-1 = who has a Samba password
man-smbclient = a client for SMB shares (Samba, Windows), like an FTP client: //server/share, -U user (asks for the Samba password; -U user%password gives it), -N without a password (guest). -L server lists the shares. -c runs commands: ls, cd folder, put local_file [name], get name [local_file], mkdir, rm. Errors: NT_STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE (no Samba password, or a wrong one), NT_STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED (the share or the Linux permissions refuse), NT_STATUS_BAD_NETWORK_NAME (no such share).
    .synopsis = smbclient -L server [-N|-U user] ; smbclient //server/share -U user -c "commands"
    .option-L = lists the server's shares.
    .option-U = the user (user%password to give the password).
    .option-N = no password (guest).
    .option-c = commands to run, separated by ";".
    .example-1 = the shares of this machine
    .example-2 = what's in the photos share, as Sam
    .example-3 = add a photo, then list
    .example-4 = fetch a file
man-smb_conf = Samba's configuration: /etc/samba/smb.conf. [global] for the server (workgroup, map to guest…), then one section per share: [photos] is \\server\photos. path = the folder shared; read only = no (or writable = yes) to write, read-only by default; valid users = @group or names (the others can't enter); guest ok = yes lets people in without a password (avoid); create mask and directory mask: the most a new file or folder may have (0744 and 0755 by default: only the author can write); force group = group: what is created belongs to that group. Names are case-insensitive and spaces don't count (valid users = validusers). Check with testparm.
    .synopsis = /etc/samba/smb.conf
    .example-1 = a share named photos
    .example-2 = the folder it serves
    .example-3 = writable
    .example-4 = for the club group only
    .example-5 = files group-writable
    .example-6 = what is created belongs to the club

## FreeBSD (the story "The Other Unix"): its own tools, and the BSD versions of date, sed and stat

man-sed = the stream editor: reads text line by line and applies a small script to each line. Its most used command, s/pattern/replacement/, substitutes the first match on each line (with g: every match; & in the replacement is what matched, \1 a group). d deletes the line, p prints it (with -n, only what p prints comes out). An address in front chooses the lines: a number (2p), $ (the last), /regex/ (/^#/d deletes the comments). -E for extended regular expressions. -i edits the file in place (GNU: -i alone, or -i.bak to keep a copy; BSD's sed requires the suffix: -i '').
    .synopsis = sed [-n] [-E] [-i[SUFFIX]] script [file...] ; sed -e script [-e script]... [file...]
    .option-n = print only what p prints.
    .option-E = extended regular expressions.
    .option-i = edit the files in place (-i.bak: keep a copy with that suffix).
    .option-e = add a script (several -e follow each other).
    .example-1 = replace http: with https: on each line (shown, the file is unchanged)
    .example-2 = change the line in the file itself (GNU)
    .example-3 = print only line 2
    .example-4 = delete the comment lines
man-sed-bsd = the stream editor (BSD's): reads text line by line and applies a script to each line. s/pattern/replacement/ substitutes (g: every match), d deletes the line, p prints it (with -n, only what p prints), an address chooses the lines (a number, $, /regex/). -E for extended regular expressions. -i EXTENSION edits the file in place and keeps a copy with that extension: the extension is REQUIRED, as the next word; give an empty one, -i '', for no copy. Careful with a script written for GNU: sed -i 's/a/b/' file takes 's/a/b/' as the extension, and file as the script ("invalid command code").
    .synopsis = sed [-Ealnru] [-i extension] script [file ...] ; sed [-Ealnu] [-i extension] [-e script] ... [file ...]
    .options = E i n
    .option-E = extended regular expressions.
    .option-i = edit the files in place; the next word is the backup copy's extension ('' for none).
    .option-n = print only what p prints.
    .command-1 = sed -i '' 's/^last=.*/last=2029-10-05/' state.conf
    .example-1 = change the line in the file itself, without a copy
    .command-2 = sed -i .bak 's/80/8080/' nginx.conf
    .example-2 = change it, and keep the old version in nginx.conf.bak
    .command-3 = sed -n 2p notes.txt
    .example-3 = print only line 2
man-stat = shows a file's details: size, rights, owner, dates. -c FORMAT prints only what you ask: %s the size in bytes, %n the name, %U the owner, %G the group, %a the rights in octal (644), %A in letters, %y the date of the last change, %Y in seconds since 1970. GNU's stat (Debian); BSD's stat, on FreeBSD, takes its format with -f and other letters (%z for the size).
    .synopsis = stat [-c FORMAT] file...
    .option-c = print only FORMAT (%s size, %n name, %U owner, %a rights…).
    .example-1 = all the details of the archive
    .example-2 = its size in bytes, alone (handy in a script)
    .example-3 = owner, rights in octal and name
man-stat-bsd = shows a file's details (BSD's stat). -f FORMAT prints only what you ask: %z the size in bytes, %N the name, %Su the owner, %Sg the group, %Lp the rights in octal, %Sp in letters, %Sm the date of the last change, %m in seconds since 1970. There is no -c (that's GNU's stat, on Linux).
    .synopsis = stat [-FLnq] [-f format | -l | -r | -s | -x] [-t timefmt] [file ...]
    .options = f l
    .option-f = print only FORMAT (%z size, %N name, %Su owner, %Lp rights…).
    .option-l = like ls -l.
    .command-1 = stat -f %z backup.tar.gz
    .example-1 = its size in bytes, alone (handy in a script)
    .command-2 = stat -f '%Su %Lp %N' /etc/master.passwd
    .example-2 = owner, rights in octal and name
man-date-bsd = prints the date and time (BSD's date). With +FORMAT, your way: %Y the year, %m the month, %d the day, %H:%M:%S the time, %F for 2029-10-05. -v adjusts the date before printing it: -v-1d is yesterday, -v+2w in two weeks, -v-1m a month ago (y years, m months, w weeks, d days, H hours, M minutes, S seconds); several -v add up. Without a sign, -v sets the field (-v1d: the first of the month). There is no -d (that's GNU's date, on Linux).
    .synopsis = date [-jnRu] [-v[+|-]val[y|m|w|d|H|M|S]] [+output_fmt]
    .options = j u v
    .option-j = don't try to set the clock (with a date given).
    .option-u = in UTC.
    .option-v = adjust the date (-v-1d: yesterday).
    .command-1 = date -v-1d +%F
    .example-1 = yesterday's date, in ISO format
    .command-2 = date -v-1m -v1d +%F
    .example-2 = the first day of last month
    .command-3 = date +%F
    .example-3 = today's date, in ISO format
man-service = drives FreeBSD's services: their rc scripts, in /etc/rc.d (base system) and /usr/local/etc/rc.d (packages). service NAME start, stop, restart, reload, status. A service only runs if /etc/rc.conf switches it on (NAME_enable="YES"): otherwise start refuses ("Cannot 'start' …"). onestart runs it this once, whatever rc.conf says (it won't come back after a reboot). enable and disable write or remove the rc.conf line. service -e lists what starts at boot. Root to change anything.
    .synopsis = service name start|stop|restart|reload|status|onestart|enable|disable ; service -e
    .example-1 = is nginx running?
    .example-2 = start it (it must be enabled in rc.conf)
    .example-3 = enable it at boot (writes nginx_enable="YES" in rc.conf)
    .example-4 = start it once, without enabling it
    .example-5 = the services started at boot
man-sysrc = reads and writes /etc/rc.conf safely, without opening an editor. sysrc NAME prints a variable; sysrc NAME=VALUE sets it (and says old -> new); -x removes it; -a lists them all. Root to change anything.
    .synopsis = sysrc [-x] name[=value] ... ; sysrc -a
    .example-1 = is nginx enabled at boot?
    .example-2 = enable it
    .example-3 = remove the line
    .example-4 = every variable set
man-pkg = FreeBSD's package manager (binary packages, what apt is on Debian). pkg search finds a package, pkg install installs it with what it needs (asks for confirmation; -y to skip it), pkg info lists what is installed (pkg info NAME: details), pkg delete removes it. Packages go under /usr/local: their programs in /usr/local/bin and /usr/local/sbin, their configuration in /usr/local/etc, their rc script in /usr/local/etc/rc.d. Some print a message after installing: read it. Root to install or remove.
    .synopsis = pkg search pattern ; pkg install [-y] name... ; pkg info [name] ; pkg delete name
    .example-1 = find nginx
    .example-2 = install it
    .example-3 = install bash, without the question
    .example-4 = what is installed
    .example-5 = details on nginx
    .example-6 = remove it
man-sockstat = lists the open sockets, with who opened them: the account, the program, its PID, the protocol and the addresses. -l the listening ones, -4 and -6 IPv4 or IPv6 only. FreeBSD's answer to ss -tlnp (or netstat -tlnp) on Linux.
    .synopsis = sockstat [-46l]
    .example-1 = what listens, in IPv4
    .example-2 = every listening socket
    .example-3 = listening, IPv4 and IPv6
man-pfctl = drives pf, the packet filter (FreeBSD's firewall, from OpenBSD). -nf FILE checks a configuration without loading it; -f FILE loads its rules; -e enables pf, -d disables it; -sr shows the rules in force (as pf understands them); -si its status. The rules are in /etc/pf.conf (man pf.conf). Careful when enabling it over ssh: rules that block port 22 cut the next connection. For pf to start at boot: pf_enable="YES" in rc.conf (then service pf start). Root only.
    .synopsis = pfctl [-n] -f file ; pfctl -e | -d ; pfctl -s rules | -s info
    .example-1 = check pf.conf without loading it
    .example-2 = load the rules
    .example-3 = enable pf
    .example-4 = the rules in force
    .example-5 = pf's status
    .example-6 = disable pf
man-pf_conf = pf's configuration (/etc/pf.conf), read by pfctl. One rule per line: pass or block, in or out, quick, proto tcp, from SOURCE, to DESTINATION, port N (or a list {"{"} 22 80 {"}"}); "all" for any address. MACRO = "value" defines a name used as $MACRO. Essential: pf reads EVERY rule, and the LAST one that matches a packet decides — the opposite of iptables, where the first one wins. So "block in all" comes FIRST, then the passes; or a rule marked quick decides at once. set skip on lo0 leaves the loopback alone.
    .synopsis = /etc/pf.conf
    .example-1 = block everything coming in (first: the rules below will open what's needed)
    .example-2 = then let ssh and the web in
    .example-3 = decide at once for this address (quick)
    .example-4 = let out what the server opens
man-rc_conf = FreeBSD's system configuration (/etc/rc.conf): shell variables read at boot. The host name, the network, and which services start: NAME_enable="YES" (nginx_enable, sshd_enable, pf_enable…). Without it, the service doesn't start at boot, and service NAME start refuses. Edited with sysrc (or an editor); the packages' defaults stay in their rc scripts.
    .synopsis = /etc/rc.conf
    .example-1 = start nginx at boot
    .example-2 = enable pf at boot
    .example-3 = start sshd at boot
man-freebsd-version = prints the version of FreeBSD installed (the userland, 14.2-RELEASE…), where uname -r gives the running kernel's.
    .synopsis = freebsd-version [-kru]
    .example-1 = the installed version
    .example-2 = the system and its kernel
man-ps-bsd = lists the processes (BSD's ps). Without options, only YOUR processes attached to a terminal. a adds those of the other accounts, x those without a terminal (the daemons): ps aux (or ps -ax) shows them all; u gives the user-oriented columns (USER, %CPU, %MEM, STARTED…), j the parents (PPID). -U account keeps one account's, -p PID one process; -o chooses the columns (pid,ppid,user,%cpu,command…). Careful: -e shows each process's ENVIRONMENT (its variables), not every process: ps -ef, a Linux habit, lists only your own processes, with their variables. No long options (--sort…).
    .synopsis = ps [-aCcdefHhjlmrSTuvwXxZ] [-O fmt | -o fmt] [-p pid[,pid...]] [-U user[,user...]]
    .options = a x u j e U p o
    .option-a = the processes of every account (with a terminal).
    .option-x = also those without a terminal (the daemons).
    .option-u = user-oriented columns: USER, %CPU, %MEM, VSZ, RSS, STARTED…
    .option-j = the job columns, with the parent (PPID).
    .option-e = show each process's environment (its variables).
    .option-U = only this account's processes.
    .option-p = only these processes.
    .option-o = the columns to show (pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,command…).
    .command-1 = ps aux
    .example-1 = every process, with its account and its use of the machine
    .command-2 = ps -axj
    .example-2 = every process with its parent (PPID): the BSD answer to ps -ef
    .command-3 = ps -U www
    .example-3 = the www account's processes
    .command-4 = ps -ax -o pid,ppid,user,command
    .example-4 = chosen columns
man-ls-bsd = lists the contents of a place (BSD's ls). -a shows the hidden files (and . and ..), -l the long format, -h readable sizes with -l, -d the place itself, -i the inode numbers, -t sorted by date, -r in reverse, -G in colour (or --color=auto). No other long option: --all, --human-readable… are GNU's (unknown option).
    .synopsis = ls [-ABCFGHILPRSTUWZabcdfghiklmnopqrstuvwxy1,] [--color=when] [-D format] [file ...]
    .options = a l h d G t r
    .option-a = everything, hidden files included.
    .option-l = long format.
    .option-h = with -l, readable sizes.
    .option-d = the place itself, not its contents.
    .option-G = in colour (like CLICOLOR=1).
    .option-t = sorted by modification date, newest first.
    .option-r = in reverse order.
    .command-1 = ls -la
    .example-1 = everything, in long format
    .command-2 = ls -lG /usr/local/etc
    .example-2 = in colour
man-grep-bsd = prints the lines that contain the pattern (FreeBSD's grep). Same options as GNU's for the usual cases: -E extended expressions, -F the text as is, -i ignore case, -v invert, -c count, -q quiet, -r in a whole tree, -n line numbers, --color. No -P (Perl expressions): use -E, where [0-9] stands for \d.
    .synopsis = grep [-abcdDEFGHhIiLlmnOopqRSsUVvwxz] [-A num] [-B num] [-C num] [-e pattern] [pattern] [file ...]
    .options = E F i v c q r n
    .option-E = extended regular expressions.
    .option-F = the pattern as is.
    .option-i = ignore case.
    .option-v = the lines that do NOT match.
    .option-c = count the lines.
    .option-q = quiet: only the exit code.
    .option-r = search a whole tree.
    .option-n = the line numbers.
    .command-1 = grep -E '[0-9]+ bytes' /var/log/messages
    .example-1 = what grep -P '\d+ bytes' would find on Linux
    .command-2 = grep -rn nginx_enable /etc
    .example-2 = where is nginx enabled, with the line numbers
man-find-bsd = searches for files in a place and all its sub-places (BSD's find). -name, -type f or d, -mtime ±N, -maxdepth N, -delete, -print, and -ls for details. No -printf (GNU's): to print each file your way, combine find with stat: find . -type f -exec stat -f '%z %N' {"{"}{"}"} +
    .synopsis = find [-H | -L | -P] [-EXdsx] path ... [expression]
    .command-1 = find /var/log -name '*.log' -mtime +30
    .example-1 = logs older than 30 days
    .command-2 = find /usr/local/etc -maxdepth 1 -type d
    .example-2 = the packages' configuration places
man-du-bsd = measures the space used, place by place (BSD's du): in blocks of BLOCKSIZE (1K by default here). -s a single total, -h readable sizes, -d N stops N levels deep, -A the apparent size instead of the blocks really used. No -b (GNU's bytes): BLOCKSIZE=1 with -A, or stat -f %z for one file.
    .synopsis = du [-Aclnx] [-H | -L | -P] [-g | -h | -k | -m] [-a | -s | -d depth] [file ...]
    .options = s h d A
    .option-s = a single total per place.
    .option-h = readable sizes: K, M, G.
    .option-d = only N levels deep.
    .option-A = the apparent size, not the blocks used.
    .command-1 = du -sh /usr/local/www/club
    .example-1 = the site's total size
    .command-2 = du -d 1 -h /var
    .example-2 = what takes room in /var, one level deep

## Solaris (the story "The Old Sun"): SMF, ZFS, zones, packages, and the old /usr/bin tools

man-svcs = lists the services managed by SMF, Solaris's Service Management Facility. Each service is named by an FMRI (svc:/application/database/postgresql_84:default_64bit) and is in a state: online (running), disabled, maintenance (it failed: svcs -x says why), legacy_run (an old rc script). Without options, the services that aren't disabled; -a all of them. -x explains what doesn't run although it should; -l gives a service's details (its log, its contract); -p its processes. A pattern can be the end of an FMRI (postgresql_84, seed-catalogue).
    .synopsis = svcs [-aHpv] [service ...] ; svcs -l service ... ; svcs -x [service ...]
    .example-1 = the services that aren't disabled
    .example-2 = all of them, disabled included
    .example-3 = what is broken, and why
    .example-4 = the details of the database service
    .example-5 = the catalogue and its processes
man-svcadm = drives SMF services: enable starts a service and keeps it started at every boot, disable stops it (and keeps it stopped), restart restarts it, refresh rereads its configuration, clear takes it out of maintenance once fixed. -t: only until the next boot (temporary). A pattern names the service (the end of its FMRI). Root only (or the matching RBAC authorisation).
    .synopsis = svcadm enable [-t] service ... ; svcadm disable [-t] service ... ; svcadm restart | refresh | clear service ...
    .example-1 = stop the catalogue (and keep it stopped)
    .example-2 = start it again
    .example-3 = restart it (it rereads its configuration file)
    .example-4 = stop the database until the next boot only
    .example-5 = take sendmail out of maintenance, once fixed
man-svcprop = shows the properties of an SMF service: what its manifest says (the data directory of a database, the command that starts it…). -p group/name gives one property's value.
    .synopsis = svcprop [-p group/name] service
    .example-1 = where PostgreSQL keeps its data
    .example-2 = all of the catalogue's properties
man-prstat = shows the processes and what they use, refreshed regularly: Solaris's top. PID, account, memory (SIZE, RSS), state, priority, CPU time and share, and the program. -a adds a summary per account.
    .synopsis = prstat [-a] [interval [count]]
    .example-1 = the busiest processes
    .example-2 = with a summary per account
man-zfs = manages ZFS datasets (file systems in a pool) and their snapshots. zfs list lists them (-t snapshot the snapshots); zfs snapshot pool/dataset@name freezes a dataset in an instant, at almost no cost (only what changes afterwards takes room); zfs rollback brings it back; zfs destroy removes a snapshot; zfs get shows a property (mountpoint…). Root to change anything.
    .synopsis = zfs list [-t filesystem|snapshot|all] [dataset] ; zfs snapshot dataset@name ; zfs destroy dataset@name ; zfs get property dataset
    .example-1 = the datasets and where they are mounted
    .example-2 = the snapshots
    .example-3 = take a snapshot before a risky change
    .example-4 = where a dataset is mounted
    .example-5 = remove a snapshot that's no longer needed
man-zpool = manages ZFS pools: the disks (often mirrored) on which datasets live. zpool list gives their size and health; zpool status their disks, errors, and the last check (scrub).
    .synopsis = zpool list ; zpool status [pool]
    .example-1 = the pools, their size and health
    .example-2 = their disks and errors
man-zoneadm = manages Solaris zones: isolated systems sharing one kernel (the ancestors of containers). The global zone is the machine itself. zoneadm list -cv lists them all, with their state and path.
    .synopsis = zoneadm list [-cipv]
    .example-1 = every zone, with its state
man-zonename = prints the name of the zone you are in: global for the machine itself.
    .synopsis = zonename
    .example-1 = which zone am I in?
man-pkginfo = lists the SVR4 packages installed on Solaris 10: category, name (SUNW… for Sun's) and description. -l gives a package's details (version, architecture, state).
    .synopsis = pkginfo [-l] [package ...]
    .example-1 = every installed package
    .example-2 = those about PostgreSQL
    .example-3 = the details of one
man-isainfo = describes the processor's instruction sets: sparcv9 sparc on a 64-bit SPARC, amd64 i386 on a PC. -v the details, -b the number of bits of the native applications.
    .synopsis = isainfo [-bkv]
    .example-1 = the instruction sets
    .example-2 = in detail
    .example-3 = 64 or 32 bits?
man-iconv = converts a text from one character set to another: -f the one it is in (from), -t the one to write (to), for example LATIN1 (ISO 8859-1, one byte per accented letter), UTF-8, ASCII, or a mainframe's EBCDIC (IBM037, also called CP037; EBCDIC-US leaves some bytes undefined). The result goes to the screen: redirect it to a new file. Converting a text already in UTF-8 "from LATIN1" damages it (é becomes Ã©). iconv converts characters: binary fields (packed numbers, COMP-3) in the middle of a file aren't characters, and a conversion damages them or stops on them ("illegal input sequence at position N"). -l lists the character sets.
    .synopsis = iconv [-c] -f from -t to [-o output] [file]
    .option-f = the character set of the input.
    .option-t = the character set to write.
    .option-c = drop the characters that can't be converted (instead of stopping).
    .option-o = write to this file rather than to the screen.
    .option-l = list the known character sets.
    .example-1 = convert a LATIN1 dump to UTF-8, into a new file
    .example-2 = the known character sets
    .example-3 = an EBCDIC text file (no binary fields) into UTF-8
man-od = shows a file's bytes (octal dump): the offset of each line, then its bytes in the chosen form. -c as characters (\n, \0, or the octal value of a byte that is no ASCII character), -t x1 in hexadecimal, -b in octal. -A says how to write the offsets (d decimal, x hexadecimal, o octal, n none); -j skips bytes, -N reads only so many. A line equal to the previous one shows as *.
    .synopsis = od [-A RADIX] [-t TYPE] [-c] [-j BYTES] [-N BYTES] [file]
    .option-A = how to write the offsets: d, x, o or n (none).
    .option-t = the type of each value: c (character), x1 (a byte in hexadecimal), o1, u1, d1, or x2, x4… for 2 or 4 bytes.
    .option-c = the bytes as characters (like -t c).
    .option-b = the bytes in octal (like -t o1).
    .option-x = 2-byte values in hexadecimal (like -t x2).
    .option-N = read only this many bytes.
    .option-j = skip this many bytes first.
    .option-v = show every line, even those equal to the previous one.
    .example-1 = the file's first bytes, as characters
    .example-2 = every byte in hexadecimal, offsets in decimal
    .example-3 = only the 5 bytes of the first record's amount (after 33 bytes)
man-xxd = shows a file's bytes in hexadecimal, 16 per line, with the offset on the left and the printable characters on the right (a dot for the others). -c sets how many bytes per line (40, the length of a record, puts one record per line), -s where to start, -l how many to read, -p plain hexadecimal only.
    .synopsis = xxd [-c COLS] [-g BYTES] [-s OFFSET] [-l LENGTH] [-p] [-u] [file]
    .option-s = start at this offset.
    .option-l = stop after this many bytes.
    .option-c = bytes per line (16 by default).
    .option-g = bytes per group (2 by default).
    .option-p = plain hexadecimal, without offsets nor characters.
    .option-u = capital letters for the hexadecimal digits.
    .example-1 = the first lines of the file
    .example-2 = one 40-byte record per line
    .example-3 = only the second record
man-hexdump = shows a file's bytes. -C is the canonical form: the offset, 16 bytes in hexadecimal, then the printable characters between | |. A line equal to the previous one shows as *.
    .synopsis = hexdump [-C] [-n LENGTH] [-s OFFSET] [-v] file...
    .option-C = canonical form: hexadecimal and characters.
    .option-n = read only this many bytes.
    .option-s = skip this many bytes first.
    .option-v = show every line, even those equal to the previous one.
    .example-1 = the file's first bytes, in hexadecimal and as characters
    .example-2 = only the first two records (80 bytes)
man-dd = copies a file by blocks, and can convert it on the way. It takes operands rather than options: if=FILE to read (the pipe otherwise), of=FILE to write (the screen otherwise), bs=SIZE the size of a block, count=N how many blocks, skip=N how many blocks to skip first. conv=ascii translates EBCDIC to ASCII byte by byte (with cbs=SIZE, each record of that size becomes a line, trailing spaces removed), conv=ebcdic the other way; conv=ucase, lcase change the case. At the end, dd says how many blocks went in and out (full+partial) and how many bytes it copied; status=none says nothing. Its translation table changes every byte, binary ones included: never on a file with packed numbers. if=/dev/urandom reads random bytes (with count).
    .synopsis = dd [if=FILE] [of=FILE] [bs=SIZE] [count=N] [skip=N] [cbs=SIZE] [conv=CONVERSIONS] [status=none]
    .example-1 = the first 40-byte record, in hexadecimal
    .example-2 = copy only the 14th record (the trailer)
    .example-3 = an EBCDIC text file of 20-character records into text lines
    .example-4 = count the records: "14+0 records in" for 560 bytes
    .example-5 = a key file of 4096 random bytes (/dev/urandom never ends: count is required)
man-cobc = compiles COBOL (GnuCOBOL). By default, the source is in fixed (reference) format: columns 1 to 6 for a sequence number, column 7 for the indicator (* a comment, - a continuation, a space for code), the program text from column 8 to column 72; anything else in column 7 is an "invalid indicator". COPY brings in a copybook, looked for in the current directory and the -I directories (with .cpy, .cbl, .cob or no extension). -x builds an executable; without it, a module (NAME.so) that cobcrun or another program loads. A program's RETURN-CODE becomes its exit code ($?).
    .synopsis = cobc [-x | -m | -c] [-free | -fixed] [-I DIRECTORY] [-o FILE] source.cob
    .option-x = build an executable program.
    .option-m = build a module (NAME.so), the default.
    .option-c = compile only, into an object file (NAME.o).
    .option-o = the name of the file to build.
    .option-I = also look for copybooks (COPY) in this directory.
    .option-free = free format: no columns (*> for a comment).
    .option-fixed = fixed (reference) format, the default.
    .example-1 = build the batch, its copybooks in /opt/batch/copybooks
    .example-2 = build it straight where the batch looks for it
    .example-3 = a program written in free format
    .example-4 = the compiler's version
man-file = says what a file contains, by looking at its bytes rather than its name: ASCII text, UTF-8 text, ISO-8859 text (LATIN1), a tar archive, data…
    .synopsis = file file ...
    .example-1 = which character set is this dump in?
    .example-2 = what is this backup?
man-pg_ctl = starts, stops or checks a PostgreSQL server on a data directory, by hand (-D the directory). On Debian it lives in /usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin, outside the PATH, and the clusters are normally driven by pg_ctlcluster or systemctl. A data directory only works with the major version, and the processor architecture, that wrote it: to move a database elsewhere, use pg_dump.
    .synopsis = pg_ctl -D directory start | stop | status
    .example-1 = try to start a server on a copied data directory
    .example-2 = is the server of this cluster running?
man-grep-solaris = prints the lines that contain the pattern (Solaris 10's /usr/bin/grep, older than POSIX). Only -b, -c, -h, -i, -l, -n, -s, -v, -w: no -q, no -E, no -r, no -e. The POSIX grep, with -q, -E, -F, is /usr/xpg4/bin/grep; scripts call it by its full path.
    .synopsis = grep [-bchilnsvw] pattern [file ...] ; /usr/xpg4/bin/grep [-E|-F] [-q] [-c|-l] pattern [file ...]
    .options = i v c l n w
    .option-i = ignore case.
    .option-v = the lines that do NOT match.
    .option-c = count the lines.
    .option-l = only the names of the files that match.
    .option-n = the line numbers.
    .option-w = whole words only.
    .command-1 = svcs -a | grep postgres
    .example-1 = the database's services
    .command-2 = svcs | /usr/xpg4/bin/grep -q catalogue
    .example-2 = quiet, for a script (POSIX grep)
man-ps-solaris = lists the processes (Solaris's /usr/bin/ps, System V's). -e every process, -f the full format (UID, PID, PPID, start time, command), -u account, -p PID, -o the columns. ps -ef works as on Linux; ps aux, BSD's, doesn't: it is /usr/ucb/ps aux.
    .synopsis = ps [-aAdeflcjLPyZ] [-o format] [-u userlist] [-p proclist]
    .options = e f u p o
    .option-e = every process.
    .option-f = the full format, with the parent (PPID).
    .option-u = only these accounts' processes.
    .option-p = only these processes.
    .option-o = the columns to show (pid,ppid,user,args…).
    .command-1 = ps -ef
    .example-1 = every process, full format
    .command-2 = ps -u postgres
    .example-2 = the database's processes
    .command-3 = /usr/ucb/ps aux
    .example-3 = the BSD format, from /usr/ucb

## IBM i (The Order Book): the 5250 client, PASE's system and db2, the CL commands

man-tn5250 = opens a 5250 session (the IBM i "green screen") on a machine, over telnet: the sign-on display (user and password), then the main menu and its command line, "===>", where CL commands are typed (WRKACTJOB, DSPMSG QSYSOPR…). SIGNOFF (or option 90) ends the session. In the game, the function keys are replaced by commands: CMD ? shows a command's help, and at a message's reply prompt, ? shows its second-level text (F1).
    .synopsis = tn5250 HOST
    .example-1 = the mill's IBM i
    .example-2 = the same, by its full name
man-system = runs a CL command from PASE (IBM i's Unix-like shell): its messages are printed with their ID (CPF2105: …), its printed output (spooled) on the standard output. The exit code is 0 if the command succeeded. Quote the command: CL uses parentheses and asterisks that the shell would read. -v prints the command before running it.
    .synopsis = system [-v] "CL command"
    .example-1 = the active jobs, from PASE
    .example-2 = the operator's messages (to answer an inquiry, use a 5250 session)
    .example-3 = a library's objects, echoing the command
man-db2 = runs an SQL statement on DB2 for i from PASE. A table is a physical file of a library: ORDERS.ORDHDR in SQL is ORDERS/ORDHDR in CL. In the game, SELECT only: columns or *, COUNT(*), a WHERE with one comparison, ORDER BY, FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY. The output ends with the number of records selected.
    .synopsis = db2 "SELECT ... FROM LIBRARY.TABLE [WHERE ...]"
    .example-1 = how many orders are open
    .example-2 = the first five, two columns
    .example-3 = one order
man-SIGNOFF = ends your interactive (5250) job and closes the session. In a batch job or from PASE, it isn't allowed (CPD0031).
    .synopsis = SIGNOFF [LOG(*NOLIST | *LIST)]
    .option-LOG = keep the job log of the session (*LIST) or not (*NOLIST).
    .example-1 = leave the green screen
    .example-2 = the same, from the main menu (option 90)
man-WRKACTJOB = work with active jobs. Every job running on the system, grouped by subsystem (QBATCH for batch jobs, QINTER for the 5250 sessions…), with its user, type (BCH batch, INT interactive, SBS a subsystem), CPU share, function (the program or command it runs) and status. The status says what it does: RUN (running), DSPW (waiting at a display), DEQW (waiting for work), LCKW (waiting for a lock), MSGW (waiting for a reply to a message: someone must answer it, DSPMSG QSYSOPR).
    .synopsis = WRKACTJOB [SBS(name | *ALL)]
    .option-SBS = only the jobs of this subsystem.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = every active job
    .example-2 = the batch jobs only
man-WRKSBS = work with subsystems. The active subsystems (QBATCH, QINTER, QCTL…), each with its storage pools. A subsystem runs jobs: those its job queues feed, and its autostart jobs.
    .synopsis = WRKSBS
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = the active subsystems
man-WRKJOBQ = work with job queues. Without a name, every job queue and how many jobs wait in it; with a name, the jobs waiting there (RLS: released, they start when the subsystem has room; HLD: held). QBATCH runs one batch job at a time: a job stuck in MSGW holds back the whole queue.
    .synopsis = WRKJOBQ [JOBQ(library/name | *ALL)]
    .option-JOBQ = the job queue (QBATCH…).
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = every job queue
    .example-2 = what waits in QBATCH
man-WRKSYSSTS = work with system status. The processor's use, the jobs in the system, and the auxiliary storage: the capacity of the system ASP (the disks) and the share used ("% system ASP used"). Past the ASP's threshold, QSYSOPR receives CPF0907; at 100 %, the system stops.
    .synopsis = WRKSYSSTS
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .option-RESET = *NO, or *YES to restart the counters.
    .option-ASTLVL = the assistance level.
    .example-1 = the system's status, its disk use
man-DSPMSG = displays the messages of a message queue. The queue is QSYSOPR (the system operator's: what jobs ask and what the system reports) or a user's (the default: yours). An inquiry message waits for a reply: its line shows "Reply . . . :" and the job that sent it stays in MSGW until it is answered. The valid replies are in parentheses at the end of the message (R C G D F); its second-level text (F1; in the game, ? at the reply prompt) gives the cause, the recovery and what each reply does. Answering without reading it can lose a night's work.
    .synopsis = DSPMSG [MSGQ(QSYSOPR | *SYSOPR | *WRKUSR | name)]
    .option-MSGQ = the message queue: QSYSOPR (*SYSOPR), *WRKUSR (yours), or a queue's name.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .option-MSGTYPE = *ALL, *INFO or *INQ.
    .option-START = *LAST or *FIRST: the newest messages first, or the oldest.
    .option-SEV = the minimum severity shown.
    .example-1 = the operator's messages, and the inquiries waiting for a reply
    .example-2 = your own messages
man-WRKMSGQ = work with message queues. Without a name, the list of queues; with a name, its messages, like DSPMSG.
    .synopsis = WRKMSGQ [MSGQ(name | *ALL)]
    .option-MSGQ = the message queue.
    .example-1 = the message queues
    .example-2 = the operator's messages
man-DSPJOBLOG = displays a job's log. Every message the job received, in order, with its ID, type and severity (CPF1124 when it started, a diagnostic CPF5027 when a record was in use, the inquiry it is waiting on…). The first place to look when a job stops. Without JOB, your own job.
    .synopsis = DSPJOBLOG [JOB(number/user/name)]
    .option-JOB = the job: number/user/name (as WRKACTJOB and the messages give it), or its name alone.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .option-MSGF = the message file.
    .example-1 = the night import's log
    .example-2 = your session's log
man-WRKOBJLCK = work with object locks. The jobs that hold a lock on an object (a file open for update holds *SHRUPD), and the lock's state. A file locked this way can't be saved without save-while-active (SAVACT).
    .synopsis = WRKOBJLCK OBJ(library/object) OBJTYPE(*FILE | *DTAARA | ...)
    .option-OBJ = the object, qualified by its library.
    .option-OBJTYPE = its type (*FILE…).
    .option-MBR = a member of a database file.
    .example-1 = who holds the order headers
man-DSPRCDLCK = displays a database member's record locks. For each locked record (its relative record number), the job that holds it (HELD) and the jobs waiting for it (WAIT). A record held by a 5250 session left on an update screen blocks every batch job that needs it.
    .synopsis = DSPRCDLCK FILE(library/file) [MBR(name)] [RCDNBR(number)]
    .option-FILE = the physical file, qualified by its library.
    .option-MBR = its member (*FIRST by default).
    .option-RCDNBR = only this record.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = the locked records of ORDHDR
man-ENDJOB = ends a job. OPTION(*CNTRLD), the default, lets it end on its own within DELAY seconds; *IMMED ends it at once. Ending a batch job in the middle of its work loses what it hadn't done (and sometimes leaves half of it done): read its log and its messages first. Ending a 5250 session releases its locks.
    .synopsis = ENDJOB JOB(number/user/name) [OPTION(*CNTRLD | *IMMED)] [DELAY(seconds)]
    .option-JOB = the job: number/user/name, or its name.
    .option-OPTION = *CNTRLD (controlled, the default) or *IMMED.
    .option-DELAY = the time given to a controlled end, in seconds.
    .option-SPLFILE = keep (*NO) or delete (*YES) its spooled files.
    .option-LOGLMT = how many messages its job log keeps.
    .example-1 = end a 5250 session left open
    .example-2 = the same, by its name, at once
man-SBMJOB = submits a job. The command in CMD runs in batch, as a new job, when its job queue lets it start (QBATCH: one job at a time). The usual way to run a program again: SBMJOB CMD(CALL PGM(LIB/PGM)). JOB names the job (by default, its job description's), USER the profile it runs under (*CURRENT: yours).
    .synopsis = SBMJOB CMD(command) [JOB(name)] [JOBQ(library/name)] [USER(name)]
    .option-CMD = the command the job runs (CALL PGM(ORDERS/ORDIMP)…).
    .option-JOB = the job's name.
    .option-JOBD = the job description (*USRPRF).
    .option-JOBQ = the job queue (*JOBD: QBATCH).
    .option-USER = the user profile it runs under.
    .example-1 = run the night import again
    .example-2 = print the invoices again
man-CALL = calls a program in your own job. A program that must run in batch (the mill's night programs check it) is submitted with SBMJOB instead.
    .synopsis = CALL PGM(library/program) [PARM(values)]
    .option-PGM = the program, qualified by its library.
    .option-PARM = its parameters.
    .example-1 = call the night import (it refuses: SBMJOB)
man-ENDSBS = ends a subsystem and the jobs running in it (OPTION *CNTRLD with a DELAY, or *IMMED). Its users and its autostart jobs stop: plan it, and start it again with STRSBS.
    .synopsis = ENDSBS SBS(name) [OPTION(*CNTRLD | *IMMED)] [DELAY(seconds)]
    .option-SBS = the subsystem.
    .option-OPTION = *CNTRLD (the default) or *IMMED.
    .option-DELAY = the time given to a controlled end.
    .example-1 = end the orders subsystem within 30 seconds
    .example-2 = the same, at once, by position
man-STRSBS = starts a subsystem from its description (library/name). Its autostart jobs start, its job queues are served again.
    .synopsis = STRSBS SBSD(library/name)
    .option-SBSD = the subsystem description.
    .example-1 = start the orders subsystem again
man-WRKOUTQ = work with output queues. Without a name, every output queue and its number of spooled files; with a name, the files waiting there (to print, held or saved). A queue shared by several applications holds everyone's files: what you delete there, you delete for everyone.
    .synopsis = WRKOUTQ [OUTQ(library/name | *ALL)]
    .option-OUTQ = the output queue (QPRINT…).
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = every output queue
    .example-2 = what is in QPRINT
man-WRKSPLF = work with spooled files. Printed output waiting in output queues. Each has a file name (the printer file: INVOICE, DBGLIST…), a user, a queue, user data (set by the program), a status (RDY ready, HLD held, SAV saved after printing) and a number of pages. SELECT chooses them: user, then print device or queue, form type, user data.
    .synopsis = WRKSPLF [SELECT(user [device [form-type [user-data]]])]
    .option-SELECT = user (*CURRENT, *ALL or a name), device or queue, form type, user data (*ALL for any).
    .option-ASTLVL = the assistance level.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = your spooled files
    .example-2 = those of the batch user
    .example-3 = the invoices, whoever printed them
man-DLTSPLF = deletes spooled files. FILE names them in one job (JOB, SPLNBR: one number, *LAST, *ALL); FILE(*SELECT) deletes every file matching SELECT, across jobs: user, print device, form type, user data. Choose them as narrowly as possible: an output queue shared by several applications holds files that still have to print.
    .synopsis = DLTSPLF FILE(name | *SELECT) [JOB(number/user/name)] [SPLNBR(n | *ONLY | *LAST | *ALL)] [SELECT(user device form-type user-data)]
    .option-FILE = the spooled file's name, or *SELECT.
    .option-JOB = the job that created it (* for yours).
    .option-SPLNBR = its number in the job, *ONLY, *LAST or *ALL.
    .option-JOBSYSNAME = the system where the job ran.
    .option-CRTDATE = its creation date.
    .option-SELECT = with FILE(*SELECT): user, print device, form type, user data.
    .example-1 = one of your own listings
    .example-2 = every DBGLIST of one night's job
    .example-3 = every debug listing of the batch user, whatever the job
man-CLROUTQ = clears an output queue. Deletes every spooled file in it, whoever created it and whatever its status. On a queue of its own (a test queue), fine; on the shared QPRINT, it deletes what still had to print.
    .synopsis = CLROUTQ OUTQ(library/name)
    .option-OUTQ = the output queue.
    .example-1 = clear a test queue
man-DSPDTAARA = displays a data area. A small named object holding a value (a counter, a setting, a switch read by programs).
    .synopsis = DSPDTAARA DTAARA(library/name)
    .option-DTAARA = the data area, qualified by its library.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .option-OUTFMT = *CHAR or *HEX.
    .example-1 = the switch of ORDPICK's debug listing
man-CHGDTAARA = changes a data area's value. All of it, or a substring (start length) given after its name, in parentheses. A value with lower-case letters or blanks is written between quotes.
    .synopsis = CHGDTAARA DTAARA(library/name [(start length)]) VALUE(value)
    .option-DTAARA = the data area, and possibly the substring to change.
    .option-VALUE = the new value.
    .example-1 = turn the debug listing off
    .example-2 = the same, on its first character
man-DSPLIB = displays a library. Its objects, their type (*FILE, *PGM, *DTAARA…), attribute (PF a physical file, a table; PRTF a printer file; SAVF a save file; RPGLE, CLLE a program's language), size and text.
    .synopsis = DSPLIB LIB(name)
    .option-LIB = the library.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = the order book's objects
    .example-2 = the save files
man-DSPLOG = displays the history log (QHST). What the system recorded, job starts and ends (CPF1124, CPF1241 "completed normally", CPF1164 "ended abnormally"), subsystems, saves (CPF3771, "not saved"). A job that "completed normally" may still have done less than asked: read its messages.
    .synopsis = DSPLOG [JOB(name)] [MSGID(id)]
    .option-LOG = QHST, the history log.
    .option-PERIOD = the period shown.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .option-JOB = only this job's messages.
    .option-MSGID = only this message.
    .example-1 = the whole history log
    .example-2 = what the night's save job recorded
    .example-3 = the saves that left objects behind
man-CRTSAVF = creates a save file. A file that holds a save (SAVLIB, SAVOBJ) on disk, to copy elsewhere afterwards.
    .synopsis = CRTSAVF FILE(library/name) [TEXT('description')]
    .option-FILE = the save file, qualified by its library.
    .option-TEXT = its description.
    .option-AUT = its public authority.
    .example-1 = a new save file
man-DSPSAVF = displays what a save file holds. The library saved, when, whether while active, and every object saved. Compare with DSPLIB: an object missing here wasn't saved.
    .synopsis = DSPSAVF FILE(library/name)
    .option-FILE = the save file.
    .option-OBJ = only these objects.
    .option-OUTPUT = * (display) or *PRINT.
    .example-1 = the last save of the order book
man-SAVLIB = saves a library, here to a save file (DEV(*SAVF) SAVF(...)). An object in use (a file open for update by a job) isn't saved: CPF3202, CPF3741, and the summary CPF3771 "n objects saved, 1 not saved". SAVACT(*LIB) saves while active: the library reaches a checkpoint (CPI3712) and the save goes on while the jobs work. A save file that already holds a save asks CPA4067 (C cancel, G clear it and go on), unless CLEAR(*ALL).
    .synopsis = SAVLIB LIB(name) DEV(*SAVF) SAVF(library/name) [SAVACT(*NO | *LIB | *SYSDEFN | *SYNCLIB)] [CLEAR(*NONE | *ALL)]
    .option-LIB = the library to save.
    .option-DEV = *SAVF to save to a save file.
    .option-SAVF = the save file.
    .option-SAVACT = save while active: *NO (the default), *LIB, *SYSDEFN, *SYNCLIB.
    .option-SAVACTWAIT = how long to wait for locks.
    .option-CLEAR = *ALL: clear the save file without asking.
    .example-1 = save the order book while it works, over the last save
    .example-2 = the same, the first two parameters by position
man-CPYTOIMPF = copies a database file to an import file. Here a stream file of the IFS, in CSV (DTAFMT(*DLM): fields separated by FLDDLM, strings between STRDLM, one record per line ended by RCDDLM). STMFCCSID gives the file's character set: *STMF (the default) keeps an existing file's, and gives a new file the table's: 37, EBCDIC, unreadable on Linux. 1208 is UTF-8, *PCASCII Windows-1252. MBROPT(*ADD), the default, appends to an existing file: run twice, every record twice; *REPLACE writes it again.
    .synopsis = CPYTOIMPF FROMFILE(library/file) TOSTMF('path') [MBROPT(*ADD | *REPLACE)] [STMFCCSID(*STMF | *PCASCII | *STDASCII | ccsid)] [RCDDLM(*EOR | *CRLF | *LF | *CR)] [ADDCOLNAM(*NONE | *SQL | *SYS)]
    .option-FROMFILE = the database file (the table).
    .option-TOSTMF = the stream file to write (its directory must exist).
    .option-MBROPT = *ADD (append, the default) or *REPLACE.
    .option-STMFCCSID = the stream file's CCSID: *STMF, *STDASCII (819), *PCASCII (1252), or a number: 1208 (UTF-8), 37 (EBCDIC).
    .option-RCDDLM = the end of each record: *EOR (CRLF), *CRLF, *LF, *CR.
    .option-STRDLM = the string delimiter (*DBLQUOTE by default, or *NONE).
    .option-FLDDLM = the field delimiter (a comma by default, *TAB).
    .option-ADDCOLNAM = a first line with the columns' names (*SQL, *SYS).
    .example-1 = the orders in UTF-8, one per line (LF)
    .example-2 = with the columns' names, CRLF line ends

## Next Door Windows: line endings, the Windows share, PowerShell's cmdlets

man-dos2unix = converts a text file's line endings from DOS/Windows (CR LF, \r\n) to Unix (LF, \n), in place. A script written on Windows has a \r at the end of each line: bash takes it as part of the line's last word ("/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter", "$'\r': command not found"). Without a file, converts what comes through a pipe. Its messages: "dos2unix: converting file NAME to Unix format...". unix2dos does the opposite.
    .synopsis = dos2unix [-ikq] [file...] ; dos2unix -n infile outfile
    .option-i = information only: the number of DOS (CR LF), Unix (LF) and Mac (CR) line breaks, then the file's name; nothing is changed.
    .option-k = keep the file's date.
    .option-n = write the result to a new file (pairs: infile outfile).
    .option-q = quiet.
    .example-1 = convert the script to Unix line endings
    .example-2 = count its line breaks by kind, without changing anything
    .example-3 = write the converted text to another file
man-unix2dos = converts a text file's line endings from Unix (LF) to DOS/Windows (CR LF), in place: for a file that will be opened on Windows by an old program. dos2unix does the opposite.
    .synopsis = unix2dos [-ikq] [file...] ; unix2dos -n infile outfile
    .option-i = information only: the number of DOS, Unix and Mac line breaks.
    .option-k = keep the file's date.
    .option-n = write the result to a new file.
    .option-q = quiet.
    .example-1 = convert a file to Windows line endings
    .example-2 = count its line breaks by kind
man-tr = translates or deletes characters, from what comes through a pipe to its output (translate). It reads no file: give it one through cat FILE | tr …, and send the result to ANOTHER file (> new), never to the one being read. Sets understand ranges (a-z), classes ([:upper:], [:space:]) and escapes (\r carriage return, \n newline, \t tab).
    .synopsis = tr [-ds] SET1 [SET2]
    .option-d = delete the characters of SET1.
    .option-s = squeeze: a run of the same character of the last set becomes one.
    .example-1 = remove the carriage returns (Windows line endings) into a new file
    .example-2 = upper case
    .example-3 = squeeze repeated spaces
man-gitattributes = the .gitattributes file, at the root of a git repository (and committed with it), gives attributes to paths, one pattern per line: "*.sh text eol=lf". text: the file is text, git stores it with LF line endings whatever the working copy has (git add warns: "CRLF will be replaced by LF"); eol=lf (or eol=crlf): the line endings written in the working copy; text=auto: git decides if it is text; binary or -text: never touched. Unlike core.autocrlf, a setting of each machine, .gitattributes travels with the repository: every clone follows it.
    .synopsis = PATTERN ATTRIBUTE...   (in .gitattributes)
    .example-1 = the shell scripts in LF, whatever the editor
    .example-2 = commit it with the converted file
    .example-3 = every text file in LF
man-mount_cifs = mounts a Windows (SMB) share in the tree: mount -t cifs //SERVER/SHARE POINT -o OPTIONS runs it (package cifs-utils; without it, mount asks for "a /sbin/mount.<type> helper program"). Options (-o, separated by commas): credentials=FILE (a file of root's, mode 600, with username=, password= and domain= lines: never the password on the command line, which history and ps keep), username=, password=, uid= and gid= (who the files belong to on Linux: a Windows server has no Unix owners, root by default), file_mode= and dir_mode= (their rights, 0755 by default; chown and chmod change nothing on such a mount), vers= (the SMB version: 3.1.1 by default; vers=1.0 asks for SMB1, which modern Windows refuses: "mount error(112): Host is down"), ro. In /etc/fstab, add _netdev (a network file system: wait for the network) and nofail (the boot doesn't depend on the server). Errors: mount error(13) Permission denied (account or password), mount error(2) (no such share).
    .synopsis = mount -t cifs //server/share point -o option,option... ; mount.cifs //server/share point -o options
    .example-1 = the server's shares (smbclient), before mounting
    .example-2 = mount the share for the account plotter, the password in a file of root's
    .example-3 = the same with mount.cifs, asking for SMB 3.0
    .example-4 = the line for /etc/fstab: _netdev and nofail, the boot doesn't wait for the server
man-Get-ChildItem = gets the items in a location: the files and folders of a directory (aliases ls, dir, gci). On Windows, paths are C:\Users\you or D:\Shares, and the case doesn't matter. The items are objects with properties (Name, Length in bytes, LastWriteTime, FullName, Mode): Sort-Object, Where-Object and Select-Object work on those properties. -Recurse goes down every subfolder. Linux's options don't exist: ls -la gives "A parameter cannot be found that matches parameter name 'la'".
    .synopsis = Get-ChildItem [[-Path] <String[]>] [[-Filter] <String>] [-Recurse] [-Depth <UInt32>] [-Force] [-Name] [-File] [-Directory]
    .option-Path = where to look (by default, the current location); wildcards * and ? allowed.
    .option-Filter = only the names that match (*.zip).
    .option-Recurse = go down every subfolder.
    .option-Depth = how many levels to go down.
    .option-Force = include hidden and system items.
    .option-Name = only the names, as text.
    .option-File = only files.
    .option-Directory = only folders.
    .example-1 = what's here
    .example-2 = a folder, hidden items included
    .example-3 = the five largest files of the drive D:
    .example-4 = the alias ls works too (without Linux's options)
man-Get-Content = gets the content of a file, line by line (aliases cat, type, gc).
    .synopsis = Get-Content [-Path] <String[]> [-TotalCount <Int64>] [-Tail <Int32>] [-Raw] [-Encoding <Encoding>]
    .option-Path = the file(s).
    .option-TotalCount = only the first lines (also -Head, -First).
    .option-Tail = only the last lines.
    .option-Encoding = the text's encoding (ignored by the game).
    .example-1 = read a file
    .example-2 = its last five lines
    .example-3 = the alias cat
    .option-Raw = the whole file as one string.
man-Set-Location = sets the current working location (aliases cd, chdir, sl). cd.. and cd\ work as well.
    .synopsis = Set-Location [[-Path] <String>]
    .option-Path = where to go (D:\, .., ~).
    .example-1 = go to D:\Shares
    .example-2 = go up one level
    .example-3 = go back to your profile's folder
man-Get-Location = gets the current working location (aliases pwd, gl).
    .synopsis = Get-Location
    .example-1 = where am I?
    .example-2 = the alias pwd
man-Get-Service = gets the services on the computer: their status (Running, Stopped), their name and their display name. Windows' services are what systemd's units are on Linux: LanmanServer is the file server (the shares), sshd the OpenSSH server, wuauserv Windows Update.
    .synopsis = Get-Service [[-Name] <String[]>] [-DisplayName <String[]>] [-DependentServices] [-RequiredServices]
    .option-Name = the services' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = their display names.
    .example-1 = every service
    .example-2 = the file server's service
    .example-3 = the OpenSSH services
    .example-4 = those that run
    .option-DependentServices = the services that depend on this one.
    .option-RequiredServices = the services this one needs.
man-Get-PSDrive = gets the drives of the session: the volumes (C, D) with what they use and have free, in GB, and the other providers' drives (the registry, HKLM; the environment, Env).
    .synopsis = Get-PSDrive [[-Name] <String[]>] [-PSProvider <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the drives' names (C, D).
    .option-PSProvider = only the drives of a provider (FileSystem).
    .example-1 = every drive, with used and free space
    .example-2 = the drive D:
man-Get-Volume = gets the volumes: drive letter, label (FriendlyName), file system (NTFS), health, size and free space (SizeRemaining). The Windows side of df -h.
    .synopsis = Get-Volume [[-DriveLetter] <Char[]>]
    .option-DriveLetter = the letters (D).
    .example-1 = every volume
    .example-2 = the volume D:
    .example-3 = every property, sizes in bytes
man-Get-WinEvent = gets events from the event logs: System (the system and its services), Application, Security… Each event has a time, an ID, a level (Information, Warning, Error), a provider (the program that wrote it) and a message. The Windows side of journalctl.
    .synopsis = Get-WinEvent [[-LogName] <String[]>] [-MaxEvents <Int64>] [-ProviderName <String[]>] [-FilterHashtable <Hashtable[]>] [-ListLog <String[]>] [-Oldest]
    .option-LogName = the log (System, Application).
    .option-MaxEvents = only the most recent ones.
    .example-1 = the ten most recent events of the System log
    .example-2 = its warnings
    .option-ProviderName = the program that wrote the events (Service Control Manager…).
    .option-FilterHashtable = a filter: @{"{"}LogName="System"; Id=7000,7038; Level=2; StartTime=(Get-Date).AddDays(-1){"}"} (Level: 1 critical, 2 error, 3 warning, 4 information).
    .option-ListLog = the logs themselves (* for all): their size, their number of events.
    .option-Oldest = oldest first.
    .example-3 = the last ten errors
man-Get-EventLog = gets the events of a classic event log (System, Application), the older cmdlet that Get-WinEvent replaces; still in Windows PowerShell 5.1.
    .synopsis = Get-EventLog [-LogName] <String> [-Newest <Int32>] [-EntryType <String[]>] [-Source <String[]>] [-InstanceId <Int64[]>] [-After <DateTime>] [-Before <DateTime>] [-List]
    .option-LogName = the log (System, Application).
    .option-Newest = only the most recent ones.
    .option-EntryType = only one level (Error, Warning, Information).
    .option-Source = only one source (srv).
    .example-1 = the ten most recent events of the System log
    .example-2 = its warnings
    .option-InstanceId = the event ids (7000, 4625…).
    .option-After = after a date.
    .option-Before = before a date.
    .option-List = the classic logs.
    .example-3 = the last failed logons
man-Get-SmbShare = gets the SMB shares of the server: their name, their path on the server, their description. The ones ending with $ (C$, ADMIN$, IPC$) are Windows' administrative shares.
    .synopsis = Get-SmbShare [[-Name] <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the shares' names.
    .example-1 = every share
    .example-2 = one share, every property
man-Where-Object = selects the objects of the pipeline that match a condition: on a property and an operator (Where-Object Length -gt 1GB), or in a script block where $_ is the current object ({"{"} $_.Status -eq 'Running' {"}"}). Operators: -eq, -ne, -gt, -ge, -lt, -le, -like (wildcards), -match (regular expression); text is compared without case. Numbers take KB, MB, GB, TB.
    .synopsis = Where-Object [-Property] <String> [[-Value] <Object>] -EQ|-NE|-GT|-GE|-LT|-LE|-Like|-Match ; Where-Object [-FilterScript] <ScriptBlock>
    .option-FilterScript = the condition, in braces, on $_.
    .option-Property = the property to compare.
    .option-Value = the value to compare it with.
    .option-EQ = equal.
    .option-NE = not equal.
    .option-GT = greater than.
    .option-GE = greater than or equal.
    .option-LT = less than.
    .option-LE = less than or equal.
    .option-Like = matches a wildcard pattern.
    .option-NotLike = doesn't match a wildcard pattern.
    .option-Match = matches a regular expression.
    .option-NotMatch = doesn't match a regular expression.
    .example-1 = the files over 10 GB on D:
    .example-2 = the stopped services
    .example-3 = the zip files here
man-Sort-Object = sorts objects by the value of their properties (alias sort): numbers as numbers, text without case. The pipeline carries objects, so sort by Length, not by a column of text.
    .synopsis = Sort-Object [[-Property] <Object[]>] [-Descending] [-Unique]
    .option-Property = the properties to sort by, in order.
    .option-Descending = largest first.
    .option-Unique = without duplicates.
    .example-1 = the files, largest first
    .example-2 = the services by status, then name
man-Select-Object = selects objects (the first, the last) or some of their properties (alias select). -ExpandProperty gives a property's values alone.
    .synopsis = Select-Object [[-Property] <Object[]>] [-First <Int32>] [-Last <Int32>] [-Skip <Int32>] [-ExpandProperty <String>]
    .option-Property = the properties to keep.
    .option-First = only the first objects.
    .option-Last = only the last objects.
    .option-Skip = skip the first objects.
    .option-ExpandProperty = the values of one property.
    .example-1 = the first five
    .example-2 = two properties only
    .example-3 = a value alone, in bytes
man-Format-Table = formats the output as a table of the chosen properties (alias ft). Comes last in a pipeline: what it produces is text to display, not objects.
    .synopsis = Format-Table [[-Property] <Object[]>] [-AutoSize] [-Wrap]
    .option-Property = the columns.
    .option-AutoSize = columns sized to their content (the game always does).
    .option-Wrap = long values on several lines (ignored by the game).
    .example-1 = name and size
    .example-2 = the services and how they start
man-Format-List = formats the output as a list, one property per line (alias fl): Format-List * shows every property.
    .synopsis = Format-List [[-Property] <Object[]>]
    .option-Property = the properties (* for all).
    .example-1 = every property of the volumes
    .example-2 = every property of a file
man-Measure-Object = counts objects, and computes the sum, average, maximum or minimum of a numeric property (alias measure).
    .synopsis = Measure-Object [[-Property] <String[]>] [-Sum] [-Average] [-Maximum] [-Minimum]
    .option-Property = the property to measure.
    .option-Sum = its sum.
    .option-Average = its average.
    .option-Maximum = its largest value.
    .option-Minimum = its smallest value.
    .example-1 = how many files, and their total size in bytes
man-Write-Output = sends objects down the pipeline, or to the screen at the end of it (aliases echo, write).
    .synopsis = Write-Output [-InputObject] <PSObject[]>
    .option-InputObject = what to send.
    .example-1 = print a word
    .example-2 = the alias echo
man-Clear-Host = clears the screen (aliases cls, clear).
    .synopsis = Clear-Host
    .example-1 = clear the screen
    .example-2 = the alias cls
man-Get-Help = displays the help of a cmdlet (aliases help, man): its synopsis, syntax and description, its parameters and examples with -Full, its examples alone with -Examples. NAME -? shows it too. The "manual first" reflex, on Windows.
    .synopsis = Get-Help [[-Name] <String>] [-Examples] [-Detailed] [-Full]
    .option-Name = the cmdlet (or one of its aliases).
    .option-Examples = only the examples.
    .option-Detailed = with the parameters and examples.
    .option-Full = everything.
    .example-1 = the help of Get-ChildItem
    .example-2 = the examples, through its alias
    .example-3 = everything about Get-Volume
    .example-4 = a cmdlet's help, with -?
man-Get-Command = gets the commands available: cmdlets (verb-noun: Get-ChildItem), functions and aliases. The way to find the cmdlet you need: Get-Command *event*.
    .synopsis = Get-Command [[-Name] <String[]>] [-Verb <String[]>] [-Noun <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-Verb = only one verb (Get).
    .option-Noun = only one noun (Service).
    .example-1 = every command
    .example-2 = the Get- cmdlets
    .example-3 = those about events
man-Get-Alias = gets the aliases: the short names (ls, cat, cd, sort) and the cmdlet behind each.
    .synopsis = Get-Alias [[-Name] <String[]>] [-Definition <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the aliases.
    .option-Definition = the aliases of one cmdlet.
    .example-1 = every alias
    .example-2 = what is ls?
    .example-3 = the aliases of Get-ChildItem

## The Windows engine: cmdlets and Windows programs (windows-*.ts).
man-ForEach-Object = runs a script block for each object that comes through the pipe; inside it, $_ (or $PSItem) is the current object. Its aliases: foreach and %. What the block outputs goes on down the pipe.
    .synopsis = ForEach-Object [-Process] <ScriptBlock[]> [-Begin <ScriptBlock>] [-End <ScriptBlock>]
    .option-Process = the block run for each object.
    .option-Begin = a block run once, before the first object.
    .option-End = a block run once, after the last object.
    .option-MemberName = a property to take from each object (ForEach-Object Name).
    .example-1 = each service name in capitals
    .example-2 = 10, 20, 30
    .example-3 = the users' names, with the short alias
man-Group-Object = groups the objects that have the same value of a property, and counts them: a table of Count, Name and Group (the objects themselves).
    .synopsis = Group-Object [[-Property] <Object[]>] [-NoElement]
    .option-Property = the property to group by.
    .option-NoElement = only Count and Name, without the objects.
    .example-1 = how many services run, how many are stopped
    .example-2 = the most frequent events
man-Out-String = turns objects into the text PowerShell would show (their table or list), as one string, or one line per string with -Stream.
    .synopsis = Out-String [-Stream] [-Width <Int32>]
    .option-Stream = one string per line.
    .option-Width = the width of the lines.
    .example-1 = the table as text
    .example-2 = search the shown lines, like grep
man-Out-Null = throws away what comes through the pipe: nothing is shown. Like > /dev/null on Linux.
    .synopsis = Out-Null [-InputObject <PSObject>]
    .option-InputObject = the object to throw away.
    .example-1 = create a folder without showing it
man-Out-Host = sends the objects to the screen, formatted (what happens anyway at the end of a pipeline).
    .synopsis = Out-Host [-Paging]
    .option-Paging = one screen at a time (like more).
    .example-1 = the services on the screen
man-Write-Host = writes text straight to the screen, not into the pipe: what Write-Host shows can't be piped, filtered nor saved. To pass a value on, use Write-Output (or just the value).
    .synopsis = Write-Host [[-Object] <Object>] [-NoNewline] [-Separator <Object>] [-ForegroundColor <ConsoleColor>]
    .option-Object = what to write.
    .option-NoNewline = no new line at the end.
    .option-Separator = what goes between the objects.
    .option-ForegroundColor = the text colour.
    .example-1 = a message on the screen
    .example-2 = a, b
man-Write-Warning = writes a warning, in front of WARNING:, on the screen.
    .synopsis = Write-Warning [-Message] <String>
    .option-Message = the warning.
    .example-1 = WARNING: Disk D: almost full
man-Write-Error = writes an error (a non-terminating one: what follows still runs), in PowerShell's layout; it goes into $Error.
    .synopsis = Write-Error [-Message] <String>
    .option-Message = the error's text.
    .example-1 = an error of your own
man-Get-Member = shows what an object is made of: its type (TypeName) and its members, properties and methods. The first reflex in front of an unknown object: Get-Service | Get-Member.
    .synopsis = Get-Member [[-Name] <String[]>] [-MemberType <PSMemberTypes>]
    .option-Name = the members to show (wildcards allowed).
    .option-MemberType = Property or Method.
    .example-1 = a service object's properties and methods
    .example-2 = only the properties
man-Get-Date = gets the date and time (a DateTime object, with Year, Month, Day, Hour, DayOfWeek… and methods like AddDays). With -Format, a text: yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm.
    .synopsis = Get-Date [[-Date] <DateTime>] [-Format <String>]
    .option-Date = a given date rather than now.
    .option-Format = the format (yyyy-MM-dd, HH:mm, dddd…).
    .example-1 = now
    .example-2 = today, for a file name
    .example-3 = this time yesterday
man-Read-Host = reads a line typed by the user. With -AsSecureString, the typing isn't shown and the result is a SecureString: the way to pass a password to Set-LocalUser or New-LocalUser without leaving it in the history.
    .synopsis = Read-Host [[-Prompt] <Object>] [-AsSecureString]
    .option-Prompt = the question shown (followed by ": ").
    .option-AsSecureString = a hidden input, as a SecureString.
    .example-1 = ask for a password without showing it
    .example-2 = then use it
man-New-Object = creates a .NET object: here System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemAccessRule (an access rule for Set-Acl), System.Security.Principal.NTAccount, PSObject, Collections.ArrayList, DateTime.
    .synopsis = New-Object [-TypeName] <String> [[-ArgumentList] <Object[]>] [-Property <IDictionary>]
    .option-TypeName = the type.
    .option-ArgumentList = the constructor's arguments (identity, rights, inheritance, propagation, Allow or Deny).
    .option-Property = the properties of a PSObject.
    .example-1 = a Modify rule inherited by files and folders
man-Select-String = finds text: the lines of files (or of what comes through the pipe) that match a regular expression, without case by default. The PowerShell side of grep; with -Path it shows FILE:LINE:text.
    .synopsis = Select-String [-Pattern] <String[]> [[-Path] <String[]>] [-SimpleMatch] [-CaseSensitive] [-NotMatch]
    .option-Pattern = the regular expression.
    .option-Path = the files to read.
    .option-SimpleMatch = plain text, not a regular expression.
    .option-CaseSensitive = tell capitals apart.
    .option-NotMatch = the lines that don't match.
    .example-1 = the error lines of the logs
    .example-2 = one line of a program's output
man-Get-Variable = lists the session's variables ($x = … creates one) and their values.
    .synopsis = Get-Variable [[-Name] <String[]>] [-ValueOnly]
    .option-Name = the variables (wildcards allowed).
    .option-ValueOnly = only the values.
    .example-1 = every variable you created
man-Start-Sleep = waits a moment (in the game, it returns at once).
    .synopsis = Start-Sleep [-Seconds] <Double> [-Milliseconds <Int32>]
    .option-Seconds = how long.
    .option-Milliseconds = in milliseconds.
    .example-1 = wait five seconds
man-Get-History = lists the lines typed in this session (aliases h and history).
    .synopsis = Get-History [[-Id] <Int64[]>] [-Count <Int32>]
    .option-Id = the lines by number.
    .option-Count = the last N lines.
    .example-1 = the last five lines
man-Get-Item = gets an item itself (a file, a folder, a registry key), not its contents: its properties (Length, LastWriteTime, FullName…) for one object.
    .synopsis = Get-Item [-Path] <String[]> [-Force]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Force = hidden items too.
    .example-1 = the folder's object
    .example-2 = a file's size
    .example-3 = a registry key and its values
man-Set-Content = writes a file's contents, replacing what was there (the file is created if needed): each value, or each object of the pipe, is a line. Writing needs the NTFS right to write (or to create files in the folder). Alias sc: sc.exe, the service tool, must be typed with its extension.
    .synopsis = Set-Content [-Path] <String[]> [-Value] <Object[]> [-Encoding <Encoding>] [-Force]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Value = the lines to write.
    .option-Encoding = the encoding (UTF8, ASCII, Unicode).
    .option-Force = even over a read-only file.
    .option-NoNewline = no new line at the end.
    .example-1 = a one-line file
    .example-2 = what comes through the pipe
man-Add-Content = adds lines at the end of a file (created if needed), like >> on Linux.
    .synopsis = Add-Content [-Path] <String[]> [-Value] <Object[]> [-Encoding <Encoding>]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Value = the lines to add.
    .option-Encoding = the encoding.
    .option-Force = even over a read-only file.
    .example-1 = a line at the end of a log
man-New-Item = creates a file (-ItemType File), a folder (-ItemType Directory; mkdir does the same) or a registry key (New-Item HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Name). A new file or folder inherits its parent's permissions; it belongs to whoever created it (the Administrators group, when an administrator did).
    .synopsis = New-Item [-Path] <String[]> [-Name <String>] [-ItemType <String>] [-Value <Object>] [-Force]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = its name (in the folder given by -Path).
    .option-ItemType = File or Directory.
    .option-Value = a file's contents.
    .option-Force = create the missing folders; overwrite a file.
    .example-1 = a folder
    .example-2 = a file with a line
    .example-3 = a registry key
man-Remove-Item = deletes files, folders or registry keys. A folder with something in it asks for confirmation unless -Recurse; hidden items need -Force. Deleting needs the NTFS right Delete (or Delete subfolders and files on the folder above), for every file below. Aliases rm, del, rd, erase.
    .synopsis = Remove-Item [-Path] <String[]> [-Recurse] [-Force] [-Include <String[]>] [-Exclude <String[]>]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Recurse = what is inside too.
    .option-Force = hidden and read-only items too.
    .option-Include = only these names.
    .option-Exclude = not these names.
    .option-WhatIf = say what would be deleted, delete nothing.
    .example-1 = first, what would go
    .example-2 = a folder and its contents
man-Copy-Item = copies files or folders. A folder without -Recurse is copied empty. A copy takes the permissions of the folder it lands in (it is a new file), and belongs to whoever copied it. Reading the source and writing the destination are both checked; an error on one file doesn't stop the others. Aliases cp, copy.
    .synopsis = Copy-Item [-Path] <String[]> [[-Destination] <String>] [-Recurse] [-Force] [-PassThru]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Destination = where to.
    .option-Recurse = a folder's contents too.
    .option-Force = overwrite.
    .option-PassThru = output the copies.
    .example-1 = a whole share into a backup folder
man-Move-Item = moves (or renames) files and folders. Moving needs the right to delete the source and to write the destination. Aliases mv, move.
    .synopsis = Move-Item [-Path] <String[]> [[-Destination] <String>] [-Force] [-PassThru]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Destination = where to.
    .option-Force = overwrite.
    .option-PassThru = output the items moved.
    .example-1 = files into another folder
man-Rename-Item = renames a file or a folder in place (the new name only, no path). Aliases ren, rni.
    .synopsis = Rename-Item [-Path] <String> [-NewName] <String> [-PassThru]
    .option-Path = the item.
    .option-NewName = its new name.
    .option-PassThru = output the item.
    .example-1 = a new name
man-Test-Path = says whether a path exists: True or False (a file, a folder, a registry key). In a script: if (Test-Path X) {"{"} … {"}"}.
    .synopsis = Test-Path [-Path] <String[]> [-PathType <TestPathType>]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-PathType = Leaf (a file) or Container (a folder).
    .example-1 = does the folder exist?
    .example-2 = a registry key
man-Push-Location = remembers where you are (on a stack), then goes to the path: Pop-Location comes back. Alias pushd.
    .synopsis = Push-Location [[-Path] <String>]
    .option-Path = where to go.
    .example-1 = into the registry for a moment
man-Pop-Location = goes back where Push-Location was called. Alias popd.
    .synopsis = Pop-Location
    .example-1 = back
man-Get-ItemProperty = gets the values of a registry key (each value is a property: (Get-ItemProperty KEY).Name), with PSPath and PSChildName; on a file, the file's object.
    .synopsis = Get-ItemProperty [-Path] <String[]> [[-Name] <String[]>]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = only these values.
    .example-1 = OpenSSH's settings (DefaultShell)
    .example-2 = which Windows
man-Get-ItemPropertyValue = gets a registry value's data alone (not an object).
    .synopsis = Get-ItemPropertyValue [-Path] <String[]> [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = the values.
    .example-1 = the shell OpenSSH starts
man-Set-ItemProperty = changes a registry value (it is created if missing). Under HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NAME, Start (2 automatic, 3 manual, 4 disabled) and ObjectName are the service's start type and logon account.
    .synopsis = Set-ItemProperty [-Path] <String[]> [-Name] <String> [-Value] <Object> [-Type <RegistryValueKind>]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = the value.
    .option-Value = its data.
    .option-Type = String, ExpandString, DWord, QWord, MultiString, Binary.
    .option-PassThru = output the value.
    .example-1 = a value
    .example-2 = the Print Spooler disabled
man-New-ItemProperty = creates a registry value, of a given type (String, DWord…). An existing value: "The property already exists.", unless -Force. Microsoft's documented way to make OpenSSH land in PowerShell: New-ItemProperty -Path HKLM:\SOFTWARE\OpenSSH -Name DefaultShell -Value C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe -PropertyType String -Force.
    .synopsis = New-ItemProperty [-Path] <String[]> [-Name] <String> [-Value <Object>] [-PropertyType <String>] [-Force]
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = the value.
    .option-Value = its data.
    .option-PropertyType = String, ExpandString, DWord, QWord, MultiString, Binary.
    .option-Force = replace an existing value.
    .example-1 = ssh lands in PowerShell
man-Remove-ItemProperty = deletes registry values (not the key). Alias rp.
    .synopsis = Remove-ItemProperty [-Path] <String[]> [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Path = the paths (wildcards allowed; C:\…, HKLM:\…).
    .option-Name = the values.
    .option-Force = even protected ones.
    .example-1 = back to cmd.exe for ssh
man-Out-File = writes what the pipeline would show (its table, its list, its lines) to a file; the redirection > does the same, >> adds to it.
    .synopsis = Out-File [-FilePath] <String> [-Append] [-Encoding <String>]
    .option-FilePath = the file.
    .option-Append = add at the end.
    .option-Encoding = the encoding.
    .option-Force = even over a read-only file.
    .example-1 = the table in a file
    .example-2 = one more line
man-Export-Csv = writes objects to a CSV file, one line per object: the first object's properties as the header, every value in double quotes (nothing between the commas for an empty one). Windows PowerShell 5.1 starts with a #TYPE line naming the objects' type: -NoTypeInformation leaves it out. Select-Object first chooses the columns. A report a spreadsheet, a ticket or Import-Csv can read.
    .synopsis = Export-Csv [[-Path] <String>] [-InputObject <PSObject>] [-NoTypeInformation] [-Delimiter <Char>] [-Append] [-NoClobber] [-Encoding <String>]
    .option-Path = the file to write.
    .option-InputObject = the objects (usually from the pipeline).
    .option-NoTypeInformation = no #TYPE line.
    .option-Delimiter = another separator than the comma.
    .option-Append = add the rows to an existing file.
    .option-NoClobber = refuse to overwrite a file.
    .option-Encoding = the file's encoding (ASCII by default in 5.1).
    .example-1 = a report of inactive accounts
    .example-2 = check it
man-ConvertTo-Csv = turns objects into CSV lines, like Export-Csv but on the screen (or into the pipeline) rather than in a file.
    .synopsis = ConvertTo-Csv [-InputObject] <PSObject> [-NoTypeInformation] [-Delimiter <Char>]
    .option-InputObject = the objects (usually from the pipeline).
    .option-NoTypeInformation = no #TYPE line.
    .option-Delimiter = another separator than the comma.
    .example-1 = see what Export-Csv would write
man-Import-Csv = reads a CSV file as objects: one per line, a property per column of the header (the values are text). The #TYPE line, if any, is skipped.
    .synopsis = Import-Csv [-Path] <String[]> [-Delimiter <Char>] [-Header <String[]>] [-Encoding <String>]
    .option-Path = the file.
    .option-LiteralPath = the file, taken as typed.
    .option-Delimiter = another separator than the comma.
    .option-Header = the column names, for a file without a header line.
    .option-Encoding = the file's encoding.
    .example-1 = read a report back
    .example-2 = count its lines
man-Restore-ADObject = brings back an object of the AD Recycle Bin (Get-ADObject -IncludeDeletedObjects finds it): where it was (its LastKnownParent) or under -TargetPath, with its SID, its attributes, its password and its group memberships (the Recycle Bin keeps the link values). Its parent must exist: restore a deleted OU before what was in it.
    .synopsis = Restore-ADObject [-Identity] <ADObject> [-NewName <String>] [-TargetPath <String>] [-PassThru]
    .option-Identity = the deleted object: its GUID, its DN in CN=Deleted Objects, or an object from the pipeline.
    .option-NewName = another name (if the old one is taken).
    .option-TargetPath = another container than its last parent.
    .option-Partition = the partition (the domain's, by default).
    .option-PassThru = show the restored object.
    .example-1 = find it in the Recycle Bin
    .example-2 = and restore it
man-Get-ADOptionalFeature = gets the forest's optional features: the Recycle Bin Feature (EnabledScopes not empty: it is on, and a deleted object can be restored whole) and Privileged Access Management.
    .synopsis = Get-ADOptionalFeature [-Identity] <ADOptionalFeature> | -Filter <String>
    .option-Identity = the feature: its name, its GUID or its DN.
    .option-Filter = a filter in the module's language (* for all).
    .option-LDAPFilter = an LDAP filter.
    .option-Properties = more properties.
    .example-1 = is the Recycle Bin on?
    .example-2 = every feature
man-Get-GPResultantSetOfPolicy = writes the resulting set of policy of a user on a computer (logging mode: what was applied at their last logon or refresh) to a report, HTML or XML, like gpresult /h: the GPOs applied, those filtered out and why, the groups. The user must have logged on to that computer.
    .synopsis = Get-GPResultantSetOfPolicy [-Computer <String>] [-User <String>] -ReportType <Html|Xml> -Path <String>
    .option-Computer = the computer (this one by default).
    .option-User = the user (you by default).
    .option-ReportType = Html or Xml.
    .option-Path = the report's file.
    .example-1 = a librarian on her workstation, as a web page
man-Get-Acl = gets the security descriptor of a file or folder: its Owner and its Access list (entries: identity, Allow or Deny, rights like FullControl, Modify, ReadAndExecute, inherited or not, inheritance flags). Format-List shows Owner, Access and Sddl; (Get-Acl X).Access lists the entries as objects. An entry inherited from above says IsInherited True.
    .synopsis = Get-Acl [[-Path] <String[]>]
    .option-Path = the files or folders.
    .option-Audit = the audit entries too (none here).
    .example-1 = owner and permissions
    .example-2 = only the explicit entries
man-Set-Acl = applies a security descriptor (from Get-Acl, changed by its methods SetAccessRule, AddAccessRule, RemoveAccessRule, SetAccessRuleProtection, SetOwner) to a file or folder. Needs the right to change permissions (and to take ownership, for the owner).
    .synopsis = Set-Acl [-Path] <String[]> [-AclObject] <Object> [-Passthru]
    .option-Path = the files or folders.
    .option-AclObject = the descriptor.
    .option-Passthru = output the descriptor.
    .example-1 = take the folder's descriptor
    .example-2 = change it
    .example-3 = apply it
man-Get-LocalUser = gets the local user accounts: Name, Enabled, Description; Format-List adds PasswordLastSet, PasswordExpires, LastLogon, SID. (No domain here: Get-ADUser is Active Directory's.)
    .synopsis = Get-LocalUser [[-Name] <String[]>] [-SID <SecurityIdentifier[]>]
    .option-Name = the accounts (wildcards allowed).
    .option-SID = by security identifier.
    .example-1 = every account
    .example-2 = when its password was set, when it expires
man-New-LocalUser = creates a local account. The password is a SecureString (Read-Host -AsSecureString) and must meet the local policy (complexity: three kinds of characters among capitals, small letters, digits, symbols; not the account's name).
    .synopsis = New-LocalUser [-Name] <String> -Password <SecureString> [-FullName <String>] [-Description <String>] [-Disabled] [-PasswordNeverExpires]
    .option-Name = the account.
    .option-Password = its password (SecureString).
    .option-NoPassword = no password.
    .option-FullName = the full name.
    .option-Description = a description.
    .option-Disabled = created disabled.
    .option-PasswordNeverExpires = a password that never expires (a service account).
    .option-UserMayNotChangePassword = the user can't change it.
    .option-AccountNeverExpires = an account that never expires.
    .example-1 = an account, its password typed hidden
man-Set-LocalUser = changes a local account: its password (a SecureString: a plain string is refused), description, full name. A new password is not passed on: the services and scheduled tasks that run as this account keep the old one until you give it to them (sc.exe config, Set-ScheduledTask).
    .synopsis = Set-LocalUser [-Name] <String[]> [-Password <SecureString>] [-Description <String>] [-FullName <String>] [-PasswordNeverExpires <Boolean>]
    .option-Name = the accounts (wildcards allowed).
    .option-InputObject = the account objects.
    .option-Password = the new password (SecureString).
    .option-FullName = the full name.
    .option-Description = a description.
    .option-PasswordNeverExpires = $true: the password never expires.
    .option-UserMayChangePassword = $false: the user can't change it.
    .option-AccountNeverExpires = the account never expires.
    .example-1 = type the password hidden
    .example-2 = then set it
man-Enable-LocalUser = enables a local account (it can log on again).
    .synopsis = Enable-LocalUser [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Name = the accounts (wildcards allowed).
    .option-InputObject = the account objects.
    .example-1 = bob can log on
man-Disable-LocalUser = disables a local account: it can no longer log on, nor run a service or a task.
    .synopsis = Disable-LocalUser [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Name = the accounts (wildcards allowed).
    .option-InputObject = the account objects.
    .example-1 = bob's departure
man-Remove-LocalUser = deletes a local account (its files stay, owned by an unknown SID).
    .synopsis = Remove-LocalUser [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Name = the accounts (wildcards allowed).
    .option-InputObject = the account objects.
    .example-1 = what would go
man-Get-LocalGroup = gets the local groups: the built-in ones (Administrators, Users, Remote Desktop Users, Backup Operators…) and the machine's own.
    .synopsis = Get-LocalGroup [[-Name] <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the groups.
    .example-1 = every group
man-New-LocalGroup = creates a local group (to give rights to a team rather than to each person).
    .synopsis = New-LocalGroup [-Name] <String> [-Description <String>]
    .option-Name = the group.
    .option-Description = a description.
    .example-1 = a group for a team
man-Get-LocalGroupMember = lists a local group's members: ObjectClass (User or Group), Name (CABINET\alice, NT AUTHORITY\Authenticated Users), PrincipalSource.
    .synopsis = Get-LocalGroupMember [-Group] <String> [[-Member] <String>]
    .option-Group = the group.
    .option-Member = only these members.
    .example-1 = who administers the machine
man-Add-LocalGroupMember = adds accounts (or groups) to a local group. A member already there: "… is already a member of group …".
    .synopsis = Add-LocalGroupMember [-Group] <String> [-Member] <LocalPrincipal[]>
    .option-Group = the group.
    .option-Member = the accounts.
    .example-1 = alice joins the team
man-Remove-LocalGroupMember = removes accounts from a local group.
    .synopsis = Remove-LocalGroupMember [-Group] <String> [-Member] <LocalPrincipal[]>
    .option-Group = the group.
    .option-Member = the accounts.
    .example-1 = bob is no longer an administrator
man-ConvertTo-SecureString = turns a text into a SecureString. From plain text it needs both -AsPlainText and -Force (the password then sits in the history: Read-Host -AsSecureString is better).
    .synopsis = ConvertTo-SecureString [-String] <String> [-AsPlainText] [-Force]
    .option-String = the text.
    .option-AsPlainText = the text is plain.
    .option-Force = yes, plain text.
    .option-Key = a key (not simulated).
    .example-1 = a SecureString from a text
man-Start-Service = starts services (their dependencies first). A disabled service, or one whose logon account can't log on (its password changed since the service was configured, a disabled account), doesn't start: "Service 'X (x)' cannot be started due to the following error: Cannot start service x on computer '.'." and the System log says why (events 7038 and 7000).
    .synopsis = Start-Service [-Name] <String[]> [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the services' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = their display names.
    .option-InputObject = service objects (Get-Service).
    .option-PassThru = output the services.
    .example-1 = start a service
    .example-2 = why it didn't start
man-Stop-Service = stops services. A service others depend on stops only with -Force (they stop too).
    .synopsis = Stop-Service [-Name] <String[]> [-Force] [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the services' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = their display names.
    .option-InputObject = service objects (Get-Service).
    .option-Force = stop the services that depend on it too.
    .option-PassThru = output the services.
    .option-NoWait = don't wait.
    .example-1 = stop the Print Spooler
man-Restart-Service = stops then starts services (-Force for one others depend on).
    .synopsis = Restart-Service [-Name] <String[]> [-Force] [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the services' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = their display names.
    .option-InputObject = service objects (Get-Service).
    .option-Force = also when others depend on it.
    .option-PassThru = output the services.
    .example-1 = restart Windows Time
    .example-2 = through the pipe
man-Set-Service = changes a service: its start type (Automatic, Manual, Disabled), status, display name, description. Windows PowerShell 5.1's Set-Service has no -Credential: a service's logon account and password are changed with sc.exe config NAME obj= ACCOUNT password= PASSWORD.
    .synopsis = Set-Service [-Name] <String> [-StartupType <ServiceStartMode>] [-Status <String>] [-DisplayName <String>] [-Description <String>]
    .option-Name = the service.
    .option-InputObject = service objects (Get-Service).
    .option-DisplayName = its display name.
    .option-Description = its description.
    .option-StartupType = Automatic, Manual or Disabled.
    .option-Status = Running or Stopped.
    .option-PassThru = output the service.
    .example-1 = the Print Spooler never starts
    .example-2 = the logon account's password
man-Get-CimInstance = gets management objects (CIM/WMI): Win32_Service shows what Get-Service doesn't, a service's logon account (StartName), its program (PathName), its process; Win32_LogicalDisk the disks' free space; Win32_OperatingSystem the system and its last boot.
    .synopsis = Get-CimInstance [-ClassName] <String> [-Filter <String>] [-Property <String[]>]
    .option-ClassName = the class (Win32_Service, Win32_LogicalDisk, Win32_OperatingSystem, Win32_ComputerSystem).
    .option-Filter = a WQL condition: "Name='sshd'", "State='Stopped' AND StartMode='Auto'".
    .option-Query = a whole WQL query (SELECT * FROM Win32_Service WHERE …).
    .option-Property = only these properties.
    .option-Namespace = the namespace (root/cimv2).
    .example-1 = which account runs each service
    .example-2 = automatic services that don't run
man-Get-WmiObject = the older form of Get-CimInstance (WMI), still in Windows PowerShell 5.1 (alias gwmi): the same classes and filters.
    .synopsis = Get-WmiObject [-Class] <String> [-Filter <String>] [-Property <String[]>]
    .option-ClassName = the class (Win32_Service, Win32_LogicalDisk, Win32_OperatingSystem, Win32_ComputerSystem).
    .option-Filter = a WQL condition: "Name='sshd'", "State='Stopped' AND StartMode='Auto'".
    .option-Query = a whole WQL query (SELECT * FROM Win32_Service WHERE …).
    .option-Property = only these properties.
    .option-Namespace = the namespace (root/cimv2).
    .example-1 = the disks
man-Get-Process = gets the running processes: Handles, memory (NPM, PM, WS in KB), CPU seconds, Id, ProcessName. The Windows side of ps.
    .synopsis = Get-Process [[-Name] <String[]>] [-Id <Int32[]>]
    .option-Name = the processes' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-Id = their ids.
    .option-IncludeUserName = their account too.
    .example-1 = the busiest five
    .example-2 = OpenSSH's server
man-Stop-Process = stops processes (alias kill). A service's process stops its service.
    .synopsis = Stop-Process [-Id] <Int32[]> [-Name <String[]>] [-Force]
    .option-Id = the ids.
    .option-Name = the names.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .option-PassThru = output the processes.
    .example-1 = what would stop
man-Get-ScheduledTask = gets the scheduled tasks (the Task Scheduler, Windows' cron): TaskPath, TaskName, State (Ready, Disabled, Running). Format-List shows the actions (the program run), the triggers (when) and the principal (as which account).
    .synopsis = Get-ScheduledTask [[-TaskName] <String[]>] [[-TaskPath] <String[]>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .example-1 = the tasks of the root folder (the administrators' own)
    .example-2 = what a task runs
man-Get-ScheduledTaskInfo = gets a task's last run (LastRunTime), its result (LastTaskResult, in decimal: 0 success, 267011 = 0x41303 never run, 267009 = 0x41301 running, 2147943726 = 0x8007052E the user name or password is incorrect, 2147942402 = 0x80070002 file not found) and its next run (NextRunTime). A task can "succeed" (0) while its script failed: PowerShell -File returns 0 unless the script throws or ends with exit N.
    .synopsis = Get-ScheduledTaskInfo [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = did last night's backup run?
    .example-2 = the result in hexadecimal, to look it up
man-Start-ScheduledTask = runs a task now, as its account (a disabled task refuses). The result is in Get-ScheduledTaskInfo and, if the history is on, in Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational.
    .synopsis = Start-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = a rehearsal of the night's run
man-Stop-ScheduledTask = stops a running task.
    .synopsis = Stop-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = stop it
man-Enable-ScheduledTask = enables a task (State Ready): its triggers fire again.
    .synopsis = Enable-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = back in service
man-Disable-ScheduledTask = disables a task (State Disabled): it no longer runs.
    .synopsis = Disable-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = pause it
man-Set-ScheduledTask = changes a task: here its account and the password the Task Scheduler keeps for it ("Run whether user is logged on or not"). The password is checked at once: a wrong one gives "The user name or password is incorrect."
    .synopsis = Set-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>] [-User <String>] [-Password <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .option-User = the account.
    .option-Password = its password (plain text, as in the real cmdlet).
    .example-1 = give the task the account's new password
man-Unregister-ScheduledTask = deletes a task (it asks first, unless -Confirm:$false).
    .synopsis = Unregister-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>]
    .option-TaskName = the tasks' names (wildcards allowed).
    .option-TaskPath = their folder (\ by default).
    .option-InputObject = task objects (Get-ScheduledTask).
    .example-1 = without the question
man-New-ScheduledTaskAction = creates what a new task runs: a program and its arguments (for a script, powershell.exe -NoProfile -File C:\Scripts\script.ps1). Nothing is registered yet: give the object to Register-ScheduledTask -Action.
    .synopsis = New-ScheduledTaskAction [-Execute] <String> [[-Argument] <String>] [[-WorkingDirectory] <String>]
    .option-Execute = the program (powershell.exe, C:\Tools\export.exe).
    .option-Argument = its arguments, in one string.
    .option-WorkingDirectory = the folder it starts in.
    .example-1 = a script, run by PowerShell
man-New-ScheduledTaskTrigger = creates when a new task runs: -Daily, -Weekly (-DaysOfWeek), -Once, at a time (-At 3am, -At "4:30 AM"), or -AtStartup, -AtLogOn. Exactly one kind: otherwise "Parameter set cannot be resolved". Give the object to Register-ScheduledTask -Trigger.
    .synopsis = New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At <DateTime> [-DaysInterval <UInt32>] | -Weekly -At <DateTime> -DaysOfWeek <Day[]> | -Once -At <DateTime> | -AtStartup | -AtLogOn
    .option-Daily = every day (or every -DaysInterval days).
    .option-Weekly = on the days of -DaysOfWeek (Monday, Sunday…).
    .option-Once = once, at -At.
    .option-AtStartup = when the computer starts.
    .option-AtLogOn = when a user logs on.
    .option-At = the time (today's date if only a time is given).
    .option-DaysInterval = every how many days.
    .option-DaysOfWeek = the days of the week.
    .example-1 = every night at 3:00
    .example-2 = every Sunday at 4:30
man-New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal = creates the account a new task runs as: SYSTEM (-LogonType ServiceAccount, no password to keep), or a user. -RunLevel Highest gives it an administrator's full token. For a local account with a password, Register-ScheduledTask -User -Password is simpler.
    .synopsis = New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal [-UserId] <String> [-LogonType <LogonTypeEnum>] [-RunLevel <Limited|Highest>]
    .option-UserId = the account (SYSTEM, svc_backup).
    .option-LogonType = ServiceAccount (SYSTEM), Password, InteractiveToken (only while the user is logged on), S4U.
    .option-RunLevel = Limited (by default) or Highest.
    .example-1 = as SYSTEM: every right on the server, to use only when it is needed
man-Register-ScheduledTask = registers a new task: its name, its action (New-ScheduledTaskAction), its triggers (New-ScheduledTaskTrigger) and its account: -User with -Password (stored by the Task Scheduler, "Run whether user is logged on or not"; checked at once), or -Principal. A task of the same name already there: "Cannot create a file when that file already exists.", unless -Force (it replaces it).
    .synopsis = Register-ScheduledTask [-TaskName] <String> [[-TaskPath] <String>] [-Action] <CimInstance[]> [[-Trigger] <CimInstance[]>] [[-User] <String>] [[-Password] <String>] [-Principal <CimInstance>] [-RunLevel <Limited|Highest>] [-Description <String>] [-Force]
    .option-TaskName = the new task's name.
    .option-TaskPath = its folder (\ by default).
    .option-Action = what it runs.
    .option-Trigger = when it runs (none: only on demand).
    .option-User = the account it runs as.
    .option-Password = that account's password (plain text, as in the real cmdlet).
    .option-Principal = the account, as an object (New-ScheduledTaskPrincipal).
    .option-RunLevel = Limited or Highest.
    .option-Description = what it is for.
    .option-Force = replace a task of the same name.
    .example-1 = a nightly task as a dedicated account
    .example-2 = as SYSTEM, with a principal
man-compact = shows or changes the NTFS compression of files: /c compresses, /u uncompresses, with neither it lists. A text file takes several times less room on the volume (its Length doesn't change), a zip or a picture hardly less; a file a program holds open can't be compressed. It gains room, it doesn't stop what fills the disk.
    .synopsis = compact [/c | /u] [/s[:DIR]] [/i] [/q] [FILES]
    .option-c = compress.
    .option-u = uncompress.
    .option-s = the files of this folder (/s:DIR) and of its subfolders.
    .option-i = go on after an error.
    .option-q = report only the totals.
    .example-1 = the compression state of the logs
    .example-2 = compress the archives, despite errors
man-Get-SmbShareAccess = gets a share's permissions (share level: Full, Change, Read, for accounts or groups, Allow or Deny). Through the network, what a user may do is the more restrictive of these and the NTFS permissions of the files.
    .synopsis = Get-SmbShareAccess [-Name] <String[]>
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .example-1 = who may use the share, and how
man-Grant-SmbShareAccess = gives accounts a right on a share (it replaces their Allow entry). It asks for confirmation, unless -Force.
    .synopsis = Grant-SmbShareAccess [-Name] <String[]> -AccountName <String[]> -AccessRight <Full|Change|Read> [-Force]
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .option-AccountName = the accounts or groups.
    .option-AccessRight = Full, Change or Read.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .example-1 = the accounting team may change files through the share
man-Revoke-SmbShareAccess = removes the Allow entries of accounts on a share (asks unless -Force).
    .synopsis = Revoke-SmbShareAccess [-Name] <String[]> -AccountName <String[]> [-Force]
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .option-AccountName = the accounts or groups.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .example-1 = no more access for everyone
man-Block-SmbShareAccess = denies accounts any access to a share (a Deny entry, which wins).
    .synopsis = Block-SmbShareAccess [-Name] <String[]> -AccountName <String[]> [-Force]
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .option-AccountName = the accounts or groups.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .example-1 = bob refused
man-Unblock-SmbShareAccess = removes the Deny entries of accounts on a share.
    .synopsis = Unblock-SmbShareAccess [-Name] <String[]> -AccountName <String[]> [-Force]
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .option-AccountName = the accounts or groups.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .example-1 = bob no longer refused
man-New-SmbShare = shares a folder on the network. Without an access parameter, Everyone gets Read.
    .synopsis = New-SmbShare [-Name] <String> [-Path] <String> [-Description <String>] [-FullAccess <String[]>] [-ChangeAccess <String[]>] [-ReadAccess <String[]>] [-NoAccess <String[]>]
    .option-Name = the share's name.
    .option-Path = the folder.
    .option-Description = a description.
    .option-FullAccess = accounts with Full.
    .option-ChangeAccess = accounts with Change.
    .option-ReadAccess = accounts with Read.
    .option-NoAccess = accounts refused.
    .example-1 = a share for a team
man-Remove-SmbShare = stops sharing a folder (the folder stays; it asks unless -Force).
    .synopsis = Remove-SmbShare [-Name] <String[]> [-Force]
    .option-Name = the shares.
    .option-Force = without asking.
    .example-1 = no more share
man-Get-ComputerInfo = gets the system's information: product name (Windows Server 2022 Standard), version, build, computer name, domain, last boot.
    .synopsis = Get-ComputerInfo [[-Property] <String[]>]
    .option-Property = only these properties (wildcards allowed).
    .example-1 = the operating system
man-Get-NetIPAddress = gets the machine's IP addresses, with their interface and prefix length. ipconfig shows them the old way.
    .synopsis = Get-NetIPAddress [-AddressFamily <IPv4|IPv6>] [-InterfaceAlias <String[]>]
    .option-AddressFamily = IPv4 or IPv6.
    .option-InterfaceAlias = the interface (Ethernet).
    .example-1 = the IPv4 addresses
man-Test-NetConnection = tests the network to a machine: ping (PingSucceeded) or a TCP port (TcpTestSucceeded).
    .synopsis = Test-NetConnection [[-ComputerName] <String>] [-Port <Int32>]
    .option-ComputerName = the machine.
    .option-Port = a TCP port.
    .option-InformationLevel = Quiet: only True or False.
    .example-1 = the gateway
    .example-2 = SMB
man-icacls = shows and changes the NTFS permissions of files and folders. Each line: ACCOUNT:(flags)(rights). Rights: F full control, M modify, RX read and execute, R read, W write, D delete, or specific ones (RD, WD, AD…). Flags: (I) inherited from above, (OI) inherited by files, (CI) by folders, (IO) inherit only (not for this folder), (NP) no further. In PowerShell, quote ACCOUNT:(OI)(CI)(M): unquoted, the parentheses are commands. Ends with "Successfully processed N files; Failed processing N files".
    .synopsis = icacls NAME [/grant[:r] ACCOUNT:PERM] [/deny ACCOUNT:PERM] [/remove[:g|:d] ACCOUNT] [/inheritance:e|d|r] [/setowner ACCOUNT] [/reset] [/T] [/C] [/Q]
    .option-grant = add rights (ACCOUNT:PERM); /grant:r replaces the account's rights.
    .option-deny = a Deny entry, which wins over an Allow at the same level.
    .option-remove = remove an account's entries (:g the Allow, :d the Deny).
    .option-inheritance-e-d-r = enable inheritance, disable it copying the inherited entries, or disable it removing them.
    .option-setowner = change the owner.
    .option-reset = back to the inherited permissions only.
    .option-T = the folders and files below too.
    .option-C = go on after an error.
    .option-Q = no "processed file" lines.
    .example-1 = who may do what
    .example-2 = the team may modify, files and folders below included
    .example-3 = stop inheriting, keeping a copy
man-takeown = takes ownership of a file or folder: you become its owner (with /A, the Administrators group), which allows changing its permissions afterwards (icacls).
    .synopsis = takeown /F FILE [/A] [/R] [/D Y|N]
    .option-F = the file or folder.
    .option-A = the Administrators group becomes the owner.
    .option-R = everything below too.
    .option-D = the answer for folders you can't list.
    .example-1 = the administrators own the old folder
man-sc_exe = the Service Control Manager's tool: query (the state), qc (the configuration: start type, program, SERVICE_START_NAME, the logon account), start, stop, config. Each option of config is written "name=" followed by a space and its value. In PowerShell, type sc.exe: sc alone is Set-Content.
    .synopsis = sc.exe query|qc|start|stop|config NAME [start= auto|demand|disabled] [obj= ACCOUNT] [password= PASSWORD]
    .option-query = a service's state (STATE, WIN32_EXIT_CODE).
    .option-qc = its configuration.
    .option-start = start it.
    .option-stop = stop it.
    .option-config = change start=, obj= (the account), password=, DisplayName=, binPath=.
    .example-1 = its account and program
    .example-2 = the account's new password
    .example-3 = never started
man-net = the old network and accounts command, still everywhere: net user NAME shows an account (active, password last set and expiry, last logon, groups), net user NAME PASSWORD sets a password, net localgroup a group's members, net accounts the password policy, net start and net stop the services (by name or display name), net helpmsg the text of an error number.
    .synopsis = net user [NAME [PASSWORD|*] [/add] [/active:yes|no] [/expires:never|DATE]] [/domain] | net localgroup [GROUP [NAME /add|/delete]] | net accounts | net use | net start [SERVICE] | net stop SERVICE | net share | net helpmsg N
    .option-user = the accounts (/expires: when the account itself expires, not its password; /domain: the domain's accounts, asked of the domain controller, with their global groups).
    .option-localgroup = the local groups.
    .option-accounts = the password policy.
    .option-use = the network drives mapped for you (by Group Policy).
    .option-start = start a service (or list those running).
    .option-stop = stop a service.
    .option-share = the shares.
    .option-helpmsg = an error number's text (1069…).
    .example-1 = the backup account
    .example-2 = who administers
    .example-3 = The service did not start due to a logon failure.
man-schtasks = the scheduled tasks from cmd: /query lists them (Next Run Time, Status; /v adds Last Run Time, Last Result, Run As User, Task To Run), /run runs one now, /change gives a task a new account or password (checked at once), /delete removes it.
    .synopsis = schtasks /query [/tn NAME] [/fo LIST] [/v] | /run /tn NAME | /end /tn NAME | /change /tn NAME [/ru ACCOUNT] [/rp PASSWORD] [/enable|/disable] | /delete /tn NAME
    .option-query = list the tasks.
    .option-run = run a task now.
    .option-end = stop a running task.
    .option-change = change a task (/ru, /rp, /enable, /disable).
    .option-delete = delete a task.
    .option-tn = the task's name.
    .option-fo = the format: TABLE, LIST.
    .option-v = every field.
    .example-1 = everything about a task
    .example-2 = its account's new password
man-reg = reads and changes the registry from cmd: keys written HKLM\SOFTWARE\… (or HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\…), values with their type (REG_SZ, REG_EXPAND_SZ, REG_DWORD in hexadecimal, REG_MULTI_SZ). In PowerShell, the registry is also a drive: HKLM:\.
    .synopsis = reg query KEY [/v NAME] [/s] | reg add KEY [/v NAME] [/t TYPE] [/d DATA] [/f] | reg delete KEY [/v NAME] [/f]
    .option-query = show a key's values and subkeys.
    .option-add = add a key or a value.
    .option-delete = delete a key or a value.
    .option-v = the value's name.
    .option-t = its type (REG_SZ, REG_DWORD…).
    .option-d = its data.
    .option-s = every subkey.
    .option-f = without asking.
    .example-1 = OpenSSH's default shell
    .example-2 = set it
man-wevtutil = the event logs' tool: el lists the logs, gl shows a log's settings (enabled or not), sl changes them. The Task Scheduler's history (Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational) is disabled on Windows Server until wevtutil sl … /e:true.
    .synopsis = wevtutil el | gl LOG | sl LOG /e:true|false
    .option-el = list the logs.
    .option-gl = a log's settings.
    .option-sl = change them: /e:true enables the log.
    .example-1 = is the tasks' history kept?
    .example-2 = keep it from now on
man-ipconfig = shows the network configuration: each adapter's IPv4 address, mask, default gateway (/all: host name, physical address, DNS servers).
    .synopsis = ipconfig [/all]
    .option-all = everything.
    .example-1 = the address and the gateway
    .example-2 = the address only, in PowerShell
man-cmd = the Windows command interpreter (cmd.exe), where an OpenSSH session lands when no DefaultShell is set (prompt user@HOST C:\Users\user>). Its own commands: dir, cd, type, echo, set, ver, mkdir, cls, exit; the programs (ipconfig, net, sc, icacls, schtasks, reg…) work in both shells. powershell starts PowerShell in it; exit comes back.
    .synopsis = cmd [/c COMMAND]
    .option-c = run one command, then come back.
    .example-1 = Windows' version
    .example-2 = from cmd, into PowerShell
man-powershell = Windows PowerShell 5.1 (powershell.exe): alone, it starts an interactive session (exit comes back); -Command runs one line; -File runs a script, whose exit code is that of its exit N (0 when it ends normally, even after errors that didn't stop it, 1 after a throw or an error that stops it). An error that doesn't stop the script (a non-terminating one: a file Copy-Item can't read) only becomes a stopping one with -ErrorAction Stop on the command or $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop' at the top; try {"{"} … {"}"} catch {"{"} … {"}"} then takes it ($_ is the error) and can exit 1. It is how a scheduled task runs a .ps1.
    .synopsis = powershell [-NoProfile] [-Command TEXT] [-File SCRIPT.ps1]
    .option--Command = run a line.
    .option--File = run a script.
    .option--NoProfile = without the profile.
    .example-1 = what a task runs
    .example-2 = one line from cmd
    .example-3 = in a script: an error that stops it, and an exit code that says so

### Active Directory, Group Policy, Windows Defender Firewall, modules (the Windows engine, step 2).
man-Import-Module = imports a module into the session: a module of the machine (ActiveDirectory, GroupPolicy… where they are installed; Windows PowerShell 5.1 also loads them on first use) or a script module (.psm1) or script (.ps1) whose functions join the session.
    .synopsis = Import-Module [-Name] <String[]> [-Force] [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the module, or the path of a .psm1 or .ps1 file.
    .option-Force = import again.
    .option-PassThru = return the module.
    .example-1 = the Active Directory cmdlets (on a domain controller)
    .example-2 = the functions of a script module
man-Get-Module = lists the machine's modules (their type, version, name and a few of their commands).
    .synopsis = Get-Module [[-Name] <String[]>] [-ListAvailable]
    .option-Name = the modules (wildcards allowed).
    .option-ListAvailable = every module that can be imported.
    .example-1 = what the machine can do
man-Get-NetFirewallRule = gets the rules of Windows Defender Firewall, one list per rule (Name, DisplayName, Enabled, Profile, Direction, Action, PolicyStoreSource…). Their ports and addresses are in their filters: pipe a rule to Get-NetFirewallPortFilter or Get-NetFirewallAddressFilter. A connection is allowed if an allow rule matches and no block rule does (a block rule wins); without a match, inbound is blocked.
    .synopsis = Get-NetFirewallRule [[-Name] <String[]>] [-DisplayName <String[]>] [-DisplayGroup <String[]>] [-Enabled <True|False>] [-Direction <Inbound|Outbound>] [-Action <Allow|Block>] [-PolicyStore <String>]
    .option-Name = the rules by name (OpenSSH-Server-In-TCP; wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = the rules by display name ("File and Printer Sharing (SMB-In)").
    .option-DisplayGroup = every rule of a group (Remote Desktop).
    .option-Enabled = only the rules on (True) or off (False).
    .option-Direction = Inbound or Outbound.
    .option-Action = Allow or Block.
    .option-PolicyStore = ActiveStore (all, by default), PersistentStore (the local ones), or a GPO.
    .example-1 = the rule that lets ssh in
    .example-2 = what comes in
    .example-3 = from where SMB is allowed
man-New-NetFirewallRule = creates a firewall rule (inbound and allow by default; its name is a GUID unless -Name). Ports need -Protocol TCP or UDP. -RemoteAddress narrows who may come (an address, a range a-b, a subnet 192.0.2.0/24, LocalSubnet). With -PolicyStore "domain\GPO name", the rule goes into a Group Policy object instead: the machines that apply it get it at their next gpupdate.
    .synopsis = New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName <String> [-Name <String>] [-Direction <Inbound|Outbound>] [-Action <Allow|Block>] [-Protocol <TCP|UDP|ICMPv4|Any>] [-LocalPort <String[]>] [-RemoteAddress <String[]>] [-Profile <Any|Domain|Private|Public>] [-Enabled <True|False>] [-PolicyStore <String>]
    .option-DisplayName = the name shown.
    .option-Name = its identifier.
    .option-Direction = Inbound (default) or Outbound.
    .option-Action = Allow (default) or Block: a block rule wins over allow rules.
    .option-Protocol = TCP, UDP, ICMPv4 or Any.
    .option-LocalPort = the local ports (445, 5000-5100).
    .option-RemotePort = the remote ports.
    .option-RemoteAddress = who may come (an address, a range, a subnet, LocalSubnet).
    .option-Profile = Domain, Private, Public or Any.
    .option-Enabled = True (default) or False.
    .option-PolicyStore = a GPO ("harbour.internal\Server Firewall") instead of the machine.
    .example-1 = one port, from one machine
    .example-2 = answer ping
man-Set-NetFirewallRule = changes firewall rules (named, or from the pipeline): their addresses, ports, action, profiles, state.
    .synopsis = Set-NetFirewallRule [-Name] <String[]> | -DisplayName <String[]> [-NewDisplayName <String>] [-Enabled <True|False>] [-Action <Allow|Block>] [-LocalPort <String[]>] [-RemoteAddress <String[]>] [-Profile <String>] [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the rules by name (OpenSSH-Server-In-TCP; wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = the rules by display name ("File and Printer Sharing (SMB-In)").
    .option-DisplayGroup = every rule of a group (Remote Desktop).
    .option-NewDisplayName = a new display name.
    .option-Enabled = True or False.
    .option-Action = Allow or Block.
    .option-LocalPort = the local ports.
    .option-RemoteAddress = who may come.
    .option-Profile = the profiles.
    .option-PassThru = return the rule.
    .example-1 = SMB only from the local network
man-Enable-NetFirewallRule = turns firewall rules on (named, by group, or from the pipeline).
    .synopsis = Enable-NetFirewallRule [-Name] <String[]> | -DisplayName <String[]> | -DisplayGroup <String[]> [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the rules by name (OpenSSH-Server-In-TCP; wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = the rules by display name ("File and Printer Sharing (SMB-In)").
    .option-DisplayGroup = every rule of a group (Remote Desktop).
    .option-PassThru = return the rules.
    .example-1 = answer ping
    .example-2 = a whole group
man-Disable-NetFirewallRule = turns firewall rules off: they stay, but no longer match.
    .synopsis = Disable-NetFirewallRule [-Name] <String[]> | -DisplayName <String[]> | -DisplayGroup <String[]> [-PassThru]
    .option-Name = the rules by name (OpenSSH-Server-In-TCP; wildcards allowed).
    .option-DisplayName = the rules by display name ("File and Printer Sharing (SMB-In)").
    .option-DisplayGroup = every rule of a group (Remote Desktop).
    .option-PassThru = return the rules.
    .example-1 = no more Remote Desktop
man-Remove-NetFirewallRule = deletes firewall rules (named, or from the pipeline).
    .synopsis = Remove-NetFirewallRule [-Name] <String[]> | -DisplayName <String[]> | -DisplayGroup <String[]>
